window.OB_PAGES={"index":{"title":"Organizational Behavior and Management · MN4063 study guide","description":"Clear explanations, workplace cases and short notes for MN4063. Read a topic, try its questions, then revisit the essentials.","content":"<p class=\"collection-back\"><a href=\"../\">← All study guides</a></p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Start here <span>/</span> MN4063 · Study route</div><h1>Organizational Behavior and Management</h1><p class=\"lead\">Clear explanations, workplace cases and short notes for MN4063. Read a topic, try its questions, then revisit the essentials.</p><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">How people and groups work</a><a href=\"#section-1\">Study route</a><a href=\"#section-2\">How to use the guide</a></nav><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">How people and groups work</h2>\n<p>Organizational behavior examines how people think, feel, and act at work, and how teams and organizations shape those experiences. This guide follows the course topics in prerequisite order and connects concepts to workplace situations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">Study route</h2>\n<div class=\"reading-route\"><h3>Start with the foundations</h3><ul><li><a href=\"lecture-1-organizational-behaviour-foundations.html\">Organizational Behaviour Foundations</a><p>Understand behaviour at three levels, then ask which conditions make an explanation fit.</p></li></ul><h3>Understand individuals at work</h3><ul><li><a href=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction.html\">Work Attitudes and Job Satisfaction</a><p>Distinguish beliefs, feelings and intentions, then use EVLN to classify responses to dissatisfaction.</p></li><li><a href=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work.html\">Personality at Work</a><p>Compare personality frameworks and see why workplace rules and cues change what traits predict.</p></li><li><a href=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit.html\">Values, Culture, and Person-Environment Fit</a><p>Separate values, job fit and organizational fit without turning national culture into an individual label.</p></li><li><a href=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods.html\">Emotions and Moods</a><p>Distinguish emotions from moods, then connect emotional labour and daily events to work outcomes.</p></li><li><a href=\"lecture-5-managing-emotions-at-work.html\">Managing Emotions at Work</a><p>Recognize emotions, ask what support is wanted, and choose a response that fits the person and the task.</p></li><li><a href=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making.html\">Perception and Individual Decision Making</a><p>See how attention, attribution and decision shortcuts shape the conclusions people reach.</p></li><li><a href=\"individual-revision.html\">Short note: individuals at work</a><p>Review the essentials and check your recall.</p></li></ul><h3>Understand groups and teams</h3><ul><li><a href=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour.html\">Group Structure and Behaviour</a><p>Connect roles, norms, status and cohesion to effort and performance.</p></li><li><a href=\"lecture-7-group-decision-making.html\">Group Decision-Making</a><p>Compare groupthink, groupshift and three ways to organize a group decision.</p></li><li><a href=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness.html\">Work Teams and Effectiveness</a><p>Decide when a team helps, then examine its context, composition and process.</p></li><li><a href=\"groups-revision.html\">Short note: groups and teams</a><p>Review the essentials and check your recall.</p></li></ul></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">How to use the guide</h2>\n<p>Use the topic contents to jump to a concept. Compare the examples, try the scenario activities, and explain your answer before opening the practice solution. Finish each course section with its short note.</p>\n<p>The guide works offline. Tables and diagrams can be explored within their labeled regions on narrow screens.</p></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-1-organizational-behaviour-foundations.html\"><small>Next</small>Organizational Behaviour Foundations <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-1-organizational-behaviour-foundations":{"title":"Organizational Behaviour Foundations · OB and Management","description":"Understand behaviour at three levels, then ask which conditions make an explanation fit.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Foundations <span>/</span> Lecture 1</div><h1>Organizational Behaviour Foundations</h1><p class=\"lead\">Understand behaviour at three levels, then ask which conditions make an explanation fit.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 11 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">People, groups and the workplace</a><a href=\"#section-1\">What work means to people</a><a href=\"#section-2\">What organizational behaviour studies</a><a href=\"#section-3\">Why OB draws on several disciplines</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Hawthorne: classroom accounts and historical qualification</a><a href=\"#section-5\">OB uses contingent explanations</a><a href=\"#section-6\">The organizational iceberg</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Management as integration</a><a href=\"#section-8\">Implications for managers</a><a href=\"#section-9\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-11\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">People, groups and the workplace</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Work can provide income, identity, connection and a sense of competence.</li><li>Investigate the person, the group and the organizational system together.</li><li>A useful OB claim specifies its conditions. The same policy can work differently in different settings.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">What work means to people</h2>\n<p>Work can be understood as an activity that produces something of value for other people. This definition includes paid jobs, unpaid care, volunteering, and other productive activity; wages are one important function of work, not the whole meaning.</p>\n<p>Work may provide an economic function, social connection, community status, identity, self-esteem, and for some people self-actualisation. These functions differ across people and situations. A job can provide income while offering little social contact, or support identity and mastery without high community status.</p>\n<p>Two routes connect work to self-esteem. First, a person can demonstrate competence or mastery over themselves and their environment: they discover that they can do something. Second, they can see that their activity creates value for other people and that they have something significant to offer.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a valued contribution in a routine job</summary><p>A hospital supply worker checks that essential items reach the right ward. The work provides income, but its value is not limited to pay: reliable checks help clinicians care for patients. Mastering the ordering system can demonstrate competence, and seeing that the work helps others can support self-esteem. The worker may also gain social connection with colleagues.</p><p>Which functions matter most depends on the worker and the job conditions.</p></details>\n<h3>Alienation</h3>\n<p>When work fails to provide functions that matter to a person, they may experience <strong>alienation</strong>, described in the lecture through feelings of powerlessness, meaninglessness, and normlessness. These are distinct clues: little control points toward powerlessness; no felt purpose points toward meaninglessness; unclear or disrupted expectations can contribute to normlessness.</p><p>Alienation is not a synonym for any unpleasant task, and the same job conditions need not affect every person in the same way.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: identify what is missing before proposing a fix</summary><p>A skilled warehouse employee repeats a check but cannot see how it protects customers, has no say when the process fails, and receives contradictory instructions about what counts as a defect. The weak connection to a useful outcome may feel meaningless; lack of influence may feel powerless; conflicting expectations may create normlessness.</p><p>A manager should ask the employee what they experience and examine the work process before choosing a response. A pay increase alone would not necessarily address all three problems.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">What organizational behaviour studies</h2>\n<p>Organizational behaviour studies how individuals, groups and organizational structure affect behaviour at work. It applies that knowledge to organizational effectiveness.</p><p>OB aims to understand and predict behaviour. Prediction is difficult because both people and situations vary.</p>\n<p>The three determinants are connected levels of analysis. Individual factors include a person's attributes and responses; group factors include relationships, roles, and collective processes; structural factors include formal arrangements and systems. A workplace outcome can involve more than one level at once.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"OB levels of analysis and diagnostic questions\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level of analysis</th>\n<th>Questions an OB analysis might ask</th>\n<th>Example factors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual</td>\n<td>What does a person bring to or experience in this situation?</td>\n<td>Skills, perception, motivation, attitudes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group</td>\n<td>How do interaction and shared expectations shape the behaviour?</td>\n<td>Roles, communication, group processes, influence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organization / structure</td>\n<td>How do formal arrangements and systems shape the work?</td>\n<td>Work design, rules, technology, structure, change</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: diagnose a missed handoff at three levels</summary><p>A customer order is delayed after a shift change. At the individual level, check whether the employee had the needed information and understood the task. At the group level, check whether the incoming and outgoing teams share a reliable handoff practice. At the organizational level, check whether the schedule, record system, or work design leaves a gap.</p><p>These are lines of inquiry, not competing explanations: evidence may show that several levels interact.</p></details>\n<p>OB's core topics include motivation; leader behaviour and power; interpersonal communication; group structure and processes; attitude development and perception; change processes; conflict and negotiation; and work design. These topics connect: for example, work design can affect communication and motivation, while leadership and power can influence how a group handles change or conflict.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Why OB draws on several disciplines</h2>\n<p>OB draws on several behavioural sciences. The table maps their contributions to levels of analysis in the lecture. Use the links to identify a useful lens, while remembering that disciplinary contributions overlap.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Disciplinary contributions and connected analysis levels\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Discipline</th>\n<th>Contribution areas named in the lecture</th>\n<th>Connected unit(s) in the lecture map</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychology</td>\n<td>Learning, motivation, personality, emotions, perception, training, leadership effectiveness, job satisfaction, individual decision making, performance appraisal, attitude measurement, employee selection, work design, work stress</td>\n<td>Individual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social psychology</td>\n<td>Behavioural and attitude change, communication, group processes, group decision making</td>\n<td>Group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sociology</td>\n<td>Communication, power, conflict, intergroup behaviour; formal organization theory, organizational technology, change, and culture</td>\n<td>Group and organizational system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anthropology</td>\n<td>Comparative values and attitudes, cross-cultural analysis; organizational culture, environment, and power</td>\n<td>Group and organizational system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Political science</td>\n<td>Power, conflict, ethics, communication, intergroup and intragroup behaviour</td>\n<td>Group and organizational system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neuroscience</td>\n<td>Perception, attribution, learning, motivation</td>\n<td>Individual</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Psychology studies behaviour. Social psychology examines social influence. Sociology looks at relationships and social systems; anthropology contributes cultural analysis. Political science adds power and conflict. Use the contribution column below to connect a workplace question with a discipline.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: use more than one discipline to understand a new reporting rule</summary><p>Employees resist a new reporting procedure. Psychology may help examine individual attitudes, learning, or perceived effort. Social psychology may help explain how team discussion changes acceptance. Sociology can direct attention to the formal structure and communication channels; political science can help examine whose influence shaped the rule. These views can overlap.</p><p>A manager should use evidence from the relevant levels instead of assigning the problem to one discipline by label.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Hawthorne: classroom accounts and historical qualification</h2>\n<p>The lecture presents the Hawthorne studies as an important milestone in the development of management sciences and dates the series to 1924–1932 at Western Electric's Hawthorne plant in Illinois. Its relay-room illustration describes six women in a relay assembly test room in 1927.</p><p>Researchers considered rest breaks, workday length, physical health, sleep, and diet; changes were discussed with the women and could be abandoned if they objected. The slide account says researchers later attributed increased output in part to changed supervision and human relations.</p>\n<p>The company was <strong>Western Electric</strong>; <strong>Hawthorne Works</strong> was its plant. The label “Hawthorne Electrical Company” mixes the company and site names. <a href=\"https://www.library.hbs.edu/hc/hawthorne/01.html\">Baker Library’s archival exhibit</a> identifies the site as Western Electric’s Hawthorne Works.</p>\n<p>The deck distinguishes an <strong>original classroom interpretation</strong> from a <strong>later interpretation</strong>. It first says money did not cause the increased output and that employees performed better when managers treated them positively. It then says money mattered and that feeling important and group pride from being selected for the project also contributed.</p><p>Keep these as different accounts of the study rather than merging them into one proven cause.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Hawthorne interpretations and historical qualification\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Account</th>\n<th>What it emphasizes</th>\n<th>How to read it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Original interpretation presented in the lecture</td>\n<td>Positive managerial treatment and human relations; money was not the cause</td>\n<td>A classroom account of the researchers' interpretation, not a controlled estimate isolating one cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Later interpretation presented in the lecture</td>\n<td>Money mattered, alongside project importance and group pride</td>\n<td>An expanded interpretation; the factors are not shown as experimentally separated in the slide account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Later historical analysis of the original illumination records</td>\n<td>No systematic productivity rise every time lighting changed; some evidence for more subtle responses to experimental manipulation is weak and context-dependent</td>\n<td>Qualifies the popular “attention alone always raises output” story; it does not prove that observation can never affect behaviour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture uses <strong>Hawthorne effect</strong> for behaviour changing with researchers’ participation. Treat it as a possible study influence. The relay-room account and illumination experiments are different parts of the Hawthorne research.</p><details><summary>Historical qualification: illumination and relay-room studies</summary><p>The term <strong>Hawthorne effect</strong> is used in the lecture for subjects behaving differently because researchers actively participate in a study. Treat that as a possible influence to consider, not a universal law or a settled explanation of every productivity change.</p><p>The historical illumination studies and the relay-room account are distinct parts of the broader Hawthorne story; evidence about one should not be silently transferred to the other. Levitt and List's analysis of original illumination data found little evidence for the popular claim that each lighting change increased productivity, while discussing weaker patterns compatible with subtler effects.</p><p>Izawa, French, and Hedge's archival analysis also examines the illumination experiments. <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/papers/w15016\">Levitt &amp; List, NBER Working Paper 15016</a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1177/0018720811417968\">Izawa, French &amp; Hedge, <em>Human Factors</em></a>.</p></details>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a productivity rise with several changes at once</summary><p>A team produces more units after a new break schedule, extra supervisor contact, and a team bonus begin in the same week. The rise alone cannot show which change mattered: pay, rest, supervision, team pride, or another condition could be involved.</p><p>A careful manager records the timing and output, checks whether product quality and workload also changed, and compares suitable periods or teams while noting other differences. This is a reasoning example, not a recreation of the historical study.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">OB uses contingent explanations</h2>\n<p>Physical-science laws may apply consistently across many situations. OB has more limits on broad generalizations because people are complex and not all alike. This does not make OB arbitrary. It means that an explanation should specify the conditions under which a relationship is expected.</p>\n<p>The lecture expresses a contingent claim as: “X leads to Y, but only under conditions specified in Z.” Here, Z represents contingency variables. OB develops general concepts and applies them to a particular situation, person, or group. Context can include the task, group, organizational arrangements, and broader environment.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: turn “supervision always improves output” into a testable claim</summary><p><strong>Unqualified claim:</strong> More supervisor contact always improves output. <strong>Contingent version:</strong> More supervisor contact may improve output when employees need timely guidance and experience the contact as useful, but its effect may differ when work is already well understood or the contact is experienced as intrusive.</p><p>To assess the claim, define output and the conditions, compare like situations where possible, and consider other changes that happened at the same time.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-6\">The organizational iceberg</h2>\n<p>The iceberg image separates formal, overt aspects above the water from behavioural, covert aspects below it. The visible features are only part of organizational life. Less visible behaviour and relationships can affect productivity and the quality of working life; they are not automatically negative or hidden problems.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Visible and less visible organizational iceberg factors\">\n<caption>Organizational iceberg: formal aspects above water and behavioural aspects below</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Formal aspects (overt, above water)</th><th scope=\"col\">Behavioural aspects (covert, below water)</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Customers</td><td>Attitudes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Technology</td><td>Communication patterns</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Organization design</td><td>Informal team processes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Formal goals</td><td>Personality</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Financial resources</td><td>Conflict</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Physical facilities</td><td>Political behaviour</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rules and regulations</td><td>Underlying competencies and skills</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface competencies and skills</td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a policy exists, but the informal process differs</summary><p>A company posts a formal rule that defects should be reported immediately. On one team, members quietly discourage reporting because they fear being blamed. The rule, reporting system, and formal goal are visible; attitudes, informal team pressure, and communication patterns are less visible. A manager should examine how both sides operate.</p><p>The iceberg is a prompt to investigate, not a claim that covert factors are always harmful.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-7\">Management as integration</h2>\n<p>An organization includes people, relationships, goals, systems and work. Members experience it through different roles and relationships, so it can mean different things to different people.</p>\n<p>Management coordinates people’s efforts toward organizational goals. People bring their own needs, perceptions, feelings and attitudes. Managers must understand those responses and connect individual needs with organizational requirements.</p>\n<p>The lecture's integration model groups influences into four related contexts:</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Individual, group, organization, and environment factors\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Context</th>\n<th>Examples in the lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual</td>\n<td>Personality; skills and attributes; values and attitudes; needs and expectations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group</td>\n<td>Structure and functions; informal organization; role relationships; group influence and pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organization</td>\n<td>Objectives and policy; technology and work methods; formal structure; leadership styles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Environment</td>\n<td>Technical and scientific; economic; social and cultural; government</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These contexts influence behaviour in organizations. The model then links four work elements through cross-links and a central effort to improve the people–organization relationship. The relationships below are reciprocal or interconnected in the lecture's figure, not a one-way chain:</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Reciprocal relationships in the management integration model\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Linked work elements</th>\n<th>Relationship shown in the model</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Organizational processes and execution of work ↔ coordinating members' efforts</td>\n<td>Two sides of organizing work are linked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coherent pattern of activities in the total work ↔ systems of motivation, job satisfaction, and rewards</td>\n<td>Work design and people systems are connected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Each of those four elements ↔ improving the people–organization relationship</td>\n<td>The central integration task links across the work elements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Satisfying people's needs at work ↔ creating a climate where people work willingly and effectively ↔ achieving organizational goals</td>\n<td>The outcomes are related in both directions; together they support improved organizational performance and effectiveness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: integrate employee needs with a service goal</summary><p>A service center wants shorter customer wait times, while staff report that shifts change unpredictably and difficult cases are assigned without support. Management can examine the scheduling system, team handoffs, employee skills and expectations, and customer demand. A workable approach coordinates member effort, creates a coherent way to handle difficult cases, and reviews whether recognition and support fit the work.</p><p>It should then check both employee experience and service results. Meeting only the speed target may harm people; meeting only a preference without coordinating the work may miss the organizational goal.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-8\">Implications for managers</h2>\n<p>The lecture recommends using scientifically validated information about human behaviour to strengthen workplace interpersonal and leadership skills. Managers should use metrics and relevant situational variables rather than relying on hunches when explaining cause and effect. Technical and conceptual skills also need development through training and by staying current with OB trends such as big data.</p>\n<p>OB can help managers consider employee empowerment, change programs, customer service, and work–life conflicts. These applications require attention to context. Research findings may not transfer unchanged to international settings because cultural norms and expectations can differ. Managers should examine relevant individual, group, and organizational influences instead of focusing on only one level.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-9\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Defining work only as paid employment or treating income as its only function.</li>\n<li>Assuming every dissatisfying job causes alienation or that one missing function affects everyone the same way.</li>\n<li>Defining OB without all three determinants: individuals, groups, and structure.</li>\n<li>Treating disciplines as exclusive categories, such as assuming psychology only concerns isolated people or sociology only concerns organizations.</li>\n<li>Saying the Hawthorne studies proved that attention alone always increases productivity, or blending the lecture's original and later interpretations.</li>\n<li>Confusing the relay-room story with the earlier illumination experiments.</li>\n<li>Making universal OB claims without specifying the contingency conditions.</li>\n<li>Reading the iceberg as “formal is bad, hidden is good,” or assuming covert behaviour is always negative.</li>\n<li>Treating an organization as only its structure chart, and treating people as owned resources.</li>\n<li>Turning the management model into a one-way sequence that removes its reciprocal links and cross-connections.</li>\n<li>Explaining a workplace outcome at just one level when individual, group, and structural conditions may interact.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-10\"></span><h2 id=\"section-11\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">1. Why does the lecture define work as more than paid employment? Name four functions it can serve.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Work is an activity that produces value for other people, so it can include activity beyond a paid job. Four functions are economic support, social connection, community status, identity, self-esteem, or self-actualisation for some people.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">2. How can work support self-esteem, and what three experiences are associated with alienation?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Work can let a person demonstrate competence or mastery and show that they create value for others. Alienation is associated with powerlessness, meaninglessness, and normlessness when work fails to provide functions that matter.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">3. A team misses a handoff because one employee misunderstood the instructions, coworkers use different informal routines, and the system hides the latest status. Identify the levels to investigate.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Investigate the individual (understanding), group (shared routines and communication), and organizational or structural level (the status system and work process). Evidence may show an interaction across levels.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">4. Why is it inaccurate to assign every OB discipline to only one level of analysis?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The lecture map shows overlapping contributions. Sociology connects group and organizational-system analysis. Social psychology is connected to the group unit in this map. These links organize the lecture; they do not prohibit a discipline from studying other levels.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">5. What differs between the original and later Hawthorne interpretations presented in the lecture? What historical caution should accompany them?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The original classroom interpretation emphasizes positive managerial treatment and says money was not the cause. The later interpretation says money mattered and adds project importance and group pride. These are distinct accounts, and later analysis of the illumination data does not support the popular universal claim that every lighting change raised productivity.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">6. Rewrite “more supervision always raises productivity” as a contingent claim.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>One possible rewrite: “More supervision may raise productivity when employees need timely guidance and experience it as useful, under the task and team conditions being studied.” The claim should specify conditions and be checked against other possible changes.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">7. A formal safety rule is visible, but a team informally discourages reporting near misses. Identify one iceberg factor above water and two below.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The formal safety rule is an overt aspect above water. Informal team processes and communication patterns or attitudes are below-water behavioural aspects. The manager should examine how they interact.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">8. In the integration model, why should a manager consider employee needs and organizational goals together?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The model links employee need satisfaction, a climate where people work willingly and effectively, and achieving organizational goals. It also connects organizing work, coordinating effort, coherent activity, and motivation/reward systems through reciprocal relationships. Managing one side alone can undermine the other.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"index.html\"><small>Previous</small>Organizational Behavior and Management <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction.html\"><small>Next</small>Work Attitudes and Job Satisfaction <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction":{"title":"Work Attitudes and Job Satisfaction · OB and Management","description":"Distinguish beliefs, feelings and intentions, then use EVLN to classify responses to dissatisfaction.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Individual behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 2</div><h1>Work Attitudes and Job Satisfaction</h1><p class=\"lead\">Distinguish beliefs, feelings and intentions, then use EVLN to classify responses to dissatisfaction.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 13 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">Separate the evaluation from the response</a><a href=\"#section-1\">What is an attitude?</a><a href=\"#section-2\">How workplace attitudes form</a><a href=\"#section-3\">Attitude components: belief, feeling, intended action</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Visible behaviour and less visible attitudes</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Cognitive consistency and dissonance</a><a href=\"#section-6\">Job satisfaction: an overall evaluation with several facets</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Measuring satisfaction: what a rating scale can and cannot show</a><a href=\"#section-8\">Responses to dissatisfaction: the EVLN framework</a><a href=\"#section-9\">Related job attitudes and behaviour</a><a href=\"#section-10\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-12\">Practice questions</a><a href=\"#section-13\">Further practice</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">Separate the evaluation from the response</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>An attitude evaluates a target. A belief says what is true; a value says what ought to matter.</li><li>Job satisfaction is an evaluation of the job. Involvement, commitment and engagement describe different relationships.</li><li>An intention to act and an observed action are different evidence.</li></ul>\n<p>People differ in background, experience, ability, motivation, personality and perception. Those differences can contribute ideas or create misunderstanding. They do not justify predicting an attitude from one demographic characteristic.</p>\n<p>Managers need to understand their own influence as well as employees’ needs. During imposed change, recognize strain, explain what is changing and support adaptation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">What is an attitude?</h2>\n<p>An <strong>attitude</strong> evaluates a target, such as a job, policy or supervisor. “I like my job” is an attitude. Its beliefs, feelings and response tendencies are related parts of that evaluation.</p>\n<h3>Beliefs and values are related but distinct</h3>\n<p>A <strong>belief</strong> concerns what is true: “The schedule changes every week.” A <strong>value</strong> concerns what is desirable: “Employees should have predictable hours.” An <strong>attitude</strong> evaluates the target: “I resent this scheduling policy.”</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: separate a belief, a value, and an attitude</summary><p>A technician says, “My rota changed three times this month” (<strong>belief</strong> about what happened). She says, “People should have enough notice to plan childcare” (<strong>value</strong> about what ought to happen). She adds, “I feel the rota policy treats staff unfairly” (<strong>attitude</strong> toward the policy).</p><p>The statements concern one situation, but they answer different questions: what is, what should be, and how the person evaluates it.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">How workplace attitudes form</h2>\n<p>The lecture presents three explanations because no single one fully accounts for workplace attitudes.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Main explanation</th>\n<th>What to look for in a case</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dispositional</td>\n<td>Relatively stable predispositions influence how people respond, much like personality traits. The slides cite studies finding little attitude change for some people before and after changing jobs.</td>\n<td>A similar tendency appears across different work settings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Situational</td>\n<td>The uniqueness of a situation shapes attitudes, which may change when work conditions change. Boring or unrewarding work and a poor supervisor are examples.</td>\n<td>A specific feature of the job or its conditions helps explain the evaluation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social-information-processing</td>\n<td>People form attitudes from socially constructed realities as they perceive them; social context shapes how a situation is understood.</td>\n<td>Coworkers’ accounts or group expectations influence how an employee interprets an event.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The conclusion is that both dispositional and social-information-processing explanations have merit, and that predispositions and perceived social context interact. The situational lens remains useful for identifying work conditions that employees evaluate. Treat these as complementary lenses, not as competing labels where one must always win.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:474.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Social context shapes information about an event, which contributes to attitude formation.\" height=\"72pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 474.00 72.00\" width=\"474pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-figure-1-lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-social-information-processing-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 61.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- context -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-figure-1-lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-social-information-processing-node1\">\n<title>context</title>\n<path d=\"M102.6,-43.2C102.6,-43.2 12,-43.2 12,-43.2 6,-43.2 0,-37.2 0,-31.2 0,-31.2 0,-19.2 0,-19.2 0,-13.2 6,-7.2 12,-7.2 12,-7.2 102.6,-7.2 102.6,-7.2 108.6,-7.2 114.6,-13.2 114.6,-19.2 114.6,-19.2 114.6,-31.2 114.6,-31.2 114.6,-37.2 108.6,-43.2 102.6,-43.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"57.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-19.95\">Social context</text>\n</g>\n<!-- information -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-figure-1-lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-social-information-processing-node2\">\n<title>information</title>\n<path d=\"M260.45,-50.4C260.45,-50.4 158.6,-50.4 158.6,-50.4 152.6,-50.4 146.6,-44.4 146.6,-38.4 146.6,-38.4 146.6,-12 146.6,-12 146.6,-6 152.6,0 158.6,0 158.6,0 260.45,0 260.45,0 266.45,0 272.45,-6 272.45,-12 272.45,-12 272.45,-38.4 272.45,-38.4 272.45,-44.4 266.45,-50.4 260.45,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"209.53\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Information</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"209.53\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">about the event</text>\n</g>\n<!-- context&#45;&gt;information -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-figure-1-lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-social-information-processing-edge1\">\n<title>context-&gt;information</title>\n<path d=\"M115.09,-25.2C122.7,-25.2 130.57,-25.2 138.36,-25.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"138.11,-27.48 144.61,-25.2 138.11,-22.93 138.11,-27.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- attitude -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-figure-1-lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-social-information-processing-node3\">\n<title>attitude</title>\n<path d=\"M440.05,-43.2C440.05,-43.2 316.45,-43.2 316.45,-43.2 310.45,-43.2 304.45,-37.2 304.45,-31.2 304.45,-31.2 304.45,-19.2 304.45,-19.2 304.45,-13.2 310.45,-7.2 316.45,-7.2 316.45,-7.2 440.05,-7.2 440.05,-7.2 446.05,-7.2 452.05,-13.2 452.05,-19.2 452.05,-19.2 452.05,-31.2 452.05,-31.2 452.05,-37.2 446.05,-43.2 440.05,-43.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"378.25\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-19.95\">Attitude formation</text>\n</g>\n<!-- information&#45;&gt;attitude -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-figure-1-lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction-social-information-processing-edge2\">\n<title>information-&gt;attitude</title>\n<path d=\"M272.61,-25.2C280.31,-25.2 288.28,-25.2 296.22,-25.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"296.13,-27.48 302.63,-25.2 296.13,-22.93 296.13,-27.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Social context shapes information about an event, which contributes to attitude formation.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>For example, a new employee joins a work group. Coworkers describe the supervisor, pay and benefits, and acceptable output; the newcomer then forms attitudes consistent with that information. This illustrates how social context contributes to attitude formation. It does not mean every newcomer adopts the group’s view or that the information is necessarily accurate.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: explain two employees’ different reactions to the same workload</summary><p>Two analysts receive a demanding assignment. One has usually found challenging tasks energizing, while the other has often experienced them as frustrating (<strong>dispositional lens</strong>). The assignment also comes with unclear priorities and little time (<strong>situational lens</strong>). Coworkers describe the workload as evidence that management does not value the team (<strong>social-information-processing lens</strong>).</p><p>These are possible influences to investigate, not diagnoses: ask what each analyst believes, feels, and has experienced before concluding why their attitudes differ.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Attitude components: belief, feeling, intended action</h2>\n<p>The lecture describes three closely related components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cognition</strong> is the belief, description, or evaluation of how things are: “My pay is low.”</li>\n<li><strong>Affect</strong> is the emotional or feeling part: “I am angry about how little I am paid.”</li>\n<li><strong>Behaviour</strong> in this model means an <strong>intention</strong> to act toward someone or something: “I’m going to look for another job that pays better.” An intention does not guarantee that the action will happen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The salary statements show how components can describe different sides of one attitude. The distinction is useful for analysis, but the components can be difficult to separate; cognition and affect are especially intertwined.</p>\n<h3>The promotion case and the limits of a simple sequence</h3>\n<p>In one case, an employee believes they deserved a promotion that went to a coworker, dislikes the supervisor, and complains or takes action. Cognition, affect, and behaviour are linked with one another and with the overall attitude toward the supervisor.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:869.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Three attitude components reciprocally linked to one another and to an overall negative attitude\" data-figure=\"promotion\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0 0 840 430\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs><marker id=\"promotion-arrow\" markerheight=\"6\" markerwidth=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\" refx=\"9\" refy=\"5\" viewbox=\"0 0 10 10\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10Z\" fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#628a80)\"></path></marker></defs><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#628a80)\" stroke-width=\"2.3\"><path d=\"M250 104V176\" marker-end=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\" marker-start=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M250 254V326\" marker-end=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\" marker-start=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M96 65C12 65 12 365 96 365\" marker-end=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\" marker-start=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M404 65C475 65 480 185 546 198\" marker-end=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\" marker-start=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M404 215H546\" marker-end=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\" marker-start=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\"></path><path d=\"M404 365C475 365 480 245 546 232\" marker-end=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\" marker-start=\"url(#promotion-arrow)\"></path></g><g fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#eef5f2)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#8ca9a1)\"><rect height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" width=\"300\" x=\"100\" y=\"30\"></rect><rect height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" width=\"300\" x=\"100\" y=\"180\"></rect><rect height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" width=\"300\" x=\"100\" y=\"330\"></rect><rect height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" width=\"260\" x=\"550\" y=\"180\"></rect></g><g fill=\"var(--foreground,#25313c)\" font-family=\"Source Sans 3,Segoe UI,sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text font-weight=\"600\" x=\"250\" y=\"57\">Cognition</text><text font-size=\"15\" x=\"250\" y=\"81\">Promotion decision seems unfair</text><text font-weight=\"600\" x=\"250\" y=\"207\">Affect</text><text font-size=\"15\" x=\"250\" y=\"231\">Dislikes supervisor</text><text font-weight=\"600\" x=\"250\" y=\"357\">Behavioural response/action</text><text font-size=\"15\" x=\"250\" y=\"381\">Complains; seeks other work</text><text font-weight=\"600\" x=\"680\" y=\"207\">Negative attitude</text><text font-size=\"15\" x=\"680\" y=\"231\">toward supervisor</text></g></svg>\n</div><figcaption>Lecture 2, slide 27. Reciprocal links connect the three components and the overall attitude. The general definition includes intention; this case describes actions. The links do not make those actions inevitable.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The components are interrelated, especially cognition and affect. The promotion example does not establish a fixed thought → feeling → action sequence. People may respond differently and may explain their own conduct inaccurately afterward.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Source case: identify the components without assuming the next step</summary><p><strong>Situation:</strong> An employee expected a promotion, but it went to a coworker. <strong>Cognition:</strong> the employee thinks they deserved the role and judges the supervisor’s decision as unfair. <strong>Affect:</strong> the employee dislikes the supervisor. <strong>Behavioural response:</strong> the employee complains and looks for other work.</p><p>The components are linked in the example, but neither the belief nor the feeling proves that every employee would take the same action.</p></details>\n<section aria-label=\"Belief, feeling, plan or action?\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"components\"><h3>Belief, feeling, plan or action?</h3><label>Choose a case<select aria-label=\"Choose a case\" data-case=\"\"></select></label><p data-scenario=\"\"></p><div aria-label=\"Choose an answer\" class=\"activity-choices\" data-choices=\"\" role=\"group\"></div><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-feedback=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Teaching cases. Choose an answer, read why it fits, then try another case.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript for this activity. The definitions and worked examples above remain available.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-4\">Visible behaviour and less visible attitudes</h2>\n<p>An iceberg illustration separates observable behaviour above sea level—labelled “known to others”—from underlying factors below the waterline, labelled “unknown to others.” The submerged labels include impact, values, standards, judgments, attitude, motives, ethics, and beliefs. Its upward “impact” arrow links this underlying context with behaviour.</p><p>The image is a reminder that what an observer sees may be only part of a person’s evaluative context; it does not let an observer reliably infer that context from behaviour alone.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part of the illustration</th>\n<th>Labels shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Above sea level; known to others</td>\n<td>Behaviour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Below sea level; unknown to others</td>\n<td>Impact; values, standards, judgments; attitude; motives, ethics, beliefs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: the same visible behaviour can have different context</summary><p>Two employees stay quiet during a meeting. One may be considering a concern but not yet feel ready to raise it; the other may have no concern about the decision. Silence is the visible behaviour. Their beliefs, standards, motives, or feelings are not established by that behaviour alone, so a manager should ask and listen rather than assume they know the reason.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">Cognitive consistency and dissonance</h2>\n<p>People often want their behaviour to be consistent with their attitudes; the lecture calls this <strong>cognitive consistency</strong>. <strong>Cognitive dissonance</strong> is a perceived incompatibility between two or more attitudes, or between an attitude and behaviour. When someone notices such an inconsistency, the resulting tension can motivate an attempt to restore consistency.</p><p>The key condition in the lecture is that the incompatibility is perceived; a mismatch that a person does not notice is not described here as experienced dissonance.</p>\n<p>The slides do not prescribe one universal way of resolving the tension. The following are <strong>supplemental teaching illustrations</strong> of possible responses, not a fixed sequence or guaranteed result.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: an employee’s stated value and weekend work</summary><p>An employee believes staff should be able to disconnect outside scheduled hours, but agrees to work an additional weekend shift. If the employee perceives this as inconsistent, it may create tension.</p><p>They might seek to change future behaviour by declining extra shifts, revise how they evaluate this particular choice (“this was a one-off emergency”), or reconsider the belief’s application in this situation. These are possible explanations and responses; the lecture does not say which one must occur. If the employee sees no inconsistency, the situation alone does not establish dissonance.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-6\">Job satisfaction: an overall evaluation with several facets</h2>\n<p><strong>Job satisfaction</strong> is a positive feeling about a job that results from evaluating its characteristics. A person with high satisfaction holds positive feelings about the job; a person with low satisfaction holds negative feelings. Satisfaction is an emotional response to the job situation and a complex summation: tasks, coworkers, supervisors, organizational rules, performance standards, and working conditions can all contribute.</p>\n<p>The lecture also explains satisfaction through the discrepancy between <strong>job expectations</strong> and <strong>job outcomes</strong>. When outcomes meet or exceed expectations, satisfaction and a wish to remain are more likely. When outcomes fall short, dissatisfaction is a reasonable interpretation, though a single discrepancy does not tell us which job feature matters most to that employee.</p>\n<p>The five dimensions the lecture highlights are:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>What is evaluated</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Work itself</td>\n<td>Whether tasks are interesting and offer learning and responsibility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pay</td>\n<td>Amount received, perceived equity, and method of payment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Promotional opportunities</td>\n<td>Availability of realistic opportunities for advancement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervision</td>\n<td>Supervisors’ technical and managerial abilities, consideration, and interest in employees.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coworkers</td>\n<td>Whether coworkers are friendly, technically competent, and supportive.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A separate illustrative “job satisfaction components” wheel names pay and benefits, career development, respect, engagement, recognition, and job security. This list complements the five defined dimensions; it is not evidence that the wheel is a validated measurement scale.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: one employee, several satisfaction facets</summary><p>Consider an employee who enjoys solving customer problems and has supportive coworkers, but believes their pay is inequitable and sees no realistic route to advancement. The interesting work relates to <strong>work itself</strong>; pay equity relates to <strong>pay</strong>; and the blocked route relates to <strong>promotional opportunities</strong>. Supportive colleagues relate to <strong>coworkers</strong>.</p><p>A single overall satisfaction rating may combine these positive and negative evaluations, so it cannot show which facet needs attention. This is a hypothetical teaching scenario.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-7\">Measuring satisfaction: what a rating scale can and cannot show</h2>\n<p>The lecture’s questionnaire example has 16 statements rated from 1, strongly disagree, to 5, strongly agree. It mixes satisfaction with communication, training, support and team-climate questions. Not every item measures the same facet.</p>\n<p>No instrument provenance or psychometric evidence is provided, so the example should not be treated as a validated standardized scale. Two measurement caveats are explicit:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Like any self-report inventory, a rating scale assumes respondents are both willing and able to describe their feelings accurately.</li>\n<li>It assumes questionnaire items mean the same thing to all respondents, though people may interpret a phrase differently.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The example also shifts between the organization, the job, the team, and particular conditions. An item about team communication is not identical to an overall job-satisfaction item. Ratings are useful indicators to interpret carefully, not a complete explanation of an employee’s experience.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: why one survey item may need a follow-up</summary><p>Suppose an employee selects “2” for agreement with “My manager discusses ways I can improve my work performance.” The score indicates low agreement with that statement, but does not explain why. The employee may understand “discusses” as formal review meetings while a coworker counts informal coaching.</p><p>A follow-up conversation can clarify the meaning, and other items or job facets may point to different issues. One rating alone does not identify a cause or establish the employee’s total job satisfaction.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-8\">Responses to dissatisfaction: the EVLN framework</h2>\n<p>The exit–voice–loyalty–neglect framework classifies responses on two separate axes: <strong>active versus passive</strong> and <strong>constructive versus destructive</strong>. The cell placements below reproduce the slide’s matrix exactly.</p>\n<table><caption>Exit–voice–loyalty–neglect responses by activity and direction</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Response style</th><th scope=\"col\">Constructive</th><th scope=\"col\">Destructive</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Active</th><td><strong>Voice</strong><br/>Raises a concern or proposes improvement. </td><td><strong>Exit</strong><br/>Leaves or prepares to leave. </td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Passive</th><td><strong>Loyalty</strong><br/>Waits supportively for improvement. </td><td><strong>Neglect</strong><br/>Allows conditions or effort to deteriorate. </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The matrix describes a way to sort responses to dissatisfaction, not four personality types. The same person could respond differently as circumstances change. Classify each response on both axes: active/passive first and constructive/destructive second. In particular, do not swap the two active cells or the two passive cells.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: classify four responses to an unfair workload</summary><p><strong>Voice:</strong> an employee raises the unfair distribution with the manager and suggests a rotation—active and constructive. <strong>Exit:</strong> the employee seeks another job and resigns—active and destructive. <strong>Loyalty:</strong> the employee stays, continues supporting colleagues, and waits for a promised review—passive and constructive. <strong>Neglect:</strong> the employee remains but starts missing deadlines and withholding effort—passive and destructive. These are concise explanatory examples for the matrix cells.</p></details>\n<section aria-label=\"Explore the EVLN axes\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"evln\"><h3>Explore the EVLN axes</h3><label>How is the response expressed?<select data-input=\"action\"><option value=\"active\">Active</option><option value=\"passive\">Passive</option></select></label><label>What direction does it take?<select data-input=\"direction\"><option value=\"constructive\">Constructive</option><option value=\"destructive\">Destructive</option></select></label><table class=\"matrix\"><caption>Response × Direction</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Response</th><th scope=\"col\">Constructive</th><th scope=\"col\">Destructive</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Active</th><td data-cell=\"active-constructive\">Voice</td><td data-cell=\"active-destructive\">Exit</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Passive</th><td data-cell=\"passive-constructive\">Loyalty</td><td data-cell=\"passive-destructive\">Neglect</td></tr></tbody></table><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-result=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">The model classifies responses to dissatisfaction. It is not a personality test or moral rating.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to change the inputs. The course model is explained above.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-9\">Related job attitudes and behaviour</h2>\n<p>Job satisfaction is among the most widely studied job attitudes, alongside job involvement and organizational commitment. Perceived organizational support and employee engagement are also important. Organizational citizenship behaviour is a related discretionary behaviour, rather than simply another name for an attitude. These constructs have some distinctiveness but overlap considerably, including because of personality.</p><p>A person may, for example, feel attached to an organization and be dissatisfied with one task; one construct does not determine all the others.</p>\n<table class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Concept</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">What it captures</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Useful distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Job satisfaction</th>\n<td data-label=\"What it captures\">Overall positive or negative feeling from evaluating job characteristics.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction\">A broad evaluation that can summarize several facets.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Job involvement</th>\n<td data-label=\"What it captures\">Interest in and commitment to assigned tasks; strong identification with and care about one’s work.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction\">Focuses on the work or tasks, rather than membership in the organization as a whole.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Psychological empowerment</th>\n<td data-label=\"What it captures\">Beliefs about influence over the work environment, competence, meaningfulness of the job, and perceived autonomy.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction\">Describes perceived agency and meaning in doing the work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Organizational commitment</th>\n<td data-label=\"What it captures\">Strength of identification with and involvement in the organization: accepting its goals and values, willingness to exert effort, and desire to remain a member.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction\">Goes beyond passive loyalty; focuses on the organization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Perceived organizational support (POS)</th>\n<td data-label=\"What it captures\">Belief that the organization values one’s contributions and cares about one’s well-being.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction\">Fair rewards, employee voice, and supportive supervisors are cues the lecture names.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Employee engagement</th>\n<td data-label=\"What it captures\">Involvement with, satisfaction with, and enthusiasm for one’s work; passion and connection versus putting in time without energy or attention.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction\">Emphasizes energy and connection in the work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Organizational citizenship behaviour (OCB)</th>\n<td data-label=\"What it captures\">Discretionary individual actions outside the formal job description.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction\">An extra-role behaviour, not a synonym for satisfaction, commitment, or engagement.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Strong POS has been associated with higher citizenship behaviour, lower tardiness, and better customer service. These are associations, not proof that POS alone caused those outcomes. It also describes potential OCB benefits: less need for supervision, better morale, cost-saving suggestions, and freed managerial time. Managers should be mindful of the risk of burnout when extra-role contributions become excessive.</p><p>OCB refers to discretionary actions, not a feeling.</p>\n<p>A claim that 17–29% of employees are highly engaged appears without a named survey source or date, so it is not used here as a general current estimate.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: distinguish commitment, involvement, empowerment, POS, engagement, and OCB</summary><p>A software analyst cares deeply about the testing tasks (<strong>job involvement</strong>) and believes the work has meaning, that she is capable, and that she can influence how it is done (<strong>psychological empowerment</strong>). She identifies with the company’s goals and wants to remain (<strong>organizational commitment</strong>). She believes her contributions are noticed and her well-being matters to the company (<strong>POS</strong>).</p><p>She brings energy and enthusiasm to the work (<strong>engagement</strong>). On one occasion she voluntarily helps a colleague outside her formal duties (<strong>OCB</strong>). She may show some of these without showing all of them; none is a substitute label for the others or for her overall job satisfaction.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-10\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating <strong>belief</strong>, <strong>value</strong>, and <strong>attitude</strong> as synonyms: ask “what is?”, “what should be?”, and “how is the target evaluated?”</li>\n<li>Assuming one source explains every attitude: check personal predispositions, work conditions, and social information together.</li>\n<li>Forcing cognition → affect → behaviour into a fixed causal chain, or treating an intention as proof that an action occurred.</li>\n<li>Treating cognitive dissonance as any inconsistency whether or not the person perceives it, or claiming that everyone resolves it the same way.</li>\n<li>Reducing job satisfaction to pay, one pleasant event, or one overall survey item; the lecture describes a complex evaluation across facets.</li>\n<li>Calling the questionnaire example a validated scale or assuming all respondents interpret every item identically.</li>\n<li>Confusing EVLN’s cells: voice is active/constructive, exit active/destructive, loyalty passive/constructive, neglect passive/destructive.</li>\n<li>Using job involvement, empowerment, commitment, POS, engagement, and OCB as synonyms. OCB is discretionary behaviour.</li>\n<li>Treating correlations or the unsourced 17–29% engagement statement as proof of causation or a current population estimate.</li>\n<li>Turning demographic or cultural categories into predictions about an individual employee.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-11\"></span><h2 id=\"section-12\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">1. A new employee believes her supervisor ignores good work, values transparent recognition, and feels resentful about the recognition system. Identify belief, value, and attitude.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The belief is that the supervisor ignores good work; the value is that recognition should be transparent; the attitude is resentment toward the recognition system. The first describes what she thinks is true, the second what should be desirable, and the third evaluates a target.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">2. A worker carries a tendency to stay optimistic into a new job, encounters a poorly organized schedule, and hears coworkers describe management as unfair. Which attitude-formation lenses might apply?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>All three may apply: the cross-job tendency suggests a dispositional influence; the disorganized schedule is situational; coworkers’ account is social information processing. The case does not establish which influence matters most.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">3. An employee believes a promotion decision was unfair, feels angry, and plans to complain. Identify each component and name one conclusion you cannot draw.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The belief/evaluation is cognition, anger is affect, and the plan to complain is behavioural intention. You cannot infer that the complaint will occur or that every employee would react this way.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">4. An employee says she supports a policy requiring rest breaks but skips one to finish a task. What would need to be true for this to illustrate cognitive dissonance?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>She would need to perceive an incompatibility between her attitude toward the policy and her behaviour. The lecture links perceived inconsistency to tension and an attempt to restore consistency; it does not prescribe one resolution.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">5. A worker enjoys the tasks and coworkers but feels pay is inequitable and sees no realistic promotion path. Which satisfaction dimensions are involved?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Four dimensions are implicated: work itself and coworkers are positive facets, while pay and promotional opportunities are negative facets. There is no information about supervision.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">6. A team survey asks whether “communication is positive and effective,” but staff disagree about what “effective” means. Which rating-scale caveat is illustrated?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Respondents may not interpret the same questionnaire item in the same way. The answer choices can be identical while the meaning assigned to the statement differs.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">7. Classify each dissatisfaction response: (a) raise a workload concern and propose rotation; (b) resign; (c) stay and wait supportively; (d) stay while withholding effort.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>(a) Voice: active/constructive. (b) Exit: active/destructive. (c) Loyalty: passive/constructive. (d) Neglect: passive/destructive.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">8. An employee cares deeply about assigned work and believes she can influence how it is done, but says little about whether she wants to remain with the organization. Which constructs are supported, and which is not established?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Job involvement and psychological empowerment are supported by caring about the tasks and perceived influence. Organizational commitment is not established because the case gives no evidence about identification with organizational goals, effort for the organization, or desire to remain.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">9. A company gives fair rewards, invites employees’ views, and has supportive supervisors. Which construct do these cues relate to, and what outcome claim should be made carefully?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>They are cues associated with perceived organizational support. Research has linked POS with higher citizenship behaviour, lower tardiness, and better customer service; describe those as associations, not proven effects caused by POS alone.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">10. A highly committed employee voluntarily mentors a colleague outside formal duties but reports low enthusiasm for daily tasks. Which concepts fit, and why is this not contradictory?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Commitment may fit identification with and desire to remain in the organization; mentoring outside formal duties may be OCB. Low enthusiasm suggests lower engagement. These constructs overlap but differ, so one person can show this combination.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">11. Why should the 17–29% engagement figure not be repeated as a current general estimate?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The figure is not accompanied by a named survey source or date. Without that context, the range cannot be checked or generalized as a current estimate.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">12. A manager says that a colleague’s national culture explains why she dislikes a new policy. What is the problem with this conclusion?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>It turns a broad background category into a prediction about one individual. The lecture asks managers to recognize individual differences, but a person’s attitude should be understood from evidence about that person’s beliefs, values, conditions, and social context.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-13\">Further practice</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">13. Give one plausible workplace explanation for two employees evaluating the same supervisor differently without assuming either employee is irrational.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Their dispositions may differ, their experiences with the supervisor may differ, or the social information and group context they encounter may differ. Several influences can interact; the case should be investigated rather than reduced to a label.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">14. An employee rates job satisfaction highly but gives a low rating to team communication. Is this necessarily inconsistent?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>No. The overall rating can summarize several job facets, while team communication is one particular condition. Different items can reflect different parts of a complex evaluation, and their meanings should be checked.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">15. Explain why extra-role citizenship behaviour can benefit an organization and still require attention to employee well-being.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>OCB can reduce supervision, improve morale, produce cost-saving suggestions, and free managerial time. If extra-role contributions become excessive, managers should consider burnout and help employees contribute sustainably.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-1-organizational-behaviour-foundations.html\"><small>Previous</small>Organizational Behaviour Foundations <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work.html\"><small>Next</small>Personality at Work <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-3-personality-at-work":{"title":"Personality at Work · OB and Management","description":"Compare personality frameworks and see why workplace rules and cues change what traits predict.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Individual behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 3</div><h1>Personality at Work</h1><p class=\"lead\">Compare personality frameworks and see why workplace rules and cues change what traits predict.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 8 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">A pattern, expressed in a situation</a><a href=\"#section-1\">Personality, traits, and development</a><a href=\"#section-2\">Two frameworks for describing personality</a><a href=\"#section-3\">The Big Five: dimensions and workplace connections</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Other personality frameworks: the Dark Triad</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Personality and situations</a><a href=\"#section-6\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-8\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">A pattern, expressed in a situation</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Traits describe relatively stable patterns, not an explanation for every isolated action.</li><li>MBTI uses preference categories. The Big Five describes trait dimensions.</li><li>Strong rules constrain behaviour; trait-relevant cues can bring particular tendencies into play.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">Personality, traits, and development</h2>\n<p>Personality consists of relatively stable characteristics and traits that help explain behaviour. Independence, conscientiousness, agreeableness, and self-control are examples. A single action is not enough to establish a trait: a characteristic is more useful as a trait description when it appears consistently over time and frequently across diverse situations. Stability is a pattern, not a promise that behaviour never changes.</p>\n<p>Heredity influences personality, while development continues throughout life. The lecture says personality is more changeable in adolescence than adulthood. Neither inherited influence nor relative stability means that genes determine conduct or adults cannot change.</p>\n<p>The work environment is also relevant to how personality is expressed. Rules, coworkers, incentives, and situational demands can constrain behaviour or make some tendencies more relevant. The lecture develops this person–situation interaction later; it does not provide a separate environmental-development model that would justify assigning a person's traits from their background.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: distinguish a trait pattern from one response</summary><p>A coordinator speaks up in one tense meeting. That event alone does not establish that the person is generally assertive. If the person regularly offers views across different teams and over time, assertiveness may be a useful trait description. Even then, a quiet response in a formal meeting does not disprove the pattern: the meeting's expectations may constrain what is expressed.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">Two frameworks for describing personality</h2>\n<p>The lecture names the Myers–Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) and the Big Five as dominant frameworks. The MBTI is presented as a 100-question assessment asking how people usually feel or act in situations. It organizes preferences into four pairs. Combining one preference from each pair yields 16 labels.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"MBTI preference pairs and course descriptions\">\n<caption>Four MBTI preference pairs</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">MBTI preference pair</th><th scope=\"col\">Course description of the poles</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Extraversion (E) / Introversion (I)</th><td>E: outgoing, sociable, assertive; I: quiet and shy in the slide's shorthand</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Sensing (S) / Intuition (N)</th><td>S: practical, routine, order, detail; N: unconscious processes and the big picture</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Thinking (T) / Feeling (F)</th><td>T: reason and logic; F: personal values and emotions</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Judging (J) / Perceiving (P)</th><td>J: control, order, and structure; P: flexible and spontaneous</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The 16-code poster illustrates combinations, but its short type descriptions should not be treated as complete portraits of people. The lecture reports that some psychologists find theoretical inconsistencies, including the assumption of a static personality that overlooks development and change. One slide asks “Why the Myers-Briggs test is totally meaningless?” but supplies no argument or evidence beneath the headline.</p><p>That headline alone cannot establish the claim.</p>\n<p>MBTI assigns preference categories. The Big Five places people along dimensions. A category does not tell you how close someone is to its boundary, and neither framework guarantees job performance.</p><details><summary>Additional evidence on MBTI measurement</summary><p><strong>Supplemental explanation.</strong> MBTI categories select one preference from each pair; they do not show how strongly someone leans toward that preference. The Big Five instead describes traits along dimensions.</p><p>A 2025 synthesis of MBTI Form M studies found acceptable reliability and validity for scores, while also noting gaps in the evidence base, including a lack of structural-validity studies and limited test–retest evidence. These findings concern Form M and do not turn four-letter categories into fixed identities or work-performance guarantees. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/jcad.70006\">2025 psychometric synthesis</a>; <a href=\"https://www.myersbriggs.org/research-and-library/validity-reliability/\">MBTI type and trait measurement overview</a>.</p></details>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: read an MBTI code as a set of preferences</summary><p>Suppose a questionnaire response yields E, S, T, and J. The four letters mean that the framework has selected one pole from each pair: Extraversion, Sensing, Thinking, and Judging. The result is one of 16 combinations. It does not prove that the employee is always sociable, attends only to details, ignores feelings, or needs every task tightly structured.</p><p>Ask about the actual work and observed behaviour rather than treating the letters as a prediction.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">The Big Five: dimensions and workplace connections</h2>\n<p>The Big Five describes personality through five dimensions. The lecture calls these traits useful for understanding organizational behaviour and presents associations with workplace outcomes. The associations are tendencies and framework-level summaries, not universal rules for hiring or predicting an individual. Scores describe positions on dimensions; they should not be read as moral rankings.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Big Five dimensions, tendencies, and workplace connections\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<caption>Big Five dimensions and qualified workplace connections</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Dimension</th><th scope=\"col\">Higher tendency</th><th scope=\"col\">Lower / opposite tendency</th><th scope=\"col\">Workplace connection described in the lecture</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Extraversion</th><td data-label=\"Higher tendency\">More comfortable with relationships; gregarious, assertive, sociable; may prefer more stimulation</td><td data-label=\"Lower / opposite tendency\">More reserved, quiet, or less comfortable with heavy stimulation; may seek quiet recovery</td><td data-label=\"Workplace connection described in the lecture\">Interpersonal skills, social dominance, and emotional expressiveness are linked in the slide to performance*, leadership, and job/life satisfaction.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Agreeableness</th><td data-label=\"Higher tendency\">Cooperative, warm, trusting; more likely to defer</td><td data-label=\"Lower / opposite tendency\">More cold, disagreeable, or antagonistic in the slide's wording</td><td data-label=\"Workplace connection described in the lecture\">Being better liked and more compliant/conforming is linked to performance* and lower deviant behaviour.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Conscientiousness</th><td data-label=\"Higher tendency\">Reliable, responsible, organized, dependable, persistent; effort and planning</td><td data-label=\"Lower / opposite tendency\">More distractible, disorganized, or unreliable</td><td data-label=\"Workplace connection described in the lecture\">Effort, persistence, drive, discipline, and planning are linked to performance, leadership, and longevity.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Emotional stability (opposite often called neuroticism)</th><td data-label=\"Higher tendency\">Better able to withstand stress; calm, confident, secure</td><td data-label=\"Lower / opposite tendency\">More nervous, anxious, depressed, or insecure in the slide's wording</td><td data-label=\"Workplace connection described in the lecture\">Less negative thinking and hypervigilance are linked to satisfaction and lower stress.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Openness to experience</th><td data-label=\"Higher tendency\">Broader interests, curiosity, creativity, artistic sensitivity, interest in novelty</td><td data-label=\"Lower / opposite tendency\">More conventional and comfortable with the familiar</td><td data-label=\"Workplace connection described in the lecture\">Learning, creativity, flexibility, and autonomy are linked to training performance, leadership, and adaptability to change.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The trait labels summarize broader clusters, not single behaviours. Examples in the lecture include extraversion descriptors such as friendliness and excitement-seeking; agreeableness descriptors such as trust and sympathy; conscientiousness descriptors such as orderliness and self-discipline; neuroticism descriptors such as anxiety and vulnerability; and openness descriptors such as imagination and adventurousness.</p><p>OCEAN is a memory aid for the five dimensions, not a score or assessment by itself.</p>\n<p>The lecture's outcome map uses arrows from each trait to proposed reasons and outcomes. Its performance entries for extraversion and agreeableness carry an asterisk, but the footnote is not visible in the supplied figure; no explanation should be inferred. More broadly, the arrows summarize reported links, not guaranteed causal effects.</p><p>The slide says the Big Five have the most verifiable links to important organizational outcomes, while also emphasizing that they are not the only relevant traits.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: interpret a Big Five pattern with limits</summary><p>A project team member scores relatively high on conscientiousness and openness. The lecture's framework suggests that persistence and planning may help with follow-through, while curiosity may support learning and adapting to changes. The score alone cannot establish how the person will perform: task demands, resources, experience, team norms, and the measurement context also matter.</p><p>Use it as one prompt for a work-related conversation, not as a verdict about capability.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Other personality frameworks: the Dark Triad</h2>\n<p>The lecture introduces Machiavellianism, narcissism, and psychopathy as socially undesirable characteristics that can occur to varying degrees and matter for organizational behaviour. These are trait concepts in this discussion, not clinical diagnoses. Behavioural concerns should be addressed through specific evidence and workplace processes, not by diagnosing a colleague.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Dark Triad trait descriptions and workplace qualifications\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<caption>Dark Triad trait concepts in organizational behaviour</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Trait concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Core description in the lecture</th><th scope=\"col\">Possible workplace relevance and qualification</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Machiavellianism</th><td data-label=\"Core description in the lecture\">Pragmatic, emotionally distant, and willing to treat ends as justifying means; “if it works, use it” captures the slide's shorthand.</td><td data-label=\"Possible workplace relevance and qualification\">The slides associate higher Machiavellianism with manipulation, short-term advantage, aggression, and counterproductive behaviour. They also say high-Mach employees “encourage others” more, without defining that phrase. Short-term gains may carry social costs because others may not like the manipulator.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Narcissism</th><td data-label=\"Core description in the lecture\">Grandiose self-importance, a strong need for admiration, entitlement, and arrogance.</td><td data-label=\"Possible workplace relevance and qualification\">The lecture mentions possible charisma and reports associations with leadership, adaptability, and complex business decisions. These are not rules that self-centredness is necessary for success. Trait narcissism is not the same as Narcissistic Personality Disorder.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Psychopathy</th><td data-label=\"Core description in the lecture\">In the OB framing, low concern for others and little guilt or remorse when causing harm. Measures discussed attempt to assess norm compliance, deceit and its effectiveness, impulsivity, and empathic concern.</td><td data-label=\"Possible workplace relevance and qualification\">The lecture portrays workplace risks such as charm, opportunism, manipulation, taking credit for others' work, bullying, and hard influence tactics. These are reported descriptions and associations, not a manager's diagnostic checklist.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The lecture distinguishes <strong>predatory bullying</strong>, where tormenting a vulnerable person is described as an end in itself, from <strong>instrumental bullying</strong>, where bullying is used as a means to reach a goal. This describes a distinction between motives; it does not excuse either behaviour. A person can be held accountable for documented conduct without assigning a personality label.</p>\n<p>Some of the lecture's illustrations need special caution. Slides about selfies, social-media use, and mental-health effects are popular-media graphics and do not establish diagnoses or causal claims. A later graphic contrasts “psychopath” with “sociopath” and lists career prevalence, but the lecture gives no support for treating sociopath as a distinct diagnosis or for the job rankings.</p><p>Do not infer narcissism or psychopathy from phone use, a single conflict, job title, or an informal profile.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: describe harmful conduct without diagnosing</summary><p>A team member repeatedly presents a colleague's work as their own and threatens coworkers who question the credit. A manager can document the incidents, hear from those involved, apply the organization's conduct process, and protect affected staff. The behaviour may resemble risks discussed in the lecture, but it does not allow the manager to diagnose psychopathy or infer a stable personality from an incomplete account.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">Personality and situations</h2>\n<p>Personality's effect on organizational behaviour depends on the situation. Two frameworks explain different parts of that relationship: <strong>situation strength</strong> describes how much the setting constrains appropriate conduct, while <strong>trait activation theory (TAT)</strong> describes how particular situations, events, or interventions make some traits more relevant or expressive.</p>\n<h3>Situation strength</h3>\n<p><strong>Situation strength</strong> is how clearly norms, cues and standards prescribe conduct. Strong situations constrain alternatives; weak situations leave more discretion, so trait differences may predict behaviour better.</p><p>More control is not always better. Tight rules can limit initiative and creativity, and their effects depend on the people and work.</p>\n<p>The lecture's example is swearing. Attention-seeking, conservatism, and risk tolerance may relate to how likely someone is to swear; casual time with friends permits more than a classroom or meeting with a boss. The same person's tendency is therefore more visible in the weaker setting and constrained in the stronger one.</p><p>The slides also report that employees were more likely to behave proactively in line with their values in weaker organizational situations, where there is more room to act.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Source example: compare swearing with friends and in a meeting</summary><p><strong>Setting A:</strong> casually spending time with friends. The norms are relatively permissive, so a person's attention-seeking, conservatism, or risk tolerance may be more visible in whether they swear. <strong>Setting B:</strong> a classroom or meeting with a boss. Expectations are clearer and the social cost of swearing is higher, so the same person is less likely to express that tendency.</p><p>The comparison concerns the behaviour's context; it does not establish a trait from one utterance.</p></details>\n<h3>Trait activation theory</h3>\n<p>Trait activation theory says that some situations, events, or interventions activate one trait more than another. A commission-based compensation plan may make differences in extraversion more relevant because the slide describes extraversion as more reward-sensitive than openness to new experiences.</p><p>A study summarized in the lecture found that individual differences in prosocial tendencies mattered more when coworkers were unsupportive: when support was common, most people behaved prosocially; in a less supportive environment, individual differences became more visible.</p>\n<p>Situation strength and trait activation are related but answer different questions. Strength concerns how strongly norms and standards constrain behaviour overall. Activation concerns whether a cue is relevant to a particular trait. A cue can activate a trait while the wider setting still limits how it is expressed.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:740.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Personality-related behaviour depends on traits and context: relevant cues can activate a trait, while strong norms constrain what is expressed.\" height=\"216pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 740.00 216.00\" width=\"740pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 205.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- traits -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-node1\">\n<title>traits</title>\n<path d=\"M188.47,-194.4C188.47,-194.4 46.12,-194.4 46.12,-194.4 40.12,-194.4 34.12,-188.4 34.12,-182.4 34.12,-182.4 34.12,-156 34.12,-156 34.12,-150 40.12,-144 46.12,-144 46.12,-144 188.47,-144 188.47,-144 194.47,-144 200.47,-150 200.47,-156 200.47,-156 200.47,-182.4 200.47,-182.4 200.47,-188.4 194.47,-194.4 188.47,-194.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-172.95\">Person</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-154.95\">relatively stable traits</text>\n</g>\n<!-- activation -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-node3\">\n<title>activation</title>\n<path d=\"M499.32,-122.4C499.32,-122.4 280.47,-122.4 280.47,-122.4 274.47,-122.4 268.47,-116.4 268.47,-110.4 268.47,-110.4 268.47,-84 268.47,-84 268.47,-78 274.47,-72 280.47,-72 280.47,-72 499.32,-72 499.32,-72 505.32,-72 511.32,-78 511.32,-84 511.32,-84 511.32,-110.4 511.32,-110.4 511.32,-116.4 505.32,-122.4 499.32,-122.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"389.9\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-100.95\">Trait activation</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"389.9\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-82.95\">relevant trait is brought into play</text>\n</g>\n<!-- traits&#45;&gt;activation -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-edge1\">\n<title>traits-&gt;activation</title>\n<path d=\"M200.96,-147.21C227.5,-140.15 257.24,-132.23 285.09,-124.82\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"285.35,-127.11 291.05,-123.24 284.18,-122.71 285.35,-127.11\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- cue -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-node2\">\n<title>cue</title>\n<path d=\"M222.6,-122.4C222.6,-122.4 12,-122.4 12,-122.4 6,-122.4 0,-116.4 0,-110.4 0,-110.4 0,-84 0,-84 0,-78 6,-72 12,-72 12,-72 222.6,-72 222.6,-72 228.6,-72 234.6,-78 234.6,-84 234.6,-84 234.6,-110.4 234.6,-110.4 234.6,-116.4 228.6,-122.4 222.6,-122.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-100.95\">Trait-relevant cue</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-82.95\">situation, event, or intervention</text>\n</g>\n<!-- cue&#45;&gt;activation -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-edge2\">\n<title>cue-&gt;activation</title>\n<path d=\"M235.02,-97.2C243.45,-97.2 251.99,-97.2 260.48,-97.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"260.25,-99.48 266.75,-97.2 260.25,-94.93 260.25,-99.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- expression -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-node5\">\n<title>expression</title>\n<path d=\"M706.3,-86.4C706.3,-86.4 557.2,-86.4 557.2,-86.4 551.2,-86.4 545.2,-80.4 545.2,-74.4 545.2,-74.4 545.2,-48 545.2,-48 545.2,-42 551.2,-36 557.2,-36 557.2,-36 706.3,-36 706.3,-36 712.3,-36 718.3,-42 718.3,-48 718.3,-48 718.3,-74.4 718.3,-74.4 718.3,-80.4 712.3,-86.4 706.3,-86.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"631.75\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-64.95\">Behaviour</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"631.75\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-46.95\">what is expressed here</text>\n</g>\n<!-- activation&#45;&gt;expression -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-edge3\">\n<title>activation-&gt;expression</title>\n<path d=\"M511.53,-79.09C520.01,-77.82 528.5,-76.55 536.8,-75.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"537.12,-77.55 543.21,-74.34 536.45,-73.05 537.12,-77.55\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- norms -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-node4\">\n<title>norms</title>\n<path d=\"M187.72,-50.4C187.72,-50.4 46.87,-50.4 46.87,-50.4 40.87,-50.4 34.87,-44.4 34.87,-38.4 34.87,-38.4 34.87,-12 34.87,-12 34.87,-6 40.87,0 46.87,0 46.87,0 187.72,0 187.72,0 193.72,0 199.72,-6 199.72,-12 199.72,-12 199.72,-38.4 199.72,-38.4 199.72,-44.4 193.72,-50.4 187.72,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Norms and standards</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">expected conduct</text>\n</g>\n<!-- strength -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-node6\">\n<title>strength</title>\n<path d=\"M501.2,-50.4C501.2,-50.4 278.6,-50.4 278.6,-50.4 272.6,-50.4 266.6,-44.4 266.6,-38.4 266.6,-38.4 266.6,-12 266.6,-12 266.6,-6 272.6,0 278.6,0 278.6,0 501.2,0 501.2,0 507.2,0 513.2,-6 513.2,-12 513.2,-12 513.2,-38.4 513.2,-38.4 513.2,-44.4 507.2,-50.4 501.2,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"389.9\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Situation strength</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"389.9\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">how much conduct is constrained</text>\n</g>\n<!-- norms&#45;&gt;strength -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-edge4\">\n<title>norms-&gt;strength</title>\n<path d=\"M200.22,-25.2C218.63,-25.2 238.6,-25.2 258.43,-25.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"258.3,-27.48 264.8,-25.2 258.3,-22.93 258.3,-27.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- strength&#45;&gt;expression -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work-figure-1-lecture-3-personality-at-work-person-situation-interaction-edge5\">\n<title>strength-&gt;expression</title>\n<path d=\"M513.64,-43.62C521.59,-44.82 529.52,-46.01 537.3,-47.17\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"536.76,-49.39 543.53,-48.11 537.44,-44.89 536.76,-49.39\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Personality-related behaviour depends on traits and context: relevant cues can activate a trait, while strong norms constrain what is expressed.</figcaption></figure>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: one employee across two work demands</summary><p>An employee who enjoys social contact may find a commission-heavy sales role especially relevant to extraversion: the incentive can activate differences in approach to social interaction and reward. During a high-stakes compliance check, however, clear procedures and review standards constrain how work is done. The first case illustrates trait activation; the second illustrates situation strength.</p><p>Neither setting reveals the employee's whole personality, and neither guarantees a particular outcome.</p></details>\n<section aria-label=\"What does the evidence support?\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"person-situation\"><h3>What does the evidence support?</h3><label>Choose a case<select aria-label=\"Choose a case\" data-case=\"\"></select></label><p data-scenario=\"\"></p><div aria-label=\"Choose an answer\" class=\"activity-choices\" data-choices=\"\" role=\"group\"></div><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-feedback=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Teaching cases. Choose an answer, read why it fits, then try another case.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript for this activity. The definitions and worked examples above remain available.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-6\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating “stable” as fixed, or heredity as destiny. The lecture explicitly allows development and lifetime change.</li>\n<li>Inferring a trait from one action instead of looking for consistency across time and varied situations.</li>\n<li>Treating an MBTI code as a diagnosis, an immutable identity, or a work-performance forecast. The headline-only “meaningless” slide is not evidence either.</li>\n<li>Reading Big Five workplace links as promises about every person, or interpreting one dimension as morally good or bad.</li>\n<li>Confusing trait narcissism or psychopathy with a clinical disorder; treating social-media habits, job titles, or one conflict as diagnosis.</li>\n<li>Assuming workplaces are always strong situations, that strong control is always helpful, or that situation strength and trait activation mean the same thing.</li>\n<li>Assuming a trait produces identical visible conduct in every context. Norms, incentives, cues, and discretion shape its expression.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-7\"></span><h2 id=\"section-8\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">1. What makes a characteristic more useful to describe as a personality trait?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>A characteristic is more trait-like when it appears consistently over time and frequently across diverse situations. One isolated action is not enough.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">2. Name the four MBTI pairs and state one limitation highlighted in the lecture.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Extraversion–Introversion, Sensing–Intuition, Thinking–Feeling, and Judging–Perceiving. The lecture reports that critics see theoretical inconsistencies, including an assumption of static personality that overlooks development. The headline-only slide does not provide evidence for calling the instrument meaningless.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">3. A colleague follows a careful checklist in a regulated meeting but improvises during an informal brainstorming session. How can situation strength help explain this difference?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The regulated meeting has clearer standards and stronger constraints, leaving less discretion for traits to predict visible behaviour. The informal brainstorming session is weaker, so individual tendencies may be more visible. This does not mean the person's underlying personality changed between settings.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">4. How does a commission plan illustrate trait activation theory?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The incentive can make differences in extraversion more relevant or expressive because the lecture describes extraversion as more reward-sensitive than openness. It is an example of a cue activating a trait, not a guarantee that every extravert will succeed in sales.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">5. Why should a manager address documented bullying without diagnosing the person involved?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The Dark Triad concepts describe traits, not a manager's clinical diagnosis. Specific conduct can be documented and addressed through workplace procedures without inferring a disorder or a fixed personality.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-2-work-attitudes-and-job-satisfaction.html\"><small>Previous</small>Work Attitudes and Job Satisfaction <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit.html\"><small>Next</small>Values, Culture, and Person-Environment Fit <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit":{"title":"Values, Culture, and Person-Environment Fit · OB and Management","description":"Separate values, job fit and organizational fit without turning national culture into an individual label.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Individual behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 3</div><h1>Values, Culture, and Person-Environment Fit</h1><p class=\"lead\">Separate values, job fit and organizational fit without turning national culture into an individual label.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 7 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">What matters, and where it fits</a><a href=\"#section-1\">What values mean at work</a><a href=\"#section-2\">Person–environment fit</a><a href=\"#section-3\">National culture and Hofstede’s framework</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Job purposing: put values into everyday work</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">What matters, and where it fits</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Values rank what is desirable. Watch everyday choices as well as stated principles.</li><li>Fit with job tasks and fit with organizational culture can differ.</li><li>National-culture dimensions describe broad patterns, with variation inside every country.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">What values mean at work</h2>\n<p>A value is a basic belief that a way of behaving or an end-state is personally or socially preferable to its opposite. Values carry a sense of what ought or ought not to happen. People bring these standards into an organization. They can help someone notice what matters in a decision, but they can also cloud objectivity.</p><p>The lecture says values influence workplace attitudes and behaviour.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplemental explanation.</strong> Keep three ideas separate. A <strong>belief</strong> describes what someone thinks is true. An <strong>attitude</strong> evaluates a person, object, or situation. A <strong>value</strong> says what outcome or conduct is desirable.</p><p>For example, “this report may contain an error” is a belief about the report; “I dislike the review process” is an attitude; “we ought to correct safety information before release” is a value. An attitude or action alone does not reveal a person’s complete value system.</p>\n<h3>Personal values and work values</h3>\n<p>Personal values concern what matters across life; work values concern what matters at work. Their priorities can differ by situation. Compare an organization’s stated principles with its routine decisions to understand the values people experience.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: distinguish a value from a belief and an attitude</summary><p>An analyst thinks a safety report contains an uncertain measurement. That is a <strong>belief</strong> about what the evidence may show. The analyst feels frustrated by the approval process. That is an <strong>attitude</strong> toward the process. The analyst believes the team ought to check a plausible safety risk before closing the report. That is a <strong>value</strong> about responsible conduct.</p><p>The analyst’s next action may reflect all three, along with deadlines and workplace rules.</p></details>\n<p><strong>Supplemental explanation.</strong> A value has a <em>content</em>, meaning which end or mode of conduct matters, and an <em>intensity</em>, meaning how strongly it matters compared with other values. Priorities across values form a value system. This distinction helps explain why two people can endorse fairness yet differ when fairness conflicts with speed. The lecture itself classifies values by their ends and means below.</p>\n<h3>Terminal and instrumental values</h3>\n<p>Rokeach’s distinction separates the desired <strong>end</strong> from the preferred <strong>means</strong>. A terminal value is a desirable end-state or lifetime goal. The lecture’s example is economic success and prosperity. An instrumental value is a preferred mode of behaviour used to reach an end. The lecture gives personal discipline and being goal-oriented as examples.</p><p>People can value both what they want to achieve and how they pursue it, so the slides emphasize balancing ends and means.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Rokeach terminal and instrumental values\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Classification</th><th scope=\"col\">Question it answers</th><th scope=\"col\">Lecture example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Terminal value</th><td>Which end-state is desirable?</td><td>Economic success and prosperity</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Instrumental value</th><td>Which conduct is a preferred means?</td><td>Personal discipline; goal-oriented behaviour</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: connect a desired end with its means</summary><p>A person wants economic success and prosperity. That desired outcome is the terminal value. The person uses personal discipline and goal-oriented effort as preferred ways to pursue it. The same end could be pursued through other behaviours, so a goal alone does not tell us which means someone values. A conflict may arise if a chosen means undermines another important value.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">Person–environment fit</h2>\n<p>The lecture considers how well people match work settings. Personality describes characteristic tendencies; values describe preferred ends and standards. They can both matter when a person weighs a job or organization, but they are not interchangeable. Holland’s person–job fit theory focuses on matching personality characteristics to job requirements.</p><p>Person–organization fit focuses on compatibility between a person’s values/personality and an organization’s culture. These are related questions with different comparison targets.</p>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes applicants’ flexibility across changing situations and commitment to the organization over their ability to perform one specific job. This is its stated hiring emphasis, not a universal rule for every role.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:696.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Two distinct fit questions: how a person matches job demands, and how a person matches an organization’s values and culture. The lines show comparison, not cause.\" height=\"144pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 696.00 144.00\" width=\"696pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-figure-1-lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-person-environment-fit-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 133.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- person -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-figure-1-lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-person-environment-fit-node1\">\n<title>person</title>\n<ellipse cx=\"150.51\" cy=\"-61.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" rx=\"150.51\" ry=\"35.64\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></ellipse>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"150.51\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-64.95\">Person</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"150.51\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-46.95\">interests, personality, values</text>\n</g>\n<!-- job -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-figure-1-lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-person-environment-fit-node2\">\n<title>job</title>\n<path d=\"M662.87,-122.4C662.87,-122.4 471.77,-122.4 471.77,-122.4 465.77,-122.4 459.77,-116.4 459.77,-110.4 459.77,-110.4 459.77,-84 459.77,-84 459.77,-78 465.77,-72 471.77,-72 471.77,-72 662.87,-72 662.87,-72 668.87,-72 674.87,-78 674.87,-84 674.87,-84 674.87,-110.4 674.87,-110.4 674.87,-116.4 668.87,-122.4 662.87,-122.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"567.32\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-100.95\">Job</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"567.32\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-82.95\">requirements and daily tasks</text>\n</g>\n<!-- person&#45;&gt;job -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-figure-1-lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-person-environment-fit-edge1\">\n<title>person-&gt;job</title>\n<path d=\"M292.52,-73.44C347.13,-78.18 408.6,-83.51 459.44,-87.92\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"12.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"380.39\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-89.41\">person–job fit</text>\n</g>\n<!-- organization -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-figure-1-lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-person-environment-fit-node3\">\n<title>organization</title>\n<path d=\"M627.62,-50.4C627.62,-50.4 507.02,-50.4 507.02,-50.4 501.02,-50.4 495.02,-44.4 495.02,-38.4 495.02,-38.4 495.02,-12 495.02,-12 495.02,-6 501.02,0 507.02,0 507.02,0 627.62,0 627.62,0 633.62,0 639.62,-6 639.62,-12 639.62,-12 639.62,-38.4 639.62,-38.4 639.62,-44.4 633.62,-50.4 627.62,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"567.32\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Organization</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"567.32\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">values and culture</text>\n</g>\n<!-- person&#45;&gt;organization -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-figure-1-lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit-person-environment-fit-edge2\">\n<title>person-&gt;organization</title>\n<path d=\"M292.52,-48.96C360.52,-43.06 439.15,-36.24 494.66,-31.42\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"12.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"380.39\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-49.27\">person–organization fit</text>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Two distinct fit questions: how a person matches job demands, and how a person matches an organization’s values and culture. The lines show comparison, not cause.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Holland’s person–job fit and RIASEC</h3>\n<p>Holland’s theory presents six personality and occupational orientations. The lecture says satisfaction should be highest and the tendency to leave lowest when a person and occupation agree. Its table links preferred activities, described characteristics, and congruent occupations. These are broad theory categories, not fixed identities or hiring rules.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Holland RIASEC types, preferences, characteristics, and lecture occupations\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Type</th><th scope=\"col\">Preferred activities</th><th scope=\"col\">Characteristics listed in the lecture</th><th scope=\"col\">Congruent occupations from the lecture</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Realistic (R)</th><td data-label=\"Preferred activities\">Physical activity needing skill, strength, and coordination</td><td data-label=\"Characteristics listed in the lecture\">Shy, genuine, persistent, stable, conforming, practical</td><td data-label=\"Congruent occupations from the lecture\">Mechanic, drill-press operator, assembly-line worker, farmer</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Investigative (I)</th><td data-label=\"Preferred activities\">Thinking, organizing, and understanding</td><td data-label=\"Characteristics listed in the lecture\">Analytical, original, curious, independent</td><td data-label=\"Congruent occupations from the lecture\">Biologist, economist, mathematician, news reporter</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Artistic (A)</th><td data-label=\"Preferred activities\">Ambiguous, unsystematic activity that allows creative expression</td><td data-label=\"Characteristics listed in the lecture\">Imaginative, disorderly, idealistic, emotional, impractical</td><td data-label=\"Congruent occupations from the lecture\">Painter, musician, writer, interior decorator</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Social (S)</th><td data-label=\"Preferred activities\">Helping and developing others</td><td data-label=\"Characteristics listed in the lecture\">Sociable, friendly, cooperative, understanding</td><td data-label=\"Congruent occupations from the lecture\">Social worker, teacher, counsellor, clinical psychologist</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Enterprising (E)</th><td data-label=\"Preferred activities\">Verbal activity that influences others and may attain power</td><td data-label=\"Characteristics listed in the lecture\">Self-confident, ambitious, energetic, domineering</td><td data-label=\"Congruent occupations from the lecture\">Lawyer, real-estate agent, public-relations specialist, small-business manager</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Conventional (C)</th><td data-label=\"Preferred activities\">Rule-regulated, orderly, unambiguous activity</td><td data-label=\"Characteristics listed in the lecture\">Conforming, efficient, practical, unimaginative, inflexible</td><td data-label=\"Congruent occupations from the lecture\">Accountant, corporate manager, bank teller, file clerk</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The source hexagon places <strong>Realistic, Investigative, Artistic, Social, Enterprising, and Conventional</strong> in that order around six sides, returning to Realistic. Adjacent categories are described as similar and more compatible. Diagonally opposite categories are highly dissimilar. The hexagon represents similarity, not a sequence of development or a causal path.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:720px\"><svg aria-label=\"RIASEC hexagon with all fifteen undirected connections\" height=\"439\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"-25 0 810 490\" width=\"720\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" x1=\"240\" x2=\"480\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"80\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--muted,#526570)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"240\" x2=\"600\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"250\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"240\" x2=\"480\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"420\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--muted,#526570)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"240\" x2=\"240\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"420\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" x1=\"240\" x2=\"120\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"250\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" x1=\"480\" x2=\"600\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"250\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--muted,#526570)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"480\" x2=\"480\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"420\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"480\" x2=\"240\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"420\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--muted,#526570)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"480\" x2=\"120\" y1=\"80\" y2=\"250\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" x1=\"600\" x2=\"480\" y1=\"250\" y2=\"420\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--muted,#526570)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"600\" x2=\"240\" y1=\"250\" y2=\"420\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"600\" x2=\"120\" y1=\"250\" y2=\"250\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" x1=\"480\" x2=\"240\" y1=\"420\" y2=\"420\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--muted,#526570)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" x1=\"480\" x2=\"120\" y1=\"420\" y2=\"250\"></line>\n<line stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" x1=\"240\" x2=\"120\" y1=\"420\" y2=\"250\"></line>\n<circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"80\" fill=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" r=\"5\"></circle>\n<text fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" font-family=\"Georgia,serif\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"240\" y=\"32\">Realistic<tspan dy=\"22\" x=\"240\">(R)</tspan></text>\n<circle cx=\"480\" cy=\"80\" fill=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" r=\"5\"></circle>\n<text fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" font-family=\"Georgia,serif\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"480\" y=\"32\">Investigative<tspan dy=\"22\" x=\"480\">(I)</tspan></text>\n<circle cx=\"600\" cy=\"250\" fill=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" r=\"5\"></circle>\n<text fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" font-family=\"Georgia,serif\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"614\" y=\"245\">Artistic<tspan dy=\"22\" x=\"614\">(A)</tspan></text>\n<circle cx=\"480\" cy=\"420\" fill=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" r=\"5\"></circle>\n<text fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" font-family=\"Georgia,serif\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"480\" y=\"452\">Social<tspan dy=\"22\" x=\"480\">(S)</tspan></text>\n<circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"420\" fill=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" r=\"5\"></circle>\n<text fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" font-family=\"Georgia,serif\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"240\" y=\"452\">Enterprising<tspan dy=\"22\" x=\"240\">(E)</tspan></text>\n<circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"250\" fill=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" r=\"5\"></circle>\n<text fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" font-family=\"Georgia,serif\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"end\" x=\"106\" y=\"245\">Conventional<tspan dy=\"22\" x=\"106\">(C)</tspan></text>\n</svg></div><figcaption>RIASEC: all fifteen undirected connections are shown. Neighbours are more similar; dashed opposite pairs are highly dissimilar.</figcaption></figure>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Source example: compare three Realistic job matches</summary><p>A Realistic orientation prefers practical physical activities involving skill, strength, or coordination. A mechanic is listed as a congruent occupation, so this is the clearest match of the three examples. An Investigative occupation such as biologist is adjacent to Realistic on the lecture’s hexagon and is less congruent in the slide’s comparison.</p><p>Social is opposite Realistic, and the lecture calls a Realistic person in a Social job the most incongruent case. This is Holland’s prediction about fit, not a guarantee about an individual’s satisfaction or ability.</p></details>\n<h3>Person–organization fit</h3>\n<p>Person–organization fit asks whether a person’s values and personality are compatible with an organization’s culture. The lecture’s theory says people are attracted to and selected by organizations that match their values, and may leave organizations that do not fit their personalities. It proposes that stronger cultural fit may support satisfaction and lower turnover.</p><p>Fit should be assessed with evidence about the person and the actual workplace. It does not mean that every employee should think alike or that job competence can be ignored.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: separate job fit from organization fit</summary><p>A person enjoys helping and developing others but applies for a role dominated by solitary data validation. The daily task mix raises a person–job fit question. Whether the employer’s culture values careful service, collaboration, or rapid independent completion raises a separate person–organization fit question.</p><p>Before judging either match, learn more about the actual tasks, the person’s preferences, and how the organization works. One interest alone cannot decide the outcome.</p></details>\n<section aria-label=\"Fit with which part of work?\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"fit\"><h3>Fit with which part of work?</h3><label>Choose a case<select aria-label=\"Choose a case\" data-case=\"\"></select></label><p data-scenario=\"\"></p><div aria-label=\"Choose an answer\" class=\"activity-choices\" data-choices=\"\" role=\"group\"></div><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-feedback=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Teaching cases. Choose an answer, read why it fits, then try another case.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript for this activity. The definitions and worked examples above remain available.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-3\">National culture and Hofstede’s framework</h2>\n<p>Hofstede’s framework compares national-culture patterns. The deck is inconsistent: slide 72 says five dimensions; slide 73 shows six. The table follows the six-dimension graphic and preserves its endpoint wording.</p><details><summary>Source context for the six dimensions</summary><p>The slide describes more than 116,000 IBM employees in 40 countries. The framework developed over time; the final two dimensions came from later work. These are national comparisons with substantial within-country variation. <a href=\"https://geerthofstede.com/culture-geert-hofstede-gert-jan-hofstede/6d-model-of-national-culture/\">Hofstede’s six-dimension model</a>.</p></details>\n<table aria-label=\"Six Hofstede cultural dimensions and the endpoint wording in the lecture graphic\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Dimension</th><th scope=\"col\">Lower end in the graphic</th><th scope=\"col\">Higher end in the graphic</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Power distance</th><td>Egalitarian</td><td>Embraces hierarchy</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Collectivism vs. individualism</th><td>Collectivist</td><td>Individualist; greater priority to individual goals over group goals</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Uncertainty avoidance</th><td>Comfortable with uncertainty</td><td>Uncomfortable with uncertainty</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Femininity vs. masculinity</th><td>Nurture is important</td><td>Power is important</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Long-term orientation</th><td>Traditional and short-term</td><td>Futuristic and long-term</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Restraint vs. indulgence</th><td>Restraint is good</td><td>Satisfaction is good</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>These dimensions compare cultural patterns at a broad level. They are not personality categories, individual employee scores, or reliable explanations of one person’s choices. A request for written decision rationale, for example, may reflect role needs, past team experience, or individual preference. Nationality alone does not establish which value a colleague holds.</p><p>Reasoning from a country-level pattern to an individual is an ecological fallacy. A related research discussion warns against carrying national-culture associations down to individuals or organizations: <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0170840613517602\">Brewer and Venaik, “The Ecological Fallacy in National Culture Research” (2014)</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: respond to a request for written decisions</summary><p>An international teammate asks the group to document why it chose one proposal. Do not infer a national-culture dimension from that request. Ask what information the role requires, whether the teammate has had trouble tracing past decisions, and what format would help the team.</p><p>Hofstede’s framework can prompt a careful comparison of group-level patterns, but it cannot tell you this colleague’s motive or preference by itself.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Job purposing: put values into everyday work</h2>\n<p>Bea Boccalandro calls small changes to ordinary work that contribute to a social purpose <strong>job purposing</strong>. A worker can put values into daily interactions and decisions without changing employers or waiting for a company-wide program.</p>\n<p>Examples in the article include inviting quieter colleagues into discussion, addressing bias in property sales and buying from minority-run small businesses. These illustrate possible actions, not universal instructions.</p>\n<details><summary>What the assigned article reports about outcomes</summary><p>The author reports 13% higher average job satisfaction and higher likelihoods of raises and promotion in research on social purpose. An image-labeling experiment and another study are separate examples. The latter reports 24% faster work and 43% less downtime without lower quality; those figures should not be assigned to the image-labeling experiment.</p><p>These are the HBR author’s research summaries. The underlying studies were not independently replicated here, and the numbers are not guaranteed individual outcomes.</p></details>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Source case: Tom supports a colleague during a work interaction</summary><p><strong>Situation:</strong> An injured patient objects to a woman patroller handling the sled. <strong>Value at stake:</strong> respectful and fair treatment at work. <strong>Job-purposing action:</strong> Tom says the patient’s objection has no basis and points to the patroller’s competence. He uses an ordinary work interaction to support a broader social purpose while continuing the task.</p><p><strong>Reported outcome:</strong> the author says the patient apologized about a year later. That ending belongs to the author’s story; it does not show that every challenge will change someone’s views or be safe in every context.</p></details>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: find a values-based action within a routine task</summary><p>An analyst values accuracy and public service, while the team’s written process emphasizes closing reports quickly. The analyst notices an uncertain safety result and proposes a short escalation check before closure. Accuracy and public service are values; the uncertainty about the measurement is a belief; frustration with delay would be an attitude.</p><p>The analyst has adapted a routine task toward a social purpose. The team still needs to check the evidence, authority, and applicable procedure before acting.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating values, beliefs, attitudes, and personality as interchangeable. They answer different questions.</li>\n<li>Reversing Rokeach’s categories: terminal values are desired ends; instrumental values are preferred means.</li>\n<li>Treating a single action as proof of a person’s values, or assuming a stated organizational principle matches daily practice.</li>\n<li>Confusing person–job fit with person–organization fit. One concerns job demands; the other concerns organizational values and culture.</li>\n<li>Reading the RIASEC hexagon as a causal sequence or assuming a type predicts ability, satisfaction, or career destiny.</li>\n<li>Memorizing five Hofstede dimensions from slide 72 while ignoring the six shown in slides 73–74, or silently pretending the conflict is resolved.</li>\n<li>Using national-culture patterns to assign motives or values to an individual employee.</li>\n<li>Treating the Adam Grant social-media quote on character and personality as a validated definition or empirical result.</li>\n<li>Treating HBR’s anecdotes and reported study figures as universal effects, or confusing job purposing with leaving a job to volunteer.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-6\"></span><h2 id=\"section-7\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">1. Classify each statement: “The report may contain an error”; “I dislike the review process”; “We ought to correct safety information before release.”</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The first is a belief about what may be true. The second is an attitude toward the process. The third is a value about desirable conduct.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">2. In Rokeach’s classification, which is the end and which is the means: economic prosperity, personal discipline?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Economic prosperity is the terminal value, or desired end-state. Personal discipline is an instrumental value, or preferred means.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">3. What is the RIASEC order around the lecture’s hexagon, and what does adjacency mean?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Realistic, Investigative, Artistic, Social, Enterprising, Conventional, then back to Realistic. Adjacent types are described as more similar and compatible. Opposite types are highly dissimilar. The relationships do not imply causation.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">4. A role’s tasks suit an employee, but the organization’s routine decisions conflict with values the employee considers important. Which fit lens addresses each issue?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Person–job fit addresses the match between the person and job requirements or tasks. Person–organization fit addresses compatibility with organizational values and culture.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">5. Why should a manager avoid concluding that an employee holds a particular value because of their nationality?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Hofstede’s framework describes broad national-culture patterns. Applying an aggregate pattern directly to an individual is an ecological fallacy. The manager needs evidence about that employee and the situation.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">6. Slide 72 says five dimensions, but slides 73–74 display six. How should you report the framework from this deck?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>State that slide 72’s historical note says five, while the later diagram shows six. Name the six visible dimensions and acknowledge that the deck does not explain the discrepancy.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">7. What does job purposing mean, and how does Tom’s response in the HBR story illustrate it?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Job purposing means adapting ordinary work actions so they contribute to a social purpose during the workweek. Tom used a work interaction to challenge a patient’s sexist objection and support his colleague’s competence. It is the author’s anecdote, not proof of a universal effect.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">8. How should a student describe the HBR article’s percentage claims in an exam answer?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Attribute them to the HBR author’s summary of studies, distinguish them from the article’s practice examples, and avoid presenting the figures as guaranteed outcomes. The underlying studies were not reviewed for these notes.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-3-personality-at-work.html\"><small>Previous</small>Personality at Work <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods.html\"><small>Next</small>Emotions and Moods <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods":{"title":"Emotions and Moods · OB and Management","description":"Distinguish emotions from moods, then connect emotional labour and daily events to work outcomes.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Individual behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 4</div><h1>Emotions and Moods</h1><p class=\"lead\">Distinguish emotions from moods, then connect emotional labour and daily events to work outcomes.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 11 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">Feelings, displays and work events</a><a href=\"#section-1\">Emotion, feeling, mood, and affect</a><a href=\"#section-2\">Positive and negative affect are separate mood dimensions</a><a href=\"#section-3\">Emotions contribute to rational thought and ethical judgment</a><a href=\"#section-4\">What influences emotions and moods?</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Emotional labour: what is felt and what is displayed?</a><a href=\"#section-6\">Affective Events Theory links the workday to outcomes</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Emotional intelligence and related models</a><a href=\"#section-8\">Personal and organizational ways to manage emotions</a><a href=\"#section-9\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-11\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">Feelings, displays and work events</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>An emotion usually has a specific target; a mood is more diffuse and longer-lasting.</li><li>Surface acting changes the display. Deep acting tries to change the feeling. Dissonance names a mismatch.</li><li>Work events can trigger emotional reactions; personality and mood help explain different reactions.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">Emotion, feeling, mood, and affect</h2>\n<p>An <strong>emotion</strong> is an intense response directed at someone or something. The lecture describes it biologically as chemicals released after a person interprets a specific trigger. It presents a quarter-second to identify a trigger, another quarter-second to produce chemicals, and about six seconds for those chemicals to last.</p><p>Treat these timings as the lecturer’s account, not as universal clocks for every emotion. The lecture also describes the chemicals as moving through the body and forming feedback with the brain.</p>\n<p>A <strong>feeling</strong> is the experience of sensing and thinking about an emotion as it is integrated. Feelings are more cognitively saturated, may draw on a mix of emotions, and last longer than the initial emotion. A <strong>mood</strong> is generally less intense, can lack a clear trigger, and may last hours or days.</p><p>The lecture groups mood influences into the environment, physiology, and mental state. <strong>Affect</strong> is the broad term for the range of feelings that includes emotions and moods.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Emotion, feeling, mood, and affect compared by trigger, duration, and role\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<caption>Four related terms in the lecture</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">What it describes</th><th scope=\"col\">Trigger and time course</th><th scope=\"col\">Useful clue</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Emotion</th><td data-label=\"What it describes\">Intense response directed toward a person or thing</td><td data-label=\"Trigger and time course\">Often follows a specific interpreted event; brief, often seconds or minutes</td><td data-label=\"Useful clue\">Specific and action oriented; may have a distinct facial expression</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Feeling</th><td data-label=\"What it describes\">The sensed and thought-about experience as an emotion is integrated</td><td data-label=\"Trigger and time course\">Can combine emotions and outlast the initial emotional response</td><td data-label=\"Useful clue\">More cognitively saturated</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Mood</th><td data-label=\"What it describes\">Less intense, broader state of mind</td><td data-label=\"Trigger and time course\">May have no clear contextual trigger; can last hours or days</td><td data-label=\"Useful clue\">Often general, cognitive, and not shown by one distinct expression</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Affect</th><td data-label=\"What it describes\">Umbrella term for a broad range of experienced feelings</td><td data-label=\"Trigger and time course\">Includes emotions and moods</td><td data-label=\"Useful clue\">Names the wider domain rather than one time stage</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Slide 13 describes a sequence in which emotions come first, feelings follow as the emotion is processed, and moods develop from combinations of feelings. The slide 14 figure separately places emotions and moods under affect and shows a two-way relationship between emotion and mood. Together, these ideas help describe common patterns without implying that every mood comes from one discrete event.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: separate an event reaction from the mood that follows</summary><p>A colleague criticizes your report in a meeting. The criticism may trigger brief anger directed at that colleague or the feedback. As you think about the comment, you may feel embarrassed or disappointed as well as angry. If several difficult interactions continue through the afternoon, you may notice a broader low mood without identifying one single cause.</p><p>Affect is the umbrella for the emotional and mood experience. These labels describe different aspects of the same day, not four interchangeable words.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">Positive and negative affect are separate mood dimensions</h2>\n<p>The lecture describes <strong>positive affect</strong> and <strong>negative affect</strong> as two dimensions of mood. High positive affect includes excitement, enthusiasm, and elation; low positive affect includes boredom, depression, and fatigue. High negative affect includes nervousness, stress, and anxiety; low negative affect includes contentedness, calmness, and serenity. Each dimension has its own high and low end.</p><p>A person can therefore be low in negative affect without being highly excited, or high in positive affect while also feeling nervous about a demanding event.</p>\n<p>The slides also raise cross-cultural questions and show body maps for 13 emotions. Their caption says the mapped patterns were nearly identical across cultures and languages. Because the slide gives no full study citation, retain this as a claim made by the visual rather than a universal conclusion about how every person experiences emotion.</p>\n<p>The lecture first describes positive emotions such as joy and gratitude as favourable evaluations, and negative emotions such as anger and guilt as unfavourable evaluations. It then connects the broad categories with positive and negative mood states. The separate affect dimensions below clarify that a person may vary in either dimension.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: locate two feelings on separate affect dimensions</summary><p>A presenter feels enthusiastic about sharing a new proposal and nervous about questions from senior managers. Enthusiasm suggests high positive affect, while nervousness suggests high negative affect. The two can coexist because the dimensions are separate. Calling one feeling “positive” does not erase the other.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Emotions contribute to rational thought and ethical judgment</h2>\n<p>The lecture challenges the idea that emotion automatically makes a person irrational. It reports that brain-injury studies suggest the capacity to experience emotion supports rationality because emotion provides context for understanding the world. It also describes a study in which people in a negative mood recognized truthful information better than people in a happy mood.</p><p>This is an example reported by the lecture, not a rule that negative moods always improve judgment.</p>\n<p>The practical point is to notice how emotions and moods may be affecting a decision instead of automatically dismissing emotional responses as invalid. Emotions also inform ethical judgment. The lecture names sympathy for another person’s suffering, guilt about one’s own immoral behaviour, anger at injustice, contempt for unethical conduct, and disgust at violations of moral norms.</p><p>It says moral judgments are often feeling based, while fairness assessments retain a cognitive component. People may experience their moral boundaries as logical even when feelings contribute to them.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: use an emotion as information during an ethical decision</summary><p>A supervisor learns that a high-performing employee took credit for a teammate’s work. Anger at the unfairness may signal that an ethical boundary matters here. The supervisor can acknowledge that reaction, check what happened, hear from the people involved, and apply the same standards used in comparable cases. The feeling is relevant context, but it does not replace evidence or a fair process.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">What influences emotions and moods?</h2>\n<p>The lecture separates influences into <strong>who a person is</strong> and <strong>the circumstances at the time</strong>. Personality contributes to tendencies to experience some moods or emotions more often. <strong>Affect intensity</strong> describes how strongly a person experiences both positive and negative emotions. These differences can shape reactions, but they do not let a manager predict an individual’s state from a category or demographic label.</p>\n<p>The lecture’s glass-half-full/glass-half-empty illustration connects personality to a more optimistic or pessimistic way of interpreting circumstances. It is an illustration of a tendency in perspective, not a rule for classifying a person from one comment. Affectively intense people may experience both positive and negative emotions more deeply, so a happy period can feel especially happy and a sad period especially sad.</p>\n<p>The slides report findings about age and gender. One study covering ages 18 to 94 found that negative emotions appeared less with age, highly positive moods lasted longer among older participants, and bad moods faded faster. The gender slide reports greater emotional expressiveness and intensity among women, longer retention, and more frequent displays of positive and negative emotions except anger.</p><p>These are findings as presented by the lecture. They should not be used as rules for judging an individual employee.</p>\n<p>Sleep, work stress, social activity and physical condition can influence mood. People vary, so a general pattern cannot identify why one colleague feels a particular way.</p><details><summary>Patterns reported in the lecture</summary><ul><li>Time: the slides describe daily affect rhythms and higher positive affect near the weekend in US adults, with similar patterns in several cultures.</li><li>Weather: little effect for most people in the lecture’s account.</li><li>Stress: hostile messages, deadlines, reprimands and accumulating demands can lower mood.</li><li>Activity: informal, physical and shared-meal activities are associated with positive mood more than formal meetings or sedentary activities.</li><li>Sleep and exercise: poor sleep can increase fatigue and irritability. Exercise is associated with improved positive mood, with a qualified overall effect.</li><li>Physical health: the slides discuss fatigue, illness and blood sugar. These are lecture examples of influences, not a way to diagnose a coworker.</li></ul></details>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: distinguish a person factor from a situational factor</summary><p>Two analysts receive the same last-minute deadline change. One may experience a stronger reaction because of affect intensity, a person-level tendency. Both may also be tired after poor sleep, a situational or physiological condition at the time. The deadline is a work stressor that can affect mood, and repeated stressors may accumulate.</p><p>The example does not establish a diagnosis or predict how either analyst will respond.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">Emotional labour: what is felt and what is displayed?</h2>\n<p><strong>Emotional labour</strong> is the expression of organizationally desired emotions during interpersonal transactions at work. The lecture calls it an important part of effective job performance. <strong>Felt emotions</strong> are what an employee actually experiences. <strong>Displayed emotions</strong> are the expressions the organization expects and considers appropriate for a job. Display rules are learned, and the required display may differ from what the person feels.</p>\n<p><strong>Surface acting</strong> changes the outward display while hiding or suppressing the inner feeling. <strong>Deep acting</strong> tries to change the felt emotion to align it with the display rule. <strong>Emotional dissonance</strong> is the disparity that occurs when someone projects one emotion while feeling another. Dissonance names the mismatch; surface and deep acting name different ways of responding to display expectations.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: customer service and the two acting strategies</summary><p>A customer insults a service worker. The worker feels anger, while the job’s display rule calls for a courteous response. If the worker hides the anger and smiles without changing the feeling, that is surface acting.</p><p>If the worker reframes the situation and tries to understand why the customer is upset, the worker is attempting deep acting by changing the felt emotion. Projecting courtesy while still feeling anger creates emotional dissonance. The example shows possible responses, not a requirement that workers accept mistreatment.</p></details>\n<section aria-label=\"Name the strategy or the mismatch\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"emotional-labour\"><h3>Name the strategy or the mismatch</h3><label>Choose a case<select aria-label=\"Choose a case\" data-case=\"\"></select></label><p data-scenario=\"\"></p><div aria-label=\"Choose an answer\" class=\"activity-choices\" data-choices=\"\" role=\"group\"></div><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-feedback=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Teaching cases. Choose an answer, read why it fits, then try another case.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript for this activity. The definitions and worked examples above remain available.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-6\">Affective Events Theory links the workday to outcomes</h2>\n<p><strong>Affective Events Theory</strong> links work conditions to daily hassles and uplifts, then to emotional reactions and work outcomes. Personality and mood influence how a person reacts to an event.</p><p>The same event can produce different reactions. The diagram connects reactions to job satisfaction and performance; it does not establish that every event produces the same result.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:1059.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Affective Events Theory: work conditions shape events, emotional reactions connect events to work outcomes, and personality and mood shape the event response.\" height=\"239pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 1059.00 239.00\" width=\"1059pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 228.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- environment -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node1\">\n<title>environment</title>\n<path d=\"M221.1,-217.4C221.1,-217.4 12,-217.4 12,-217.4 6,-217.4 0,-211.4 0,-205.4 0,-205.4 0,-161 0,-161 0,-155 6,-149 12,-149 12,-149 221.1,-149 221.1,-149 227.1,-149 233.1,-155 233.1,-161 233.1,-161 233.1,-205.4 233.1,-205.4 233.1,-211.4 227.1,-217.4 221.1,-217.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"116.55\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-195.95\">Work environment</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"116.55\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-177.95\">job characteristics · demands</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"116.55\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-159.95\">emotional-labour requirements</text>\n</g>\n<!-- events -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node2\">\n<title>events</title>\n<path d=\"M451.95,-208.4C451.95,-208.4 278.1,-208.4 278.1,-208.4 272.1,-208.4 266.1,-202.4 266.1,-196.4 266.1,-196.4 266.1,-170 266.1,-170 266.1,-164 272.1,-158 278.1,-158 278.1,-158 451.95,-158 451.95,-158 457.95,-158 463.95,-164 463.95,-170 463.95,-170 463.95,-196.4 463.95,-196.4 463.95,-202.4 457.95,-208.4 451.95,-208.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"365.02\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-186.95\">Work events</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"365.02\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-168.95\">daily hassles · daily uplifts</text>\n</g>\n<!-- environment&#45;&gt;events -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-edge1\">\n<title>environment-&gt;events</title>\n<path d=\"M233.57,-183.2C241.62,-183.2 249.72,-183.2 257.72,-183.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"257.66,-185.48 264.16,-183.2 257.66,-180.93 257.66,-185.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- response_link -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node3\">\n<title>response_link</title>\n<ellipse cx=\"611.03\" cy=\"-126.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" rx=\"1.08\" ry=\"1.08\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></ellipse>\n</g>\n<!-- events&#45;&gt;response_link -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-edge2\">\n<title>events-&gt;response_link</title>\n<path d=\"M464.19,-180.7C506.29,-176.12 554.7,-165.93 593.95,-144.2 602.16,-139.65 608.31,-129.28 609.72,-126.76\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n</g>\n<!-- reactions -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node4\">\n<title>reactions</title>\n<path d=\"M790.46,-151.4C790.46,-151.4 657.11,-151.4 657.11,-151.4 651.11,-151.4 645.11,-145.4 645.11,-139.4 645.11,-139.4 645.11,-113 645.11,-113 645.11,-107 651.11,-101 657.11,-101 657.11,-101 790.46,-101 790.46,-101 796.46,-101 802.46,-107 802.46,-113 802.46,-113 802.46,-139.4 802.46,-139.4 802.46,-145.4 796.46,-151.4 790.46,-151.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"723.78\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-129.95\">Emotional reactions</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"723.78\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-111.95\">positive · negative</text>\n</g>\n<!-- response_link&#45;&gt;reactions -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-edge3\">\n<title>response_link-&gt;reactions</title>\n<path d=\"M612.35,-126.2C614.19,-126.2 623.9,-126.2 636.86,-126.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"636.82,-128.48 643.32,-126.2 636.82,-123.93 636.82,-128.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- satisfaction -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node6\">\n<title>satisfaction</title>\n<path d=\"M987.93,-202.2C987.93,-202.2 885.34,-202.2 885.34,-202.2 879.34,-202.2 873.34,-196.2 873.34,-190.2 873.34,-190.2 873.34,-178.2 873.34,-178.2 873.34,-172.2 879.34,-166.2 885.34,-166.2 885.34,-166.2 987.93,-166.2 987.93,-166.2 993.93,-166.2 999.93,-172.2 999.93,-178.2 999.93,-178.2 999.93,-190.2 999.93,-190.2 999.93,-196.2 993.93,-202.2 987.93,-202.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"936.63\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-178.95\">Job satisfaction</text>\n</g>\n<!-- reactions&#45;&gt;satisfaction -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-edge5\">\n<title>reactions-&gt;satisfaction</title>\n<path d=\"M802.7,-147.63C823.19,-153.27 845.17,-159.32 865.25,-164.84\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"864.51,-166.99 871.38,-166.52 865.71,-162.61 864.51,-166.99\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- performance -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node7\">\n<title>performance</title>\n<path d=\"M992.06,-144.2C992.06,-144.2 881.21,-144.2 881.21,-144.2 875.21,-144.2 869.21,-138.2 869.21,-132.2 869.21,-132.2 869.21,-120.2 869.21,-120.2 869.21,-114.2 875.21,-108.2 881.21,-108.2 881.21,-108.2 992.06,-108.2 992.06,-108.2 998.06,-108.2 1004.06,-114.2 1004.06,-120.2 1004.06,-120.2 1004.06,-132.2 1004.06,-132.2 1004.06,-138.2 998.06,-144.2 992.06,-144.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"936.63\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-120.95\">Job performance</text>\n</g>\n<!-- reactions&#45;&gt;performance -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-edge6\">\n<title>reactions-&gt;performance</title>\n<path d=\"M802.7,-126.2C821.69,-126.2 841.96,-126.2 860.8,-126.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"860.77,-128.48 867.27,-126.2 860.77,-123.93 860.77,-128.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- other_outcomes -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node8\">\n<title>other_outcomes</title>\n<path d=\"M1025.81,-86.4C1025.81,-86.4 847.46,-86.4 847.46,-86.4 841.46,-86.4 835.46,-80.4 835.46,-74.4 835.46,-74.4 835.46,-12 835.46,-12 835.46,-6 841.46,0 847.46,0 847.46,0 1025.81,0 1025.81,0 1031.81,0 1037.81,-6 1037.81,-12 1037.81,-12 1037.81,-74.4 1037.81,-74.4 1037.81,-80.4 1031.81,-86.4 1025.81,-86.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"936.63\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-64.95\">Other outcomes</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"936.63\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-46.95\">organizational citizenship</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"936.63\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">commitment · effort</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"936.63\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">intention to quit · deviance</text>\n</g>\n<!-- reactions&#45;&gt;other_outcomes -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-edge7\">\n<title>reactions-&gt;other_outcomes</title>\n<path d=\"M790.06,-100.51C802.1,-95.77 814.96,-90.7 827.87,-85.62\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"828.42,-87.85 833.63,-83.35 826.75,-83.62 828.42,-87.85\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- dispositions -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-node5\">\n<title>dispositions</title>\n<path d=\"M435.45,-136.4C435.45,-136.4 294.6,-136.4 294.6,-136.4 288.6,-136.4 282.6,-130.4 282.6,-124.4 282.6,-124.4 282.6,-98 282.6,-98 282.6,-92 288.6,-86 294.6,-86 294.6,-86 435.45,-86 435.45,-86 441.45,-86 447.45,-92 447.45,-98 447.45,-98 447.45,-124.4 447.45,-124.4 447.45,-130.4 441.45,-136.4 435.45,-136.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"365.02\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-114.95\">Personal dispositions</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"365.02\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-96.95\">personality · mood</text>\n</g>\n<!-- dispositions&#45;&gt;response_link -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-1-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-affective-events-theory-edge4\">\n<title>dispositions-&gt;response_link</title>\n<path d=\"M447.61,-116.22C505.86,-119.8 577.97,-124.23 601.89,-125.7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"601.63,-127.96 608.25,-126.09 601.91,-123.42 601.63,-127.96\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"12.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"536.95\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-128.02\">shape event response</text>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Affective Events Theory: work conditions shape events, emotional reactions connect events to work outcomes, and personality and mood shape the event response.</figcaption></figure>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: trace a missed deadline through AET</summary><p>A job with frequent urgent requests creates a work environment in which missed deadlines and late changes can occur. A manager’s late request becomes a work event. One employee may feel frustration, while another may feel relief if the change removes a difficult task. Their current mood and personal tendencies can shape those reactions.</p><p>The reactions may affect satisfaction, effort, performance, or willingness to help coworkers. AET explains the relationships to consider; it does not claim that every missed deadline produces the same response.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-7\">Emotional intelligence and related models</h2>\n<p>The lecture introduces Howard Gardner’s multiple-intelligences theory as a challenge to judging intellect through a single IQ test alone. The slide lists linguistic, logical-mathematical, musical, spatial, bodily-kinesthetic, intrapersonal, interpersonal, and naturalist intelligences. This is a distinct framework and is not another name for emotional intelligence.</p>\n<p>The slides credit Peter Salovey and John Mayer with coining <strong>emotional intelligence (EI)</strong> in 1990. They describe EI as an ability, skill, or competency rather than a personality trait. In the workplace, EI means being able to recognize, understand, and manage one’s own emotions and the emotions of others.</p><p>The lecture connects it with teamwork, communication, leadership, organizational culture, performance, conflict resolution, satisfaction, and productivity. These are the connections stated in the slides, not guaranteed effects for every person or organization.</p>\n<p>One slide gives three abilities: <strong>perceive</strong> emotions in oneself and others, <strong>understand</strong> what the emotions mean, and <strong>regulate</strong> one’s emotions. Its cascading figure also pairs conscientiousness with perceiving emotions, cognitive ability with understanding their meaning, and emotional stability with regulating emotions. Preserve that as the lecture’s depicted mapping rather than a universal causal rule.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:745.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"The lecture’s cascading EI figure pairs three abilities with corresponding factors and orders the abilities from perceiving to understanding to regulating emotions.\" height=\"162pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 745.00 162.00\" width=\"745pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 151)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- conscientiousness -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-node1\">\n<title>conscientiousness</title>\n<path d=\"M134.85,-133C134.85,-133 12,-133 12,-133 6,-133 0,-127 0,-121 0,-121 0,-109 0,-109 0,-103 6,-97 12,-97 12,-97 134.85,-97 134.85,-97 140.85,-97 146.85,-103 146.85,-109 146.85,-109 146.85,-121 146.85,-121 146.85,-127 140.85,-133 134.85,-133\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"73.42\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-109.75\">Conscientiousness</text>\n</g>\n<!-- perceive -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-node2\">\n<title>perceive</title>\n<path d=\"M311.45,-140.2C311.45,-140.2 190.85,-140.2 190.85,-140.2 184.85,-140.2 178.85,-134.2 178.85,-128.2 178.85,-128.2 178.85,-101.8 178.85,-101.8 178.85,-95.8 184.85,-89.8 190.85,-89.8 190.85,-89.8 311.45,-89.8 311.45,-89.8 317.45,-89.8 323.45,-95.8 323.45,-101.8 323.45,-101.8 323.45,-128.2 323.45,-128.2 323.45,-134.2 317.45,-140.2 311.45,-140.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"251.15\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-118.75\">Perceive emotions</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"251.15\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-100.75\">in self and others</text>\n</g>\n<!-- conscientiousness&#45;&gt;perceive -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-edge1\">\n<title>conscientiousness-&gt;perceive</title>\n<path d=\"M147.17,-115C154.96,-115 162.91,-115 170.77,-115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"170.58,-117.28 177.08,-115 170.58,-112.73 170.58,-117.28\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- understand -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-node4\">\n<title>understand</title>\n<path d=\"M531.55,-108.2C531.55,-108.2 367.45,-108.2 367.45,-108.2 361.45,-108.2 355.45,-102.2 355.45,-96.2 355.45,-96.2 355.45,-69.8 355.45,-69.8 355.45,-63.8 361.45,-57.8 367.45,-57.8 367.45,-57.8 531.55,-57.8 531.55,-57.8 537.55,-57.8 543.55,-63.8 543.55,-69.8 543.55,-69.8 543.55,-96.2 543.55,-96.2 543.55,-102.2 537.55,-108.2 531.55,-108.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"449.5\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-86.75\">Understand the meaning</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"449.5\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-68.75\">of emotions</text>\n</g>\n<!-- perceive&#45;&gt;understand -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-edge4\">\n<title>perceive-&gt;understand</title>\n<path d=\"M323.87,-103.31C331.51,-102.07 339.38,-100.78 347.28,-99.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"347.64,-101.74 353.69,-98.45 346.91,-97.25 347.64,-101.74\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- cognitive -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-node3\">\n<title>cognitive</title>\n<path d=\"M304.7,-68C304.7,-68 197.6,-68 197.6,-68 191.6,-68 185.6,-62 185.6,-56 185.6,-56 185.6,-44 185.6,-44 185.6,-38 191.6,-32 197.6,-32 197.6,-32 304.7,-32 304.7,-32 310.7,-32 316.7,-38 316.7,-44 316.7,-44 316.7,-56 316.7,-56 316.7,-62 310.7,-68 304.7,-68\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"251.15\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-44.75\">Cognitive ability</text>\n</g>\n<!-- cognitive&#45;&gt;understand -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-edge2\">\n<title>cognitive-&gt;understand</title>\n<path d=\"M316.99,-60.9C326.69,-62.53 336.88,-64.24 347.13,-65.96\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"346.68,-68.19 353.47,-67.03 347.43,-63.71 346.68,-68.19\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- regulate -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-node6\">\n<title>regulate</title>\n<path d=\"M711.15,-68C711.15,-68 587.55,-68 587.55,-68 581.55,-68 575.55,-62 575.55,-56 575.55,-56 575.55,-44 575.55,-44 575.55,-38 581.55,-32 587.55,-32 587.55,-32 711.15,-32 711.15,-32 717.15,-32 723.15,-38 723.15,-44 723.15,-44 723.15,-56 723.15,-56 723.15,-62 717.15,-68 711.15,-68\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"649.35\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-44.75\">Regulate emotions</text>\n</g>\n<!-- understand&#45;&gt;regulate -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-edge5\">\n<title>understand-&gt;regulate</title>\n<path d=\"M543.92,-67.42C551.8,-66.1 559.73,-64.78 567.5,-63.49\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"567.68,-65.76 573.72,-62.45 566.93,-61.27 567.68,-65.76\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- stability -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-node5\">\n<title>stability</title>\n<path d=\"M512.42,-36C512.42,-36 386.57,-36 386.57,-36 380.57,-36 374.57,-30 374.57,-24 374.57,-24 374.57,-12 374.57,-12 374.57,-6 380.57,0 386.57,0 386.57,0 512.42,0 512.42,0 518.42,0 524.42,-6 524.42,-12 524.42,-12 524.42,-24 524.42,-24 524.42,-30 518.42,-36 512.42,-36\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"449.5\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-12.75\">Emotional stability</text>\n</g>\n<!-- stability&#45;&gt;regulate -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-figure-2-lecture-4-emotions-and-moods-emotional-intelligence-cascade-edge3\">\n<title>stability-&gt;regulate</title>\n<path d=\"M524.73,-30.01C538.71,-32.27 553.38,-34.64 567.54,-36.93\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"567.07,-39.16 573.85,-37.95 567.79,-34.67 567.07,-39.16\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>The lecture’s cascading EI figure pairs three abilities with corresponding factors and orders the abilities from perceiving to understanding to regulating emotions.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Slide 53 separately presents Goleman’s model in a separate two-by-two arrangement. Recognition versus regulation crosses with self versus social focus. The resulting domains are self-awareness, social awareness, self-management, and relationship management. The matrix gives examples such as emotional self-awareness and accurate self-assessment; empathy and organizational awareness; self-control, adaptability, and initiative; and influence, conflict management, building bonds, and teamwork.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Goleman emotional intelligence domains by focus and function\">\n<caption>Goleman’s four domains shown on the lecture slide</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Focus</th><th scope=\"col\">Recognition</th><th scope=\"col\">Regulation</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Self</th><td><strong>Self-awareness:</strong> emotional self-awareness, accurate self-assessment, self-confidence</td><td><strong>Self-management:</strong> self-control, transparency, adaptability, achievement drive, initiative</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Social</th><td><strong>Social awareness:</strong> empathy, organizational awareness, service orientation</td><td><strong>Relationship management:</strong> inspirational leadership, developing others, influence, change catalyst, conflict management, building bonds, teamwork and collaboration</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>These are two distinct lecture models: the three-part ability sequence and its associated cascade, and Goleman’s self/social recognition/regulation matrix. Gardner’s multiple-intelligences theory supplies context but is distinct from both EI models.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: perceive, interpret, and regulate with uncertainty</summary><p>A usually talkative teammate becomes quiet after a project meeting. First, notice the change without deciding what it means. The teammate might be tired, concerned about a decision, focused on another task, or simply quieter that day. Ask a respectful open question and listen before settling on an interpretation.</p><p>Then choose a response, such as clarifying the task or offering time to talk. The sequence uses perception, understanding, and regulation while recognizing that an observer cannot know another person’s emotion from silence alone.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-8\">Personal and organizational ways to manage emotions</h2>\n<p>The lecture lists personal strategies that can help people notice, process, or respond to emotions. These include mindfulness meditation, cognitive reappraisal, journaling, breathing techniques, physical activity, social support, setting boundaries, self-compassion, problem solving, and seeking professional help when emotion management remains difficult. The slides present them as strategies, not guaranteed outcomes or clinical treatments.</p>\n<p>Organizations can support emotional intelligence through training and workshops, coaching and mentoring, feedback, open communication, mindfulness and stress-management practices, team building, leadership development, diversity and inclusion initiatives, conflict-resolution training, and regular assessments. These are recommendations from the lecture. Their value depends on how they are implemented and on the workplace conditions employees face.</p>\n<p>The lecture closes by describing emotionally and socially intelligent people as better able to understand and express themselves, relate to others, and cope with daily demands. It highlights managing emotions alongside optimism, positivity, and self-motivation as part of that coping.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: choose a response at the individual and team levels</summary><p>An employee feels increasingly tense because urgent requests keep arriving without clear priorities. The employee might write down the main trigger, use a breathing pause before replying, and discuss workload boundaries with a manager. At the team level, the manager could invite open discussion, provide coaching or feedback, and clarify priorities.</p><p>The personal practices may help the employee reflect, while organizational changes address part of the work condition that contributes to the strain.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-9\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating emotion, feeling, mood, and affect as synonyms, or treating affect as a later time stage rather than an umbrella term.</li>\n<li>Putting positive and negative affect on one good-to-bad line. The lecture presents them as separate dimensions.</li>\n<li>Assuming any emotional response makes judgment irrational, or that a negative mood always improves judgment.</li>\n<li>Turning slide-level age, gender, weekly-pattern, or blood-sugar findings into predictions about an individual.</li>\n<li>Confusing felt emotion with displayed emotion, or surface acting with deep acting.</li>\n<li>Treating emotional dissonance as one of the acting strategies rather than the mismatch between felt and projected emotion.</li>\n<li>Flattening AET into “events cause performance” and omitting emotional reactions or the role of personality and mood.</li>\n<li>Equating Gardner’s multiple intelligences with EI, or merging the EI cascade with Goleman’s four-domain matrix.</li>\n<li>Assuming a personal or organizational strategy will work the same way for everyone or under every working condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-10\"></span><h2 id=\"section-11\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">1. A brief anger response follows a customer complaint, then a more general low mood lasts into the evening. Identify the concepts and explain how affect relates to them.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The brief anger directed at the complaint is an emotion. The broader, longer-lasting state is a mood. Affect is the umbrella term that includes both. A feeling may describe the person’s more considered, cognitively integrated experience of the initial emotion.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">2. Can a person show high positive affect and high negative affect at the same time? Explain.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Yes. Positive and negative affect are separate mood dimensions. Someone can feel enthusiastic about a presentation and nervous about the questions that may follow.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">3. A worker smiles at a customer while feeling angry, without trying to change the anger. Identify the display rule, acting strategy, and possible mismatch.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The courteous smile follows a display rule. Hiding anger while changing only the outward display is surface acting. The difference between projected courtesy and felt anger is emotional dissonance.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">4. In AET, what mediates the connection between a work event and work outcomes, and what shapes the employee’s reaction?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The employee’s positive or negative emotional reaction connects the event to outcomes such as satisfaction and performance. Personality and mood shape the event-to-reaction relationship.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">5. A teammate is unusually quiet. How can someone use the EI abilities without assuming they know the teammate’s emotion?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Notice the change, consider several possible explanations, ask a respectful question, listen, and regulate one’s own response before acting. Silence alone does not establish what the teammate feels.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">6. What distinguishes Gardner’s multiple-intelligences framework from EI, and how do the two EI figures differ?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Gardner’s framework lists different forms of intelligence and is not a synonym for EI. The lecture’s EI cascade presents perceiving, understanding, and regulating emotions, with a separate depicted factor mapping. Goleman’s matrix organizes four domains across self/social focus and recognition/regulation.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">7. Name one personal strategy and one organizational strategy from the lecture. Why should neither be presented as a guaranteed fix?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Personal examples include journaling or cognitive reappraisal. Organizational examples include coaching, open communication, or conflict-resolution training. The slides recommend these approaches but do not establish that they produce the same result for every person or workplace.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-3-values-culture-and-person-environment-fit.html\"><small>Previous</small>Values, Culture, and Person-Environment Fit <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-5-managing-emotions-at-work.html\"><small>Next</small>Managing Emotions at Work <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-5-managing-emotions-at-work":{"title":"Managing Emotions at Work · OB and Management","description":"Recognize emotions, ask what support is wanted, and choose a response that fits the person and the task.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Individual behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 5</div><h1>Managing Emotions at Work</h1><p class=\"lead\">Recognize emotions, ask what support is wanted, and choose a response that fits the person and the task.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 7 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">Notice first. Respond with context.</a><a href=\"#section-1\">Why emotions matter at work</a><a href=\"#section-2\">A leader's four practices: Notice, Name, Need, Normalize</a><a href=\"#section-3\">Respond to another person: acknowledge, then choose the timing</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Build a supportive emotional culture</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">Notice first. Respond with context.</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Notice, Name, Need and Normalize are a leader’s reflection practices, not a script to impose on someone.</li><li>Acknowledge emotion without assuming you know its cause or demanding disclosure.</li><li>Support can mean listening, helping with the task or arranging a later conversation.</li></ul>\n<p>This topic connects the previous lesson's emotion and emotional-intelligence foundations to practical leadership. The readings offer three related but distinct contributions: Dina Denham Smith's four practices for emotional awareness and a leader's climate; an HBR interview's context-sensitive guidance for responding to another person; and Sound Community's broader recommendations for workplace culture. They are not one combined research model.</p>\n<p><a href=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods.html\">← Review: Emotions and Moods</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">Why emotions matter at work</h2>\n<p>Denham Smith argues that dismissing emotions as distractions or signs of weakness can encourage suppression. Her article associates prolonged ignoring or suppression with costs to wellbeing, morale, teamwork, and organizational results. It also treats emotions as information about a person's environment and as part of connection, influence, and motivation.</p>\n<p>Sound Community's article describes stress, excitement, frustration, fear, and joy as common at work. It associates positive emotional experiences with creativity, engagement, communication, and collaboration, and unmanaged negative experiences with disengagement, conflict, or lower productivity. This broad framing is a reminder to attend to team climate, not a rule that positive feelings are always useful or negative feelings should disappear.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Workplace emotions, common contexts, and possible effects\">\n<caption>Examples of how workplace emotions may show up</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Emotion</th><th scope=\"col\">Possible context</th><th scope=\"col\">Possible workplace significance</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Stress</th><td>Heavy workload or a tight deadline</td><td>May signal pressure that needs attention; prolonged strain can affect wellbeing and participation.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Excitement</th><td>A new project or promotion</td><td>Can bring energy and create an opportunity for colleagues to share in the good news.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Frustration</th><td>Miscommunication or unmet expectations</td><td>May point to a coordination problem, though the feeling alone does not identify its cause.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Fear</th><td>Concern about failure or judgement</td><td>May inhibit speaking up or trying an idea.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Joy</th><td>A shared achievement</td><td>Recognizing it can strengthen connection and camaraderie.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: frustration as a cue:</strong> A project handoff is missed twice, and two coworkers become short with each other. Frustration may signal an unmet expectation or a process problem, but the manager should ask what happened before deciding that either person is careless. The emotion is a reason to inquire, not proof of a diagnosis or a complete explanation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">A leader's four practices: Notice, Name, Need, Normalize</h2>\n<p>Denham Smith proposes a sequence for developing emotional awareness and making emotion discussable. It begins with the leader's own experience and extends to curiosity about others. The steps are a practical mnemonic, not a substitute for the separate interpersonal response choices described later.</p>\n<h3>Notice: observe cues</h3>\n<p>Emotions can show up in bodily sensations, behaviour, and energy. A leader might notice their own jaw tightening, unusually rapid speech, withdrawal, procrastination, or an unexpected drop in energy. With another person, observable cues include tone, facial expression, body language, mismatch between words and expression, or a change from that person's usual participation.</p>\n<p>These observations do not reveal an exact emotion. They create a reason to pay attention and, if appropriate, ask. A colleague who becomes quiet might be disappointed, concentrating, tired, or simply choosing not to speak.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: notice without inference:</strong> In weekly planning, an analyst who is usually talkative says little after a schedule change. \"I noticed you have been quieter since we changed the deadline\" reports an observation. \"You are angry about the deadline\" turns that observation into an unsupported conclusion.</p>\n<h3>Name: build precision, especially about your own feeling</h3>\n<p>Emotional concepts are learned through social experience and culture. Denham Smith recommends going beyond broad words such as \"mad,\" \"sad,\" and \"glad.\" More specific words, such as frustrated, resentful, provoked, joyful, relieved, or confident, can help a person describe their own experience with greater precision. A careful question can invite someone else to be more specific, but should not impose a label on them.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: name your own feeling:</strong> A manager catches herself thinking \"I am angry\" after a tense budget meeting. On reflection, she identifies disappointment about a decision and anxiety about how to explain it to the team. That distinction helps her choose what to address, without claiming that the same vocabulary will fit every person's experience.</p>\n<h3>Need: ask what the feeling may be signaling</h3>\n<p>After noticing and naming an emotion in oneself, ask what message it might carry. It may point toward something important in one's needs, values, boundaries, relationships, or environment. For another person, the recommended move is gentle, open-ended inquiry rather than guessing what they need.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: inquire before solving:</strong> An employee has stopped contributing to design reviews. The manager says, \"I noticed you have not been joining the discussion as much lately. How have you been feeling about the work?\" If the employee says the new review format feels rushed, the manager has learned something actionable.</p><p>If the employee does not want to discuss it, that boundary should be respected.</p>\n<h3>Normalize: make room without oversharing</h3>\n<p>Denham Smith recommends that leaders model grounded, intentional honesty about both difficult and positive emotions. A brief, relevant acknowledgement can signal that emotion is allowed and can support trust and psychological safety. Normalizing does not mean disclosing everything: the article warns that oversharing can undermine credibility and destabilize a team.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: normalize rather than suppress:</strong> During a demanding release week, a manager says, \"This is a hectic week. I am feeling the pressure too, and we will take it one step at a time.\" The manager names a shared strain while staying focused on the work.</p><p>If instead the manager unloads private distress and expects employees to provide comfort, the disclosure asks the team to manage the leader.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Respond to another person: acknowledge, then choose the timing</h2>\n<p>The HBR interview with Christina Bradley and Lindy Greer adds a separate decision lens: ask whether the work is time-sensitive and whether the person is coping well enough to continue. These questions help choose timing and support; they are not a diagnostic test. The person's preference for privacy also matters.</p>\n<p>Managers may hesitate because of boundaries, discomfort with emotion or concern about mental-health topics. The interviewees also advise engaging with positive emotion, such as a colleague’s excitement.</p><details><summary>Who does the reported “80%” describe?</summary><p>The interviewees say responders default to giving advice or problem-solving about 80% of the time in the research they discuss. This is about the response people give, not a finding that employees prefer receiving advice. They also report about 90% avoidance of emotion in conflict. These are their reported figures, not universal rates.</p></details>\n<table aria-label=\"Response choices based on time pressure and whether the person is coping\">\n<caption>Two context checks for choosing when and how to respond</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Work context</th><th scope=\"col\">If the person is coping</th><th scope=\"col\">If the person is not coping</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Time-sensitive work</th><td>Avoid unnecessary interruption; check in privately when there is a suitable pause.</td><td>Intervene enough to protect the person and the task. Follow up with acknowledgement when there is time.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Time permits</th><td>Acknowledge the situation, ask how they are experiencing it, and offer help without pressure.</td><td>Pause or move the conversation somewhere private; listen, validate, and ask what support would help.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The interview's examples show why context matters. Surgeons described situations where a visibly rattled resident could put a critical task at risk, making immediate intervention necessary. A Navy SEAL described first regulating his own response, waiting when a teammate could cope during a high-pressure moment, and checking in during a lull.</p><p>These examples support the distinction between urgent task protection and later validation; they are not a universal script for every workplace.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Case: a difficult call before a client presentation</summary><p>An employee becomes quiet after a difficult call. Ignoring it assumes they will recover; announcing “You are angry” assumes you know their feelings.</p><p>Check whether they can proceed with the urgent task. Offer immediate help if needed. Otherwise, ask privately afterward: “That call sounded difficult. How are you doing, and would any support be useful?” The employee can correct your impression or decline to discuss it.</p></details>\n<h3>Validate the experience; do not diagnose the emotion</h3>\n<p>The interview cautions that confidently naming another person's precise emotion can backfire: someone described as \"frustrated\" may actually feel confused. Describing a shared situation, \"That meeting felt tense\", or asking how it landed leaves room for the person to explain. In a public meeting, asking someone to account for a subtle emotional cue can expose them.</p><p>When they are coping, a private follow-up is usually less pressuring. If they cannot participate, or safety or task performance is affected, pause and respond promptly.</p>\n<p>Acknowledgement does not require immediate advice. The interviewees describe the common impulse to solve, reframe, or make a person look on the bright side. A more useful order is to recognize what is happening, listen, and then ask whether the person wants help or advice.</p><p>If they do, offer coaching questions, such as what another perspective might reveal or what a trusted mentor might suggest, rather than dictating the \"correct\" interpretation.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: acknowledgement before advice:</strong> A teammate says they feel overlooked after a decision was made without their input. \"You should just be glad the project moved forward\" dismisses the experience. \"It sounds like being left out of that decision mattered to you.</p><p>Would you like to talk through what happened, or would you prefer help planning what to raise next time?\" acknowledges first and lets the teammate choose the next step.</p>\n<p>The interview applies to peers and clients as well as direct reports. In a digital conversation, acknowledge the message and offer an appropriate way to talk. A leader can give brief context for their own stress without making others responsible for managing it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Build a supportive emotional culture</h2>\n<p>Sound Community Services offers broad practitioner recommendations for the everyday conditions around emotional wellbeing: open and thoughtful communication, recognition of achievements, regular check-ins, work-life balance, boundaries, breaks, and emotional-intelligence development. It also mentions personal awareness and brief mindfulness or breathing practices as ways to respond rather than react.</p><p>These suggestions focus on routines and working conditions, not on replacing a one-to-one acknowledgement when someone is distressed.</p>\n<p>The HBR article and interview add that leaders can acknowledge positive emotions as well as difficult ones, anticipate emotionally significant events, and learn from examples of constructive responses. This does not mean a manager must make employees disclose. A supportive culture leaves room for people to speak and for people to keep private matters private.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: culture practice with a boundary:</strong> After a difficult reorganization announcement, a manager schedules a check-in, explains what is known, invites questions, and recognizes the uncertainty people may be experiencing. Employees can share concerns or pass. The check-in creates a channel for communication; it does not promise that everyone will feel positive or that a single meeting resolves the underlying disruption.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating emotion as unprofessional and responding with silence or suppression.</li>\n<li>Assuming that facial expression, tone, or quietness proves a particular emotion or need.</li>\n<li>Equating normalization with forced disclosure or unrestricted leader self-disclosure.</li>\n<li>Calling out a person's subtle emotion publicly when a private conversation would preserve their choice.</li>\n<li>Rushing to advice or positivity before acknowledging what the person has said.</li>\n<li>Applying one response regardless of urgency, task risk, coping, or the person's preference.</li>\n<li>Treating broad culture suggestions as a substitute for addressing workload, conflict, or other workplace conditions.</li>\n<li>Blending the three practitioner readings into one formal model or presenting their recommendations as one empirical study.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-6\"></span><h2 id=\"section-7\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">1. A normally engaged employee becomes quiet in a meeting. What can a manager conclude from this cue?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The manager can conclude that there is a change worth noticing, but not which emotion caused it. A respectful, preferably private question gives the employee room to explain or decline.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">2. Name the four practices in Denham Smith's leadership framework and state what \"Need\" asks the person to do.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Notice, Name, Need, Normalize. \"Need\" asks the person to consider what their own emotion may signal about needs, values, boundaries, relationships, or context. With someone else, ask rather than infer their need.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">3. A person seems upset during a time-critical task but is still performing safely. What response does the interview support?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Avoid unnecessary interruption, then check in privately at a suitable pause. If the person is not coping or safety is at risk, intervene enough to protect the task and person, then acknowledge and follow up when possible.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">4. Why can \"You look frustrated\" be less helpful than \"That meeting felt tense to me\" followed by an open question?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The first assigns a specific internal state that may be wrong. The second describes the situation and invites the other person to explain their own experience.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">5. A colleague shares disappointment about being passed over for a project. What should come before advice?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Acknowledge and listen to the experience, then ask whether the colleague wants to talk, wants advice, or wants another kind of support. Reframing should be offered as coaching, not imposed.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">6. Give one Sound Community culture recommendation and explain why it does not replace the response framework in the HBR interview.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>For example, regular check-ins can support open communication and help surface concerns. They are a broad team routine; a specific response still depends on urgency, whether the person is coping, privacy, and what help they want.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">7. A manager wants to normalize stress during a busy period. What makes the disclosure grounded rather than oversharing?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Keep it brief, relevant to the shared work, and paired with a constructive next step. Do not unload private distress or make employees responsible for reassuring the manager.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-4-emotions-and-moods.html\"><small>Previous</small>Emotions and Moods <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making.html\"><small>Next</small>Perception and Individual Decision Making <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making":{"title":"Perception and Individual Decision Making · OB and Management","description":"See how attention, attribution and decision shortcuts shape the conclusions people reach.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Individual behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 6</div><h1>Perception and Individual Decision Making</h1><p class=\"lead\">See how attention, attribution and decision shortcuts shape the conclusions people reach.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 11 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">An interpretation is not the whole evidence</a><a href=\"#section-1\">Perception shapes behaviour</a><a href=\"#section-2\">From stimuli to action</a><a href=\"#section-3\">Perceptual selectivity: what gets attention</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Social perception and attribution</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Shortcuts when judging other people</a><a href=\"#section-6\">Problem recognition is perceptual</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Three approaches to individual decisions</a><a href=\"#section-8\">Decision biases: mechanism, cue, and a check</a><a href=\"#section-9\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-11\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><p><a href=\"lecture-1-organizational-behaviour-foundations.html\">← Review: Organizational Behaviour Foundations</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"section-0\">An interpretation is not the whole evidence</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Perception selects and interprets information. People can read the same scene differently.</li><li>Attribution needs three comparisons: other people, other situations and other times.</li><li>The rational model optimizes under demanding assumptions. Bounded rationality searches for a satisfactory choice under limits.</li></ul>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:543.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Three distinct inputs that influence perception\" height=\"270pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 543.00 270.00\" width=\"543pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 259.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- situation -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-node1\">\n<title>situation</title>\n<path d=\"M216.6,-248.4C216.6,-248.4 18.75,-248.4 18.75,-248.4 12.75,-248.4 6.75,-242.4 6.75,-236.4 6.75,-236.4 6.75,-210 6.75,-210 6.75,-204 12.75,-198 18.75,-198 18.75,-198 216.6,-198 216.6,-198 222.6,-198 228.6,-204 228.6,-210 228.6,-210 228.6,-236.4 228.6,-236.4 228.6,-242.4 222.6,-248.4 216.6,-248.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-226.95\">Situation</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-208.95\">Time · work and social setting</text>\n</g>\n<!-- perception -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-node4\">\n<title>perception</title>\n<path d=\"M509.45,-167.4C509.45,-167.4 279.35,-167.4 279.35,-167.4 273.35,-167.4 267.35,-161.4 267.35,-155.4 267.35,-155.4 267.35,-129 267.35,-129 267.35,-123 273.35,-117 279.35,-117 279.35,-117 509.45,-117 509.45,-117 515.45,-117 521.45,-123 521.45,-129 521.45,-129 521.45,-155.4 521.45,-155.4 521.45,-161.4 515.45,-167.4 509.45,-167.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"394.4\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-145.95\">Perception</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"394.4\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-127.95\">Meaning given to the environment</text>\n</g>\n<!-- situation&#45;&gt;perception -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-edge1\">\n<title>situation-&gt;perception</title>\n<path d=\"M205.6,-197.57C235.31,-188.81 268.67,-178.98 298.94,-170.05\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"299.53,-172.25 305.12,-168.23 298.24,-167.89 299.53,-172.25\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- target -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-node2\">\n<title>target</title>\n<path d=\"M211.35,-176.4C211.35,-176.4 24,-176.4 24,-176.4 18,-176.4 12,-170.4 12,-164.4 12,-164.4 12,-120 12,-120 12,-114 18,-108 24,-108 24,-108 211.35,-108 211.35,-108 217.35,-108 223.35,-114 223.35,-120 223.35,-120 223.35,-164.4 223.35,-164.4 223.35,-170.4 217.35,-176.4 211.35,-176.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-154.95\">Target</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-136.95\">Size · intensity · background</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-118.95\">Novelty · proximity · motion</text>\n</g>\n<!-- target&#45;&gt;perception -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-edge2\">\n<title>target-&gt;perception</title>\n<path d=\"M223.71,-142.2C235.39,-142.2 247.4,-142.2 259.38,-142.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"259.23,-144.48 265.73,-142.2 259.23,-139.93 259.23,-144.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- perceiver -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-node3\">\n<title>perceiver</title>\n<path d=\"M223.35,-86.4C223.35,-86.4 12,-86.4 12,-86.4 6,-86.4 0,-80.4 0,-74.4 0,-74.4 0,-12 0,-12 0,-6 6,0 12,0 12,0 223.35,0 223.35,0 229.35,0 235.35,-6 235.35,-12 235.35,-12 235.35,-74.4 235.35,-74.4 235.35,-80.4 229.35,-86.4 223.35,-86.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-64.95\">Perceiver</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-46.95\">Attitudes · personality · motives</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Interests · past experiences</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.68\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">Expectations</text>\n</g>\n<!-- perceiver&#45;&gt;perception -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-1-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-influences-edge3\">\n<title>perceiver-&gt;perception</title>\n<path d=\"M235.58,-85.33C262.43,-95 290.35,-105.06 315.1,-113.98\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"314.03,-116.02 320.91,-116.08 315.57,-111.73 314.03,-116.02\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Three distinct inputs that influence perception</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The situation, the target, and the perceiver each influence perception. They are separate inputs, not steps in a chain. A loud voice may stand out because of a target feature; an urgent meeting may make it more noticeable; and a listener’s expectation may shape what the voice means to them.</p>\n<p>Perceiver factors include attitudes, personality, motives, interests, past experiences, and expectations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">Perception shapes behaviour</h2>\n<p>The lecture defines perception as the process by which people organize and interpret sensory impressions to give meaning to their environment. What someone perceives can differ substantially from objective reality. In organizational behaviour, the perceived world matters because people generally respond to what they think is happening, not to an observer’s account of conditions alone.</p>\n<p>The opening visual illustrates this with two people who call the same glass “almost full” or “almost empty,” and a ground marking that appears as a 6 to one viewer and a 9 to another. The shared stimulus is interpreted from different viewpoints; the illustration does not make either observer's reading a fact about the object.</p>\n<p>People may form different impressions of similar jobs and working conditions. The lecture also notes that people often evaluate those they see as similar to themselves more positively, and those they see as different more negatively. Similarity is therefore not neutral evidence of competence or fit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">From stimuli to action</h2>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:599.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Perception process: stimuli are selected, organized and interpreted before action\" height=\"72pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 599.00 72.00\" width=\"599pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 61.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- stimuli -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-node1\">\n<title>stimuli</title>\n<path d=\"M59.1,-50.4C59.1,-50.4 12,-50.4 12,-50.4 6,-50.4 0,-44.4 0,-38.4 0,-38.4 0,-12 0,-12 0,-6 6,0 12,0 12,0 59.1,0 59.1,0 65.1,0 71.1,-6 71.1,-12 71.1,-12 71.1,-38.4 71.1,-38.4 71.1,-44.4 65.1,-50.4 59.1,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"35.55\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Inputs</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"35.55\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">Stimuli</text>\n</g>\n<!-- selection -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-node2\">\n<title>selection</title>\n<path d=\"M174.2,-43.2C174.2,-43.2 115.1,-43.2 115.1,-43.2 109.1,-43.2 103.1,-37.2 103.1,-31.2 103.1,-31.2 103.1,-19.2 103.1,-19.2 103.1,-13.2 109.1,-7.2 115.1,-7.2 115.1,-7.2 174.2,-7.2 174.2,-7.2 180.2,-7.2 186.2,-13.2 186.2,-19.2 186.2,-19.2 186.2,-31.2 186.2,-31.2 186.2,-37.2 180.2,-43.2 174.2,-43.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"144.65\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-19.95\">Selection</text>\n</g>\n<!-- stimuli&#45;&gt;selection -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-edge1\">\n<title>stimuli-&gt;selection</title>\n<path d=\"M71.32,-25.2C78.84,-25.2 86.91,-25.2 94.85,-25.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"94.7,-27.48 101.2,-25.2 94.7,-22.93 94.7,-27.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- organization -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-node3\">\n<title>organization</title>\n<path d=\"M314.8,-43.2C314.8,-43.2 230.2,-43.2 230.2,-43.2 224.2,-43.2 218.2,-37.2 218.2,-31.2 218.2,-31.2 218.2,-19.2 218.2,-19.2 218.2,-13.2 224.2,-7.2 230.2,-7.2 230.2,-7.2 314.8,-7.2 314.8,-7.2 320.8,-7.2 326.8,-13.2 326.8,-19.2 326.8,-19.2 326.8,-31.2 326.8,-31.2 326.8,-37.2 320.8,-43.2 314.8,-43.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"272.5\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-19.95\">Organization</text>\n</g>\n<!-- selection&#45;&gt;organization -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-edge2\">\n<title>selection-&gt;organization</title>\n<path d=\"M186.51,-25.2C194.05,-25.2 202.07,-25.2 210.05,-25.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"210,-27.48 216.5,-25.2 210,-22.93 210,-27.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- interpretation -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-node4\">\n<title>interpretation</title>\n<path d=\"M463.65,-43.2C463.65,-43.2 370.8,-43.2 370.8,-43.2 364.8,-43.2 358.8,-37.2 358.8,-31.2 358.8,-31.2 358.8,-19.2 358.8,-19.2 358.8,-13.2 364.8,-7.2 370.8,-7.2 370.8,-7.2 463.65,-7.2 463.65,-7.2 469.65,-7.2 475.65,-13.2 475.65,-19.2 475.65,-19.2 475.65,-31.2 475.65,-31.2 475.65,-37.2 469.65,-43.2 463.65,-43.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"417.23\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-19.95\">Interpretation</text>\n</g>\n<!-- organization&#45;&gt;interpretation -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-edge3\">\n<title>organization-&gt;interpretation</title>\n<path d=\"M327.05,-25.2C334.76,-25.2 342.77,-25.2 350.68,-25.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"350.54,-27.48 357.04,-25.2 350.54,-22.93 350.54,-27.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- action -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-node5\">\n<title>action</title>\n<path d=\"M565.25,-50.4C565.25,-50.4 519.65,-50.4 519.65,-50.4 513.65,-50.4 507.65,-44.4 507.65,-38.4 507.65,-38.4 507.65,-12 507.65,-12 507.65,-6 513.65,0 519.65,0 519.65,0 565.25,0 565.25,0 571.25,0 577.25,-6 577.25,-12 577.25,-12 577.25,-38.4 577.25,-38.4 577.25,-44.4 571.25,-50.4 565.25,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"542.45\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Output</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"542.45\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">Action</text>\n</g>\n<!-- interpretation&#45;&gt;action -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-figure-2-lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making-process-edge4\">\n<title>interpretation-&gt;action</title>\n<path d=\"M475.87,-25.2C483.78,-25.2 491.78,-25.2 499.34,-25.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"499.34,-27.48 505.84,-25.2 499.34,-22.93 499.34,-27.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Perception process: stimuli are selected, organized and interpreted before action</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The process begins as sensory information arrives and ends in action. The lecture groups selection, organization, and interpretation as the “throughputs” between stimulus input and action output. People receive more information than they can process, so they select some signals and screen out others.</p><p>External features such as intensity, size, movement, and repetition, as well as internal qualities such as age, learning, and interests, can affect selection. They then organize selected information to form meaning.</p>\n<p>The lecture names three ways people organize information:</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Three ways people organize selected perceptual information\">\n<caption>Organization mechanisms</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Mechanism</th><th scope=\"col\">What the perceiver does</th><th scope=\"col\">Lecture illustration</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Grouping</th><td>Groups stimuli by similarity or proximity.</td><td>Workers from the same place may be perceived as similar because they are near one another.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Closure</th><td>Fills gaps in incomplete information to make it meaningful.</td><td>Uses past experience, data, or a hunch to supply what is missing.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Simplification</th><td>Identifies main features and simplifies the interpretation.</td><td>Reduces a complicated stimulus situation to a few salient features.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Closure and simplification help people make sense of complexity, but an interpretation that feels complete may still rest on missing evidence. A hunch can fill a gap; it does not establish that the guess is true.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching example: silence in a meeting.</strong> A manager notices that an employee says little (selection). The manager groups this with the employee’s quiet manner, fills in the missing reason as “not interested” (closure), and reduces several observations to the label “unengaged” (simplification). The employee’s silence is observed; disinterest is inferred.</p><p>Checking the employee’s written contribution and asking about the meeting can change the interpretation before the manager assigns work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Perceptual selectivity: what gets attention</h2>\n<p>Perceptual selectivity is attending to particular stimuli according to their perceived relevance, importance, and personal interests. External properties can make a signal stand out; personal influences help explain why people in the same setting notice different things.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"External and personal influences on perceptual selectivity\">\n<caption>Influences that direct selective attention</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Influence group</th><th scope=\"col\">Factors</th><th scope=\"col\">How they can shape attention</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">External stimulus properties</th><td>Size, intensity, repetition, novelty or familiarity, contrast, motion</td><td>Large, loud, repeated, new or familiar, contrasting, and moving stimuli can draw attention.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Personal influences</th><td>Self-concept, beliefs, expectations, inner needs, response disposition, response salience, perceptual defence</td><td>What a person believes, expects, needs, recognizes, or is disposed to notice can make one interpretation more prominent.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>External properties include size, intensity, repetition, novelty or familiarity, contrast, and motion. The slide illustrates how loud sounds and bright lights stand out, repeated messages attract attention, and movement can be more noticeable than stillness. Novelty and familiarity both matter: unusual stimuli attract attention, and familiar stimuli may be readily recognized.</p>\n<p>Self-concept, beliefs, expectations and needs influence attention. A manager who expects non-technical staff to lack product knowledge may overlook what their answers actually show.</p><p>Familiar stimuli are easier to perceive through <strong>response disposition</strong>. <strong>Response salience</strong> reflects cognitive predispositions: marketing, accounting and personnel staff may notice different sides of the same business problem.</p>\n<p>Perceptual defence is screening information that creates conflict or feels threatening. It can involve denying the information, distorting it to fit an older view, or accepting it only as an unrepresentative exception. Recognizing the pattern is a reason to look again at the evidence that challenges a current interpretation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Social perception and attribution</h2>\n<p>Social perception is gathering and interpreting information about people through appearance, verbal communication, and nonverbal signals. These cues help people judge characteristics, roles, and relationships, but they do not directly reveal another person's motives.</p>\n<p>Attribution theory describes how observers decide whether behaviour was caused internally or externally. An internal cause is believed to be under the person's control; an external cause is something the situation is thought to have forced. The lecture's lateness example contrasts oversleeping after partying (internal) with a traffic accident blocking the route (external).</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Attribution theory: three comparison questions and the lecture's high and low cues\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<caption>Ask what is being compared before interpreting the cue</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Cue</th><th scope=\"col\">Question</th><th scope=\"col\">High cue</th><th scope=\"col\">Low cue</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Distinctiveness</th><td data-label=\"Question\">Does this person behave differently in other situations?</td><td data-label=\"High cue\">Behaviour differs from this person’s behaviour in other situations; points toward an external attribution.</td><td data-label=\"Low cue\">Similar behaviour across situations; points toward an internal attribution.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Consensus</th><td data-label=\"Question\">Do other people respond similarly to the same situation?</td><td data-label=\"High cue\">Others respond the same way; points toward an external attribution.</td><td data-label=\"Low cue\">Others in the same situation do not; points toward an internal attribution.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Consistency</th><td data-label=\"Question\">Does this person respond this way over time?</td><td data-label=\"High cue\">Repeated behaviour; the lecture points toward an internal attribution.</td><td data-label=\"Low cue\">Not repeated over time; points toward an external attribution.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Keep the comparisons distinct: “other situations” is distinctiveness, “other people” is consensus, and “other times” is consistency. The source gives directional cues, not a deterministic test. Consider the combined pattern and investigate context before deciding what caused a behaviour.</p>\n<section aria-label=\"Teaching example: compare two lateness cases using the three attribution cues\" class=\"worked-calculation\">\n<h3>Teaching example: compare two lateness cases</h3>\n<div class=\"example-input\"><p>Two employees arrive late today. Case A is normally punctual and is punctual for other kinds of commitments, and several coworkers using the same route are also late after a traffic accident. Case B is late several times a week, is late to other commitments too, and coworkers on the same route arrive on time.</p><p>Use the lecture's three questions; do not treat the result as proof of cause.</p></div>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>1. Distinctiveness: compare other situations</h4><p>Case A: lateness is unusual for this person, so distinctiveness is high and the cue points outward to the situation. Case B: lateness occurs across settings, so distinctiveness is low and the cue points inward to the person.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>2. Consensus: compare other people</h4><p>Case A: coworkers using the route are late too, so consensus is high and the cue points outward. Case B: other commuters arrive on time, so consensus is low and the cue points inward.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>3. Consistency: compare other times</h4><p>Case A: this is not a repeated pattern, so consistency is low and the cue points outward. Case B: lateness repeats several times a week, so consistency is high and the lecture's cue points inward.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>4. Combine the cues and check</h4><p>Case A's high distinctiveness, high consensus, and low consistency point toward a temporary external explanation such as the accident. Case B's low distinctiveness, low consensus, and high consistency point toward an internal explanation. These are provisional attributions: ask what happened and check the relevant circumstances before acting.</p></section>\n</section>\n<section aria-label=\"Change the attribution evidence\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"attribution\"><h3>Change the attribution evidence</h3><label>Consensus: do other people respond similarly?<select data-input=\"consensus\"><option value=\"unknown\">Not observed</option><option value=\"high\">High</option><option value=\"low\">Low</option></select></label><label>Distinctiveness: does this person act differently elsewhere?<select data-input=\"distinctiveness\"><option value=\"unknown\">Not observed</option><option value=\"high\">High</option><option value=\"low\">Low</option></select></label><label>Consistency: does this recur over time?<select data-input=\"consistency\"><option value=\"unknown\">Not observed</option><option value=\"high\">High</option><option value=\"low\">Low</option></select></label><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-result=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Patterns suggest explanations to investigate. An unknown comparison stays unknown; a cue is not proof of cause.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to change the inputs. The course model is explained above.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-5\">Shortcuts when judging other people</h2>\n<p>Shortcuts reduce the effort of interpreting people, but they can replace individual evidence with an impression or reference point. Keep their mechanisms separate.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Common social perception shortcuts and their distinct recognition cues\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<caption>Recognize the shortcut by the source of the judgment</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Shortcut</th><th scope=\"col\">Recognition cue</th><th scope=\"col\">Workplace example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Selective perception</th><td data-label=\"Recognition cue\">Some information is attended to while other information is screened out.</td><td data-label=\"Workplace example\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> a manager notices only behaviour that fits an existing expectation.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Halo effect</th><td data-label=\"Recognition cue\">An overall impression spills over into judgments of separate attributes.</td><td data-label=\"Workplace example\"><strong>Lecture example:</strong> someone seen as attractive or likeable is also rated more favourably on skills or characteristics, regardless of actual performance.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Contrast effect</th><td data-label=\"Recognition cue\">A comparison point makes a person's qualities seem more different than they are.</td><td data-label=\"Workplace example\"><strong>Lecture example:</strong> a candidate is seen as better than they are after less suitable applicants, or worse after top-tier applicants.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Social identity effect</th><td data-label=\"Recognition cue\">Group identification shapes self-definition and attitudes toward in-group and out-group members.</td><td data-label=\"Workplace example\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> a team member feels belonging and self-esteem through group membership while becoming less favourable toward an outside group; this is a possible influence, not a prediction about any member.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Stereotyping</th><td data-label=\"Recognition cue\">A generalized group belief is applied to an individual and overlooks personal differences.</td><td data-label=\"Workplace example\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> a hiring panel assumes each applicant from a professional category has the same traits instead of checking individual evidence.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Halo is impression spillover; contrast is a reference-point effect; stereotyping applies a group expectation. Social identity concerns how group membership informs a person's self-definition and intergroup attitudes. These concepts can overlap in practice, but they are not interchangeable explanations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-6\">Problem recognition is perceptual</h2>\n<p>Decision-making is a response to a problem: a discrepancy between the current state and a desired state that calls for considering alternative courses of action. Whether a discrepancy counts as a problem is itself a perceptual issue. One manager may see a division's 2% quarterly sales decline as urgent; another manager with the same decline may consider it acceptable.</p><p>The observed change alone does not settle what response is warranted.</p>\n<p>The slides present an organizational schema with data or transaction processing at the base, then operational, tactical, and strategic decision levels. A second figure associates decision structure with management groups and information needs:</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Lecture schema connecting decision structure, management groups, and information requirements\">\n<caption>Decision-level schema from the lecture</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Decision structure</th><th scope=\"col\">Group in the slide</th><th scope=\"col\">Information requirements listed in the slide</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Unstructured</th><td>Senior management</td><td>General, broad scope, interactive, external, internal, real-time, ad hoc.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Semi-structured</th><td>Middle management</td><td>Focused, specified, interactive, internal, real-time, scheduled.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Structured</th><td>Operational management; individual employees and teams</td><td>Specified, scheduled, narrow, interactive, real-time, internal, detailed.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>This is the lecture's organizational schema, not a universal rule for every organization.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-7\">Three approaches to individual decisions</h2>\n<h3>Rational decision-making: an ideal sequence</h3>\n<p>The rational decision-making model assumes the decision-maker has complete information, can identify all relevant options without bias, and chooses the option with the highest utility. Its six steps give a useful order for analysis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the problem.</li><li>Clarify the decision criteria.</li><li>Identify alternatives.</li><li>Calculate risks and benefits.</li><li>Evaluate alternatives.</li><li>Select the best option.</li>\n</ol>\n<section aria-label=\"Teaching example: apply the rational decision sequence to choosing a delivery supplier\" class=\"worked-calculation\">\n<h3>Teaching example: choose a delivery supplier</h3>\n<div class=\"example-input\"><p>A team needs a supplier whose delivery arrives before a planned launch. It cares about delivery reliability, timing, and total cost. Three suppliers are available. For this teaching example, assume the team can obtain complete, comparable information and evaluate every relevant option fairly; those assumptions are part of the rational model, not facts guaranteed in real decisions.</p></div>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>1. Identify the problem</h4><p>The current supplier cannot reliably deliver before launch, while the desired state is dependable delivery in time. State the gap before choosing an alternative.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>2. Clarify the decision criteria</h4><p>Use the team's stated criteria: reliability, delivery timing, and total cost. Keep them visible so the most striking feature of one offer does not silently replace them.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>3. Identify alternatives</h4><p>List all relevant available suppliers, including the current arrangement if it remains a genuine option. The ideal model assumes the decision-maker can identify all relevant choices.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>4. Calculate risks and benefits</h4><p>For each supplier, gather comparable evidence about delivery reliability, timing, cost, and relevant risks. The example supplies no numbers, so none should be invented.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>5. Evaluate alternatives</h4><p>Compare every option against the criteria using the same evidence standard. Consider the trade-offs together rather than letting one attractive feature determine the judgment.</p></section>\n<section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>6. Select the best option and check</h4><p>Select the alternative with the highest utility under the criteria and available evidence. Check that the selected option meets the stated launch need and that relevant alternatives were considered. If the information is incomplete, the ideal model's assumptions are not met.</p></section>\n</section>\n<p>In practice, the lecture says most decisions do not follow this complete optimization sequence. People often look for an acceptable or reasonable solution, and important decisions frequently depend on judgment rather than a defined prescriptive model.</p>\n<h3>Bounded rationality: a satisfactory choice under limits</h3>\n<p>Bounded rationality recognizes limits in knowledge, cognitive capacity, time, and resources. People simplify a complicated problem to a level they can manage, then satisfice: they search for something satisfactory and sufficient rather than proving that they found the best possible option. Satisficing is an adaptation to limits, not necessarily carelessness; it also means the chosen option has not been shown to be optimal.</p>\n<p>The lecture's comparison distinguishes limited from unbounded assumptions:</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Lecture comparison of bounded rationality and rational choice\">\n<caption>Bounded rationality and rational choice as presented in the lecture</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Dimension</th><th scope=\"col\">Bounded rationality</th><th scope=\"col\">Rational choice</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Decision-maker capacity</th><td>Bounded mental capacity; may need assistance.</td><td>Unbounded cognitive ability.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Available actions</th><td>Knowledge of an acceptable set.</td><td>Knowledge of all available actions.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequences</th><td>Approximate and heterogeneous knowledge.</td><td>Numerical knowledge of all consequences.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Preferences</th><td>Evolutionary and unsettled.</td><td>Stable and ordered.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Time and cost</th><td>Temporary and cost limits affect decision quality.</td><td>Resources are unbounded or do not affect the process.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Decision aim</th><td>Search for a satisfactory result; help the decision-maker understand what may happen.</td><td>Search for the best possible result; inform the decision-maker what to do.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Intuition: fast pattern recognition from experience</h3>\n<p>The lecture describes intuition as nonconscious decision-making formed through concentrated experience. It works outside conscious thought, links disparate pieces of information holistically, is fast, and is affectively charged. It is not necessarily wrong, does not always conflict with analysis, and is not presented as superstition or a magical sense. It is complex and based on years of experience and learning.</p>\n<p>The lecture's practical limit is clear: use an experienced hunch to suggest a hypothesis, not as proof. Test it with objective data and careful analysis. Intuition and analysis can complement each other.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-8\">Decision biases: mechanism, cue, and a check</h2>\n<p>Each bias has a different signature. The checks below are <strong>supplemental practice suggestions</strong> to help apply the lecture's distinctions. The slides do not establish each check as a tested remedy. The clearest source-based guidance is to test hunches with objective evidence and analysis; the lecture also says a felt need for accuracy is associated with less confirmation bias.</p>\n<table aria-label=\"Decision biases with distinct recognition cues and qualified checking suggestions\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<caption>Distinguish the mechanism before choosing a check</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Bias</th><th scope=\"col\">What is happening</th><th scope=\"col\">Recognition cue or lecture case</th><th scope=\"col\">Supplemental check</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Overconfidence</th><td data-label=\"What is happening\">Confidence about one's own or others' ability exceeds what is warranted, often without awareness.</td><td data-label=\"Recognition cue or lecture case\">A person is sure the plan will work and does not prepare for problems. The lecture reports that people whose intellectual and interpersonal abilities are weakest are most likely to overestimate their own performance and ability, and that entrepreneur optimism is negatively related to new-venture performance; these are slide-reported claims, not rules about an individual.</td><td data-label=\"Supplemental check\">State what evidence supports confidence, identify uncertainties, and prepare a contingency plan. Do not infer incompetence from confidence alone.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Anchoring</th><td data-label=\"What is happening\">Initial information gets disproportionate weight and later information does not shift the judgment enough.</td><td data-label=\"Recognition cue or lecture case\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> in a negotiation, the first offer continues to dominate after relevant later evidence is available. The lecture notes that anchors are widely used where persuasion matters and that negotiations involve anchoring.</td><td data-label=\"Supplemental check\">Make an independent estimate before seeing the first proposal, then compare it with later evidence. Treat the estimate as a prompt to review, not a guaranteed correction.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Confirmation</th><td data-label=\"What is happening\">People seek or accept support for past choices or views and discount contradictory information.</td><td data-label=\"Recognition cue or lecture case\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> a decision-maker with strong opinions and confidence in their information chooses sources that echo their view.</td><td data-label=\"Supplemental check\">Ask what evidence would change the conclusion and actively seek it. The lecture notes that feeling a need for decision accuracy is associated with less confirmation bias; it does not make bias impossible.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Availability</th><td data-label=\"What is happening\">Judgment leans on information that is easy to recall, especially vivid, emotional, recent, or tied to direct experience.</td><td data-label=\"Recognition cue or lecture case\">After a vivid story, someone overestimates an unlikely event such as an aeroplane crash, medical complication, or job loss.</td><td data-label=\"Supplemental check\">Compare the memorable case with relevant base-rate and longer-term records. A recalled example is not itself a frequency estimate.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Escalation of commitment</th><td data-label=\"What is happening\">A decision is continued despite clear evidence it is wrong, especially when the decision-maker feels personally responsible.</td><td data-label=\"Recognition cue or lecture case\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> a manager keeps supporting a failing choice and searches for evidence that justifies the original commitment, fearing personal failure.</td><td data-label=\"Supplemental check\">Review current evidence and future costs with an independent person; ask what would justify stopping or changing course. Continuation alone is not proof of escalation if new evidence supports it.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Loss aversion</th><td data-label=\"What is happening\">Equivalent losses feel more painful than gains feel pleasurable.</td><td data-label=\"Recognition cue or lecture case\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> a possible loss dominates attention even when an equivalent gain is available. The slide says losses are typically about twice as psychologically powerful; treat that as its heuristic, not a universal coefficient.</td><td data-label=\"Supplemental check\">Describe outcomes in both gain and loss terms and compare equivalent consequences. This can reveal framing; it does not eliminate individual differences.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Hindsight</th><td data-label=\"What is happening\">After learning the outcome, a person falsely believes they could have predicted it accurately.</td><td data-label=\"Recognition cue or lecture case\"><strong>Teaching example:</strong> after a project fails, someone says “it was obvious” though the prediction was not recorded beforehand.</td><td data-label=\"Supplemental check\">Record expectations and reasons before outcomes are known, then compare them with what happened. This supports a fairer review but cannot recover every prior belief.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Overconfidence is not simply being confident; the cue is confidence that outruns evidence, often with limited planning for problems. Anchoring concerns the weight of early information. Confirmation concerns how supporting and conflicting evidence is searched for or evaluated. Availability concerns ease of recall. Escalation concerns continuing despite clear contrary evidence and may be reinforced by responsibility.</p><p>Loss aversion concerns unequal emotional weight for equivalent losses and gains. Hindsight appears after the outcome is known.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-9\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating perception as objective reality, or assuming observed conditions alone explain behaviour.</li>\n<li>Mixing up the situation, target, and perceiver as if they were stages or interchangeable factors.</li>\n<li>Confusing selection (what receives attention) with organization (how selected material is grouped, completed, or simplified).</li>\n<li>Treating closure or a hunch as proof rather than as an interpretation that needs checking.</li>\n<li>Mixing up distinctiveness (other situations), consensus (other people), and consistency (other times), or treating one cue as decisive.</li>\n<li>Calling halo, contrast, social identity, stereotyping, and selective perception the same shortcut.</li>\n<li>Assuming a 2% sales decline automatically is or is not a problem without considering a desired state.</li>\n<li>Describing the rational model as what people usually do; it depends on demanding assumptions.</li>\n<li>Calling satisficing careless, or claiming a satisfactory choice has been proven optimal.</li>\n<li>Treating intuition as either magic or necessarily wrong; it can suggest a hypothesis that evidence should test.</li>\n<li>Confusing confirmation (evidence sought or discounted) with availability (evidence easy to recall).</li>\n<li>Calling every persistent project effort escalation, every confident person incompetent, or the slide's “twice” loss comparison a universal rule.</li>\n<li>Claiming an outcome was predictable after it is known without checking what was recorded beforehand.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-10\"></span><h2 id=\"section-11\">Practice questions</h2>\n<p class=\"question\">1. Two employees observe the same busy customer-service desk. One notices the queue, another notices a colleague struggling with the software. Name three broad influences that could help explain their different perceptions.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The situation (such as time pressure), target features (what stands out in the scene), and perceiver characteristics (such as interests, expectations, or experience) can each influence perception. They are separate inputs, so more detail is needed to identify which mattered most.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">2. A supervisor observes an employee arrive late once, but the employee is usually punctual, is punctual for other kinds of commitments, and many people were delayed by the same road accident. Apply distinctiveness, consensus, and consistency.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Distinctiveness is high because this is unusual for the employee across situations. Consensus is high because others faced the same delay. Consistency is low because it is not a repeated pattern. Together these cues point toward an external, temporary explanation, but the supervisor should still check what happened.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">3. An interviewer rates a candidate immediately after a very weak candidate and gives extra credit for being personable. Which shortcuts may be operating?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The judgment against the previous applicant suggests contrast effect. Letting likeability shape judgments of separate qualifications suggests halo effect. Check each candidate against the same criteria and evidence.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">4. Why can two managers disagree about whether the same 2% sales decline is a problem?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>They may compare the observed result with different desired states or reference points. Recognizing a discrepancy that requires action is perceptual; the percentage alone does not settle the decision.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">5. A team chooses the first supplier that meets its time and cost limits because there is not time to examine every option. Which decision approach is this, and what should the team avoid claiming?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>This illustrates bounded rationality and satisficing: the team selects a satisfactory option within practical limits. It should not claim that the option is proven to be the best possible choice because it did not evaluate every alternative.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">6. A manager searches mainly for reports supporting a system they already selected. After a vivid one-off failure, they also conclude the failure is now likely everywhere. Distinguish the biases.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The support-seeking and discounting of contradictory reports is confirmation bias. Treating an easily recalled vivid event as evidence of broad likelihood is availability bias. One concerns information search and evaluation; the other concerns ease of recall.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">7. A project lead continues a project despite clear evidence it is failing and says after cancellation that the outcome was always obvious. Identify the two possible biases and an evidence check.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Continuing despite clear contrary evidence can be escalation of commitment, especially if the lead feels responsible. Claiming the failure was predictable only after it happened suggests hindsight bias. Review earlier forecasts, decision records, and the evidence available at each point; continuation alone does not prove escalation if new benefits justified it.</p></details>\n<p class=\"question\">8. What role should intuition play in an important decision?</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>An experience-based hunch can suggest a hypothesis. It is not proof: test it with objective data and analysis. Intuition can complement rational analysis rather than replacing or always contradicting it.</p></details><div class=\"section-finish\"><h2>Keep the essentials</h2><p><a href=\"individual-revision.html\">Short note: individuals at work</a>. A compact recap and three recall questions for this part of the course.</p></div></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-5-managing-emotions-at-work.html\"><small>Previous</small>Managing Emotions at Work <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"individual-revision.html\"><small>Next</small>Short note: individuals at work <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"individual-revision":{"title":"Short note: individuals at work · OB and Management","description":"Read the core distinctions, then test your recall. Follow a topic link for the full explanation and sources.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Individual behaviour <span>/</span> Revision</div><h1>Short note: individuals at work</h1><p class=\"lead\">Read the core distinctions, then test your recall. Follow a topic link for the full explanation and sources.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 5 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#essentials\">The distinctions to remember</a><a href=\"#responses\">Dissatisfaction and individual differences</a><a href=\"#emotion\">Emotions at work</a><a href=\"#judgment\">Before assigning a cause or choosing an option</a><a href=\"#recall\">Try without looking</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"essentials\">The distinctions to remember</h2>\n<p>OB explains workplace behaviour through individuals, groups and organizational systems. Ask which conditions make an explanation fit. A visible rule and an informal norm can pull people in different directions.</p>\n<table class=\"comparison\" data-compact=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Question it answers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Belief</th><td>What do I think is true?</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Value</th><td>What should matter?</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Attitude</th><td>How do I evaluate this target?</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Intention / action</th><td>What do I plan to do / what did I do?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Job satisfaction evaluates the job. Job involvement concerns identification with the work; organizational commitment concerns attachment to the organization. Avoid treating them as synonyms.</p>\n<h2 id=\"responses\">Dissatisfaction and individual differences</h2>\n<p>EVLN uses two axes. Active + constructive is <strong>voice</strong>; active + destructive is <strong>exit</strong>; passive + constructive is <strong>loyalty</strong>; passive + destructive is <strong>neglect</strong>.</p>\n<p>Personality describes relatively stable patterns. MBTI uses preference categories; the Big Five uses dimensions. Strong situations constrain conduct; trait activation concerns cues that make a particular trait relevant.</p>\n<p>Person–job fit compares the person with job requirements. Person–organization fit compares the person with organizational culture. National-culture dimensions describe aggregate patterns, not a colleague’s personality.</p>\n<h2 id=\"emotion\">Emotions at work</h2>\n<p>An emotion usually has a target; a mood is more diffuse. Surface acting changes the display; deep acting tries to change the feeling. Emotional dissonance is the mismatch between felt and displayed emotion.</p>\n<p>AET connects work events, emotional reactions and outcomes, with personality and mood affecting reactions. When helping someone, notice and acknowledge without assuming the cause. Ask what support is wanted and consider timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"judgment\">Before assigning a cause or choosing an option</h2>\n<ul><li>Consensus compares <strong>other people</strong>.</li><li>Distinctiveness compares <strong>other situations</strong>.</li><li>Consistency compares <strong>other times</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Check all three cues. Missing evidence stays unknown. An attribution is an explanation to investigate, not proof.</p>\n<p>The rational model seeks the best alternative under demanding information assumptions. Bounded rationality works within limits and satisfices. An experienced intuition can suggest a hypothesis; test it against evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"recall\">Try without looking</h2>\n<details><summary>“I plan to complain.” Has an action occurred?</summary><p>No. This is a stated behavioural intention. Observe or ask what happened before describing an action.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Someone is usually punctual. Which attribution cue is supported?</summary><p>Consistency over time. Distinctiveness still needs evidence from other situations.</p></details>\n<details><summary>A smiling employee still feels irritated. Name the strategy and the gap.</summary><p>The outward display suggests surface acting. The felt/displayed mismatch is emotional dissonance.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-6-perception-and-individual-decision-making.html\"><small>Previous</small>Perception and Individual Decision Making <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour.html\"><small>Next</small>Group Structure and Behaviour <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer class=\"footer\">MN4063 · Organizational Behavior and Management</footer>"},"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour":{"title":"Group Structure and Behaviour · OB and Management","description":"Connect roles, norms, status and cohesion to effort and performance.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Group behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 7A</div><h1>Group Structure and Behaviour</h1><p class=\"lead\">Connect roles, norms, status and cohesion to effort and performance.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 10 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">Shared expectations shape group behaviour</a><a href=\"#section-1\">What counts as a group?</a><a href=\"#section-2\">How groups develop</a><a href=\"#section-3\">Roles, norms, and sanctions</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Deviant workplace behaviour</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Status, voice, and group size</a><a href=\"#section-6\">Effort, cohesion, and performance</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Diversity and faultlines</a><a href=\"#section-8\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-10\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">Shared expectations shape group behaviour</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A role concerns expected conduct; a norm is a shared standard; status is a socially assigned rank.</li><li>Make contributions identifiable to reduce social loafing.</li><li>Cohesion strengthens shared performance norms. A close group can reinforce low output.</li></ul>\n<p>Two development models help explain different patterns. Tuckman’s stages describe a possible path for groups working together; punctuated equilibrium describes temporary groups working toward a deadline. The remaining ideas explain how group expectations and relationships affect everyday behaviour and results.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">What counts as a group?</h2>\n<p>A group is two or more people who interact, depend on one another, and come together to achieve particular objectives. Groups may be <strong>formal</strong> or <strong>informal</strong>. A formal group is defined by the organization: assigned work, established tasks, and behaviour directed toward organizational goals. An informal group is not created by the organization’s formal structure.</p><p>It develops through social contact, yet its interactions can still affect work behaviour and performance.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a formal project group and an informal lunch group</summary><p>A manager assigns employees from finance, operations, and sales to deliver a forecast. The assigned task and responsibilities make this a formal group. Those employees also meet for lunch because they enjoy one another’s company. That social group is informal. The informal connection may help them communicate at work, but its existence does not replace the formal group’s task or authority.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">How groups develop</h2>\n<h3>Tuckman’s five stages</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s sequence is <strong>Forming → Storming → Norming → Performing → Adjourning</strong>.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:690.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Tuckman’s five-stage sequence. The stages describe a useful pattern, not a guaranteed or irreversible path.\" height=\"58pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 690.00 58.00\" width=\"690pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 46.8)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- forming -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-node1\">\n<title>forming</title>\n<path d=\"M86.1,-36C86.1,-36 12,-36 12,-36 6,-36 0,-30 0,-24 0,-24 0,-12 0,-12 0,-6 6,0 12,0 12,0 86.1,0 86.1,0 92.1,0 98.1,-6 98.1,-12 98.1,-12 98.1,-24 98.1,-24 98.1,-30 92.1,-36 86.1,-36\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"49.05\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-12.75\">1 · Forming</text>\n</g>\n<!-- storming -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-node2\">\n<title>storming</title>\n<path d=\"M222.2,-36C222.2,-36 142.1,-36 142.1,-36 136.1,-36 130.1,-30 130.1,-24 130.1,-24 130.1,-12 130.1,-12 130.1,-6 136.1,0 142.1,0 142.1,0 222.2,0 222.2,0 228.2,0 234.2,-6 234.2,-12 234.2,-12 234.2,-24 234.2,-24 234.2,-30 228.2,-36 222.2,-36\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"182.15\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-12.75\">2 · Storming</text>\n</g>\n<!-- forming&#45;&gt;storming -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-edge1\">\n<title>forming-&gt;storming</title>\n<path d=\"M98.49,-18C106.15,-18 114.15,-18 122.02,-18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"121.81,-20.28 128.31,-18 121.81,-15.73 121.81,-20.28\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- norming -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-node3\">\n<title>norming</title>\n<path d=\"M356.05,-36C356.05,-36 278.2,-36 278.2,-36 272.2,-36 266.2,-30 266.2,-24 266.2,-24 266.2,-12 266.2,-12 266.2,-6 272.2,0 278.2,0 278.2,0 356.05,0 356.05,0 362.05,0 368.05,-6 368.05,-12 368.05,-12 368.05,-24 368.05,-24 368.05,-30 362.05,-36 356.05,-36\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"317.12\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-12.75\">3 · Norming</text>\n</g>\n<!-- storming&#45;&gt;norming -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-edge2\">\n<title>storming-&gt;norming</title>\n<path d=\"M234.56,-18C242.24,-18 250.19,-18 257.99,-18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"257.71,-20.28 264.21,-18 257.71,-15.73 257.71,-20.28\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- performing -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-node4\">\n<title>performing</title>\n<path d=\"M506.4,-36C506.4,-36 412.05,-36 412.05,-36 406.05,-36 400.05,-30 400.05,-24 400.05,-24 400.05,-12 400.05,-12 400.05,-6 406.05,0 412.05,0 412.05,0 506.4,0 506.4,0 512.4,0 518.4,-6 518.4,-12 518.4,-12 518.4,-24 518.4,-24 518.4,-30 512.4,-36 506.4,-36\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"459.23\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-12.75\">4 · Performing</text>\n</g>\n<!-- norming&#45;&gt;performing -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-edge3\">\n<title>norming-&gt;performing</title>\n<path d=\"M368.31,-18C375.96,-18 383.96,-18 391.9,-18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"391.79,-20.28 398.29,-18 391.79,-15.73 391.79,-20.28\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- adjourning -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-node5\">\n<title>adjourning</title>\n<path d=\"M656,-36C656,-36 562.4,-36 562.4,-36 556.4,-36 550.4,-30 550.4,-24 550.4,-24 550.4,-12 550.4,-12 550.4,-6 556.4,0 562.4,0 562.4,0 656,0 656,0 662,0 668,-6 668,-12 668,-12 668,-24 668,-24 668,-30 662,-36 656,-36\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"609.2\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-12.75\">5 · Adjourning</text>\n</g>\n<!-- performing&#45;&gt;adjourning -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-1-lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-tuckman-five-stages-edge4\">\n<title>performing-&gt;adjourning</title>\n<path d=\"M518.71,-18C526.46,-18 534.46,-18 542.33,-18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"542.13,-20.28 548.63,-18 542.13,-15.73 542.13,-20.28\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Tuckman’s five-stage sequence. The stages describe a useful pattern, not a guaranteed or irreversible path.</figcaption></figure>\n<ol class=\"development-stages\"><li><strong>Forming:</strong> clarify purpose and tasks; members may rely on a leader.</li><li><strong>Storming:</strong> differences about direction and power become visible.</li><li><strong>Norming:</strong> work through conflict and establish expectations, roles and trust.</li><li><strong>Performing:</strong> coordinate work and use members’ strengths.</li><li><strong>Adjourning:</strong> recognize contributions, reflect and move on.</li></ol>\n<p>The lecture slide labels Adjourning “Stage 4,” even though the same deck labels Performing “Stage 4.” Here it is correctly presented as <strong>Stage 5</strong>: the five-stage sequence added adjourning after the original four stages in Tuckman and Jensen’s review. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1177/105960117700200404\">Primary reference: Tuckman &amp; Jensen (1977)</a>.</p>\n<p>The model is a guide, not a promise that every group moves neatly forward. Groups progress at different rates; some remain in storming. Storming and performing can coexist, and a group can return to an earlier difficulty. A clear purpose and strategy, and a positive social focus, may help a group reach productive work sooner.</p><p>Moving to a later stage alone does not prove the group is effective.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: diagnosing a project team’s stage without treating it as a score</summary><p>A newly assembled project team is polite, asks who owns each task, and waits for the lead to settle basic questions. These clues fit forming. Two weeks later, members openly challenge the schedule and compete over priorities: that is consistent with storming. If they agree on handoffs and resolve disagreements constructively, norming is emerging.</p><p>A later return to conflict after a new member joins is possible; it does not mean the earlier progress was false.</p></details>\n<h3>Temporary groups and punctuated equilibrium</h3>\n<p>Temporary groups with deadlines may follow a different pattern. In the lecture’s semester-presentation example, the first meeting sets a direction. A period of inertia or slow progress follows. At the exact midpoint of the allotted time, a transition prompts major change. A second period of inertia follows, then activity accelerates at the last meeting as the deadline approaches.</p><p>The model emphasizes long periods of relative stability interrupted by a brief change triggered mainly by awareness of time and deadlines.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:760px\"><svg aria-label=\"Performance over time in a temporary deadline group\" height=\"350\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0 0 760 350\" width=\"760\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<defs><marker id=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-2-axis-arrow\" markerheight=\"8\" markerwidth=\"8\" orient=\"auto\" refx=\"7\" refy=\"4\"><path d=\"M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 Z\" fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" font-family=\"Georgia,serif\" font-size=\"17\">\n<path d=\"M90 270 V32 M90 270 H725\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour-figure-2-axis-arrow)\" stroke=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></path>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"48\" y=\"44\">High</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"48\" y=\"266\">Low</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 24 160)\" x=\"24\" y=\"160\">Performance</text>\n<path d=\"M130 270 V280 M410 270 V280 M690 270 V280\" stroke=\"var(--foreground,#34304a)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></path>\n<path d=\"M410 264 V77\" stroke=\"var(--muted,#526570)\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 5\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path>\n<path d=\"M130 220 H410 V110 H630 V90 H690\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"4\"></path>\n<path d=\"M130 211 V229 M690 81 V99\" stroke=\"var(--primary,#315e6a)\" stroke-width=\"3\"></path>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"260\" y=\"248\">Phase 1 · Inertia</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"520\" y=\"142\">Phase 2 · Inertia</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"end\" x=\"392\" y=\"174\">Midpoint transition</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"658\" y=\"43\">Final accelerated activity</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"end\" x=\"698\" y=\"75\">Completion</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"130\" y=\"301\">A</text>\n<text font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"130\" y=\"323\">First meeting</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"410\" y=\"301\">(A + B)/2</text>\n<text font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"410\" y=\"323\">Exactly halfway</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"690\" y=\"301\">B</text>\n<text font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"690\" y=\"323\">Deadline</text>\n<text text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"410\" y=\"346\">Time</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Schematic performance over time: low Phase 1, a transition exactly halfway through the allotted time, higher Phase 2, and final activity before completion. The vertical scale is qualitative.</figcaption></figure>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a semester presentation at its midpoint</summary><p>A group agrees on a topic at its first meeting, then makes little visible progress during the first half of the semester. At the midpoint, members recognize that half the available time is gone, divide the remaining work, and change their plan. Some slow progress may follow while those new arrangements settle.</p><p>The last meeting is intensely active as members combine the presentation before the deadline. The midpoint transition is a feature of the deadline model; it is not a sixth Tuckman stage.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Roles, norms, and sanctions</h2>\n<h3>Roles: position and expectations</h3>\n<p>A <strong>role</strong> is the set of behaviour patterns expected of someone in a position within a social unit. People hold several roles at work and outside it. <strong>Role perception</strong> is how a person understands the tasks, goals, and problems attached to a role. <strong>Role expectations</strong> are what the group or society expects that person to do.</p><p>A <strong>role conflict</strong> occurs when simultaneous expectations are incompatible, or when responsibilities overlap with another worker or group. Greater role conflict is associated with greater work-related stress.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: overlapping responsibility creates role conflict</summary><p>A project coordinator is told by one manager to release a report immediately and by another to withhold it until confidential figures are checked. The problem is not simply a large workload: the two role expectations are incompatible. Clarifying decision authority and the release procedure addresses the conflict more directly than asking the coordinator to work faster.</p></details>\n<h3>Group norms and their domains</h3>\n<p><strong>Norms</strong> are shared standards about what members ought or ought not to do in particular situations. They are different from formal rules, policies, and laws, which are enforced through an organization’s legitimate hierarchy. Norms emerge more naturally and often influence behaviour with little external control. They can cover nearly any aspect of group behaviour.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Norm domain</th>\n<th>What members may expect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance</td>\n<td>How hard to work, how to do a task, and what level of lateness is acceptable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appearance</td>\n<td>Dress, presentation, or unspoken expectations about looking busy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social arrangements</td>\n<td>Whom to eat with, off-job friendships, or when to offer a community elder tobacco before asking advice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resource allocation</td>\n<td>How difficult jobs, pay, equipment, or other resources are assigned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communication</td>\n<td>Turn-taking and respectful language during discussion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision-making</td>\n<td>Whether to seek consensus or use majority rule, and how members follow the outcome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: identify the norm and the formal rule</summary><p>A department has a written safety policy requiring eye protection in the workshop. That is a formal rule. Team members also tend to wait until the shift lead finishes before raising a concern. That turn-taking expectation is a communication norm. A group may have both at once; the norm is not a substitute for the written safety requirement.</p></details>\n<p><strong>Social sanctions</strong> are group responses that reinforce norms. Positive sanctions reward conformity, for example through praise, recognition, increased status, or access to group benefits. Negative sanctions express disapproval, such as ostracism, criticism, or loss of trust and support. Sanctions can promote conformity, reinforce a group’s sense of cohesion, and help members maintain order.</p><p>But conformity by itself does not show that a norm is fair, useful, or consistent with formal policy.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a sanction reinforces a harmful norm</summary><p>If coworkers praise members who stay silent about a recurring safety problem and exclude a member who raises it, praise and exclusion are positive and negative sanctions, respectively. They reinforce the group’s norm of silence, but the norm can still be harmful and conflict with formal safety expectations.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Deviant workplace behaviour</h2>\n<p>Deviant workplace behaviour—also called antisocial behaviour or workplace incivility in the lecture—is voluntary behaviour that violates significant organizational norms and threatens the well-being of the organization or its members. The lecture sorts examples along <strong>two separate dimensions</strong>: who or what is targeted, and how serious the act is.</p><p>Its image-only diagram on slide 34 places interpersonal behaviour on the vertical axis and minor-to-serious behaviour on the horizontal axis.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target ↓ / seriousness →</th>\n<th>Minor</th>\n<th>Serious</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interpersonally directed</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Political deviance:</strong> spreading rumours, favouritism, backstabbing</td>\n<td><strong>Personal aggression:</strong> sexual harassment, verbal abuse, physical assault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Organizationally directed</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Production deviance:</strong> absenteeism, lateness, withholding effort</td>\n<td><strong>Property deviance:</strong> theft, sabotage, vandalism</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture’s examples also include leaving early, intentionally working slowly, and wasting resources (production); misusing equipment and stealing from the organization (property); blaming coworkers (political); and stealing from coworkers (personal aggression). Do not sort an act by its label alone: classify its target and seriousness. The slide’s four categories summarize common patterns; a specific example may need context to judge severity.</p>\n<p>Causes described in the lecture include perceived unfairness in pay, promotion, or workload; job dissatisfaction; an unsupportive or disrespectful culture; stress or burnout; ineffective leadership; peer norms; opportunity and rationalization; and individual differences such as low conscientiousness or high impulsivity. These are possible contributors, not a diagnostic checklist. One behaviour alone does not identify its cause or establish a trait.</p>\n<p>The lecture proposes a possible group-level chain: shirking, undermining, or noncooperation can contribute to negative group moods; those moods can impair coordination and lower group performance.</p><p>Prevention therefore includes respectful and inclusive culture, clear policies and expectations, open communication and feedback, employee involvement, constructive conflict-resolution processes, support systems such as counselling or employee assistance, recognition and training, monitoring the work environment, and leaders modelling appropriate conduct.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: classify and respond to two different acts</summary><p>A team member repeatedly arrives late and withholds effort. The lecture places lateness and withholding effort in production deviance: the target is organizational work, and the examples are on the minor side of the source’s seriousness axis. A supervisor discovers deliberate equipment sabotage: that is property deviance, directed at organizational property and on the serious side.</p><p>For either case, first establish what happened and apply clear policy consistently; do not assume the same cause or response fits both people.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">Status, voice, and group size</h2>\n<p><strong>Status</strong> is a socially defined position or rank assigned to a group or member by others. A perceived mismatch between the status a person believes they have and the status others give them can motivate behaviour. The lecture identifies three sources: <strong>power</strong> over people or resources; <strong>contribution</strong> that is critical to group goals; and <strong>personal characteristics</strong> valued by that group.</p><p>What a group values varies by context; examples in the lecture are illustrations of local valuation, not universal traits or predictions about individuals.</p>\n<p>Status interacts with norms. Higher-status members may receive more freedom to depart from norms and may resist conformity, especially when they are valued but do not depend on group social rewards. Adding high-status members may improve performance only up to a point; they may also introduce counterproductive norms.</p><p>Status also affects who speaks: high-status members tend to speak, criticize, command, and interrupt more, while lower-status members may participate less. When quieter members have relevant expertise, failing to hear them can reduce creativity and performance.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a lower-status expert is not heard</summary><p>In a planning meeting, a junior technician notices that the proposed schedule conflicts with a maintenance window. Senior members talk over the warning. The group should distinguish speaking time and rank from the quality of the information: invite the technician to explain the constraint and check the schedule before deciding.</p><p>The scenario illustrates how status can shape voice; it does not imply that lower-status members are always correct.</p></details>\n<p>Group size depends on the task. The lecture suggests that a dozen or more members can be useful when the aim is to gather diverse input or facts. A smaller group of about seven may be better when members must produce something concrete within a deadline. These are task-dependent lecture guidelines, not universal cutoffs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-6\">Effort, cohesion, and performance</h2>\n<h3>Social loafing</h3>\n<p><strong>Social loafing</strong>, or free riding, is the tendency to exert less effort in a group than when working alone. When responsibility is shared, a person may feel less accountable for the overall result.</p><p>The lecture lists diffusion of responsibility, reduced accountability, unidentifiable contributions, low perceived task importance, the presence of free riders, larger group size, and negative group culture or low cohesion as possible contributors.</p>\n<p>The lecture qualifies the pattern by culture: the studies it reports found social loafing more consistently in individualist US and Canadian settings, while the Chinese and Israeli comparison groups showed no propensity to engage in social loafing and performed better in a group than alone. Treat this as a reported group-level finding, not a rule for predicting any individual or country.</p>\n<p>The suggested remedies are to set shared group goals; use intergroup competition focused on shared outcomes; use peer evaluation; select members who are motivated and prefer group work; and, where possible, base part of group rewards on each member’s identifiable contribution. These approaches make purpose, effort, and contribution more visible.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: select a remedy that addresses the cause</summary><p>A large team’s report is late, and members cannot tell who owns which section. Because contributions are hard to identify and responsibility is diffuse, assigning named sections with peer review and a shared deadline directly improves visibility and accountability. Adding a reward without clarifying contributions may not address the problem.</p><p>If low task importance is the issue, the manager should also explain how the report will be used.</p></details>\n<h3>Cohesion depends on performance norms</h3>\n<p><strong>Cohesion</strong> (also called cohesiveness or social cohesion) is the strength of the bonds linking members and their sense of unity. Cohesion affects productivity through the group’s performance-related norms. It does not guarantee high output by itself: a cohesive group reinforces the standards it shares.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Performance-related norms</th>\n<th>High cohesion</th>\n<th>Low cohesion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High</strong></td>\n<td>High productivity</td>\n<td>Productivity rises, but less than when both are high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low</strong></td>\n<td>Low productivity</td>\n<td>Low-to-moderate productivity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture lists ways to encourage cohesion: make the group smaller, agree on group goals, spend more time together, raise group status and the perceived difficulty of membership, stimulate competition with other groups, give rewards to the group, and physically isolate it. These are possible levers, not unconditional recommendations; choose them only when they support the group’s task and healthy norms.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: cohesion amplifies the norm a group already has</summary><p>Two teams feel equally close to one another. Team A has strong norms for quality and cooperation, so its high cohesion supports coordinated, high productivity. Team B treats low output as acceptable; its cohesion can reinforce that low standard, leaving productivity low. To improve Team B, changing the performance norm matters as well as strengthening bonds.</p></details>\n<section aria-label=\"Why closeness alone is not enough\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"cohesion\"><h3>Why closeness alone is not enough</h3><label>Performance norms<select data-input=\"norms\"><option value=\"high\">High</option><option value=\"low\">Low</option></select></label><label>Group cohesion<select data-input=\"cohesion\"><option value=\"high\">High</option><option value=\"low\">Low</option></select></label><table class=\"matrix\"><caption>Performance norms × Cohesion</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Performance norms</th><th scope=\"col\">High</th><th scope=\"col\">Low</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">High</th><td data-cell=\"high-high\">High productivity</td><td data-cell=\"high-low\">Productivity rises, but less</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Low</th><td data-cell=\"low-high\">Low productivity</td><td data-cell=\"low-low\">Low-to-moderate productivity</td></tr></tbody></table><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-result=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Change the norms while keeping cohesion high. Read how the predicted direction changes.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to change the inputs. The course model is explained above.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-7\">Diversity and faultlines</h2>\n<p>Group diversity means including members with varied backgrounds and perspectives, including differences such as age, gender, ethnicity, socioeconomic position, and culture. Variety can add creativity, insight, and problem-solving capacity, while also creating challenges for group dynamics.</p><p>The lecture says diverse groups may experience more conflict early in their time together, with lower morale and greater dropout, and may perform better over time if initial conflict is managed. This is a qualification about group processes, not an individual prediction based on demographic identity.</p>\n<p>A <strong>group faultline</strong> is a hypothetical dividing line that can form when member attributes align and divide a group into subgroups. Slides 68–69 call the topic “Faultiness,” but slide 68’s examples use “group faultline”; the correct term is <strong>faultline</strong>.</p><p>The concept was developed as “faultlines” in Lau and Murnighan’s primary article on how member characteristics align into potential subgroups (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5465/amr.1998.533229\">Lau &amp; Murnighan, 1998</a>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source illustration</th>\n<th>Attribute alignment</th>\n<th>What makes the alignment strong or weak</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong faultline</td>\n<td>Two young female Asian members and two senior male Caucasian members</td>\n<td>Age, gender, and ethnicity align into two internally similar subgroups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak faultline</td>\n<td>A young female Asian, a young male Caucasian, a senior female Caucasian, and a senior male Asian member</td>\n<td>The source describes the possible subgroups as diverse rather than split into two homogeneous sets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are hypothetical examples of <strong>attribute alignment</strong>, not claims about the abilities, preferences, or behaviour of people in any demographic category. Faultline effects arise from subgroup division and interaction. The lecture associates splits with competition that takes time from core work, slower learning, riskier decisions, lower creativity, greater conflict, and less trust between subgroups.</p><p>Overall group satisfaction may be lower even while subgroup satisfaction is high.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: reduce a subgroup split by changing the work structure</summary><p>A four-person team notices that two members always consult one another while the other two are left out of decisions. Rather than infer why from anyone’s identity, the manager can rotate mixed pairs, make information visible to the full team, and give the group a shared task requiring cross-pair coordination.</p><p>The aim is to reduce separation and improve trust; it does not assume that mixing people automatically removes every disagreement.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-8\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating Tuckman’s stages as mandatory, perfectly sequential, or as a score of group effectiveness. Groups can stall, overlap stages, or regress.</li>\n<li>Calling the deadline midpoint a Tuckman stage. Punctuated equilibrium is a separate model for temporary groups.</li>\n<li>Confusing role perception (how a person understands a role) with role expectations (what others expect), or calling any busy workload role conflict.</li>\n<li>Treating norms as formal policies, or assuming that a sanctioned norm is beneficial.</li>\n<li>Classifying deviance by just one axis. Use both target and seriousness; the categories are not a single mild-to-severe list.</li>\n<li>Assuming one cause explains deviance or that an observed act diagnoses an individual trait.</li>\n<li>Assuming status equals expertise, or overlooking useful contributions because the speaker has less status.</li>\n<li>Treating the lecture’s approximate group sizes as fixed rules, or assuming social loafing occurs equally in every cultural context.</li>\n<li>Assuming cohesion alone raises output, or that diversity automatically guarantees either conflict or success.</li>\n<li>Reading the source’s demographic faultline illustration as a prediction about an individual. It demonstrates aligned attributes and hypothetical subgroup structure.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-9\"></span><h2 id=\"section-10\">Practice questions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>A newly assigned group is polite, seeks clarity, and relies on its leader. Which Tuckman stage best fits, and what would be a useful caution?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p><strong>Forming.</strong> The clues are polite interaction, uncertainty about purpose, and reliance on the leader. It is a provisional description, not proof the group will follow a fixed sequence.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>A short-lived committee changes its plan exactly halfway to a deadline after a slow start. Which model explains this pattern?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p><strong>Punctuated equilibrium.</strong> It describes a midpoint transition in a temporary deadline group, followed by another period of inertia and accelerated activity near the final meeting.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Classify lateness and deliberate sabotage using the lecture’s deviance axes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Lateness is an example of <strong>minor, organizationally directed production deviance</strong>. Sabotage is <strong>serious, organizationally directed property deviance</strong>.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>A group has strong cohesion but weak performance norms. What productivity relationship should you expect?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p><strong>Low productivity.</strong> Cohesion reinforces the norms the group holds; high cohesion does not guarantee high output.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Why might a lower-status member’s silence harm a group even when the meeting appears orderly?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The member may hold relevant expertise or insight. If status suppresses participation, the group can lose information and reduce its creativity or performance.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>What makes the lecture’s first faultline example strong, and what should not be inferred from it?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Several attributes align into two internally similar subgroups, creating a stronger hypothetical dividing line. Do not infer abilities or behaviour from age, gender, or ethnicity; the example illustrates alignment only.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Name two ways to reduce social loafing and connect each to a cause.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>For example, peer evaluation and identifiable contribution-based rewards address reduced accountability and unidentifiable effort. Shared group goals can address weak common purpose. Select remedies that match the actual cause.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"individual-revision.html\"><small>Previous</small>Short note: individuals at work <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-7-group-decision-making.html\"><small>Next</small>Group Decision-Making <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-7-group-decision-making":{"title":"Group Decision-Making · OB and Management","description":"Compare groupthink, groupshift and three ways to organize a group decision.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Group behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 7B</div><h1>Group Decision-Making</h1><p class=\"lead\">Compare groupthink, groupshift and three ways to organize a group decision.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 7 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">Pool knowledge without silencing doubt</a><a href=\"#section-1\">When a group decision helps—and when it costs</a><a href=\"#section-2\">Groupthink: conformity that blocks critical appraisal</a><a href=\"#section-3\">Groupshift: discussion moves positions toward an extreme</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Three decision techniques</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">Pool knowledge without silencing doubt</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Groups can pool information, but discussion also costs time and can suppress dissent.</li><li>Groupthink weakens critical appraisal. Groupshift moves a position toward an extreme, risky or cautious.</li><li>Choose a technique for its trade-offs. No method wins every comparison.</li></ul>\n<p>Two group processes can damage objective appraisal in different ways. <strong>Groupthink</strong> is conformity pressure that suppresses critical examination of dissent. <strong>Groupshift</strong> describes members’ decisions becoming more extreme after discussion, in either a riskier or more cautious direction. Decision techniques structure participation differently and suit different criteria.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">When a group decision helps—and when it costs</h2>\n<p>Pooling people’s information and knowledge can make the available picture more complete. Different members bring more input and heterogeneous views, which can expose additional approaches and alternatives. Participation can also increase acceptance: members who took part are more likely to support and encourage others to accept the solution.</p>\n<p>The same discussion takes longer than an individual decision. Members may hold back disagreement to remain accepted, one or a few people may dominate, and a dominant member with low or medium ability can reduce the group’s effectiveness. Responsibility for the outcome is also less clearly assigned than when one individual decides.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: a safety review under deadline pressure</summary><p>A review team includes operators, a technician, and a supervisor. Together they can pool observations that no single person has. But a deadline and a dominant senior voice may prevent the technician from raising a warning, while shared responsibility makes it unclear who must act. The group format helps only if the useful evidence is heard and someone owns the follow-up.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">Groupthink: conformity that blocks critical appraisal</h2>\n<p><strong>Groupthink</strong> occurs when pressure for conformity deters a group from critically appraising unusual, minority, or unpopular views. The diagnostic clue is not agreement by itself; it is a process in which dissent and warnings are suppressed or dismissed. That can reduce opposition and creativity, raise overconfidence, cause critical information to be ignored, and limit consideration of diverse perspectives.</p>\n<div aria-label=\"Groupthink symptoms and recognition cues\" class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\"><table aria-label=\"Eight groupthink symptoms and how to recognize them\"><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Illusion of invulnerability</td><td>Members feel overly optimistic and ignore potential risks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collective rationalization</td><td>Members dismiss warnings and negative feedback.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Belief in inherent group morality</td><td>Members assume the group’s decisions are morally superior.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stereotyping outsiders</td><td>People outside the group are viewed negatively.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pressure to conform</td><td>Members pressure dissenters to accept the group’s view.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Self-censorship</td><td>Members keep their own doubts to themselves.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Illusion of unanimity</td><td>Silence is interpreted as consent.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mind guards</td><td>Members shield the group from dissenting information.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: apparent agreement hides groupthink</summary><p>A committee is confident that its plan cannot fail. Members dismiss a risk report, a colleague privately withholds a concern, and the chair treats silence as unanimous support. Dismissing the warning is collective rationalization; withholding a doubt is self-censorship; reading silence as consent is an illusion of unanimity. Together, these clues show suppressed appraisal, not reliable consensus.</p></details>\n<p>Groups focused more on performance than learning are especially likely to suppress majority-challenging views. The lecture reports one study in which people influenced by groupthink were more confident about their course of action early on. It also notes that strong certainty about correctness is more likely to suppress disagreement than skepticism.</p><p>These are qualified lecture claims, not a rule that every confident or performance-focused group has groupthink.</p>\n<p>Prevention should make it possible to surface and examine disagreement: encourage open dialogue; appoint one or more devil’s advocates; include diverse perspectives; use structured processes such as brainstorming or nominal group technique; collect anonymous feedback; encourage independent thought and research before discussion; use breakout groups; and leave time for reflection and feedback. These are safeguards, not guarantees.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Groupshift: discussion moves positions toward an extreme</h2>\n<p><strong>Groupshift</strong>, also called <strong>group polarization</strong> here, is a change in which members’ opinions or decisions become more extreme than the positions they would have taken individually. Discussion can amplify existing attitudes toward either riskier or more cautious choices. Unlike groupthink, groupshift describes the direction and intensity of the resulting position; it does not by itself mean dissent was suppressed.</p>\n<p>The lecture lists several possible explanations: people adjust toward what seems to be the dominant view to gain approval (<strong>social comparison</strong>); cohesion and a wish for harmony can strengthen group identity; members encounter new persuasive arguments (<strong>informational influence</strong>); groups may seek distinction from other groups; and social loafing or diffused accountability can make members bolder.</p><p>These mechanisms are proposed explanations, not outcomes that every group must show.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: the same shift can be risky or cautious</summary><p>Before discussion, one committee is mildly in favour of a bold expansion. After hearing supporting arguments and wanting to show commitment, it endorses a much more aggressive plan: this is a riskier groupshift. Another committee begins mildly cautious, hears further concerns, and rejects even a modest change: this is a cautious groupshift. Both positions moved further in their initial direction.</p></details>\n<div aria-label=\"Compare groupthink and groupshift\" class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\"><table aria-label=\"Diagnostic distinction between groupthink and groupshift\"><thead><tr><th>Check the process</th><th>Groupthink</th><th>Groupshift / polarization</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>What changed?</td><td>Critical appraisal is suppressed by conformity pressure.</td><td>The post-discussion position is more extreme than individual positions.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Useful clues</td><td>Self-censorship, pressure on dissenters, mind guards, silence treated as agreement.</td><td>Movement toward greater risk or greater caution after discussion.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Core distinction</td><td>A conformity process.</td><td>A change in position and its extremity.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>They can coexist, but they are not synonyms: a group might suppress dissent while becoming more extreme, yet either process can occur without the other.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Three decision techniques</h2>\n<p>The lecture compares interacting groups, brainstorming, and nominal group technique (NGT). Their strengths and limits differ, so selection depends on the criteria that matter and the cost-benefit trade-off.</p>\n<div aria-label=\"Decision technique process and trade-offs\" class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\"><table aria-label=\"Process, strengths, and trade-offs of the three decision techniques\" class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Technique</th><th scope=\"col\">Process</th><th scope=\"col\">Strengths</th><th scope=\"col\">Trade-offs</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Interacting group</th><td data-label=\"Process\">Members meet face to face and communicate verbally and nonverbally; decision rules vary, with majority vote as one example.</td><td data-label=\"Strengths\">Familiar interaction; the comparison rates commitment and cohesiveness high.</td><td data-label=\"Trade-offs\">The comparison rates idea quality low and social pressure and interpersonal conflict high.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Brainstorming</th><td data-label=\"Process\">A leader states the problem; roughly 6–12 members freely generate alternatives for a set time; criticism is paused and ideas are recorded for later analysis.</td><td data-label=\"Strengths\">Encourages unusual ideas without immediate criticism; the comparison rates task orientation and cohesiveness high.</td><td data-label=\"Trade-offs\">The lecture cautions that people working alone often generate more ideas: simultaneous talk and waiting can interrupt thought and sharing. It is not always more productive than independent generation.</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Nominal group technique</th><td data-label=\"Process\">Members first work independently; ideas are presented and recorded before discussion; the group clarifies and evaluates; members silently rank ideas and aggregate rankings.</td><td data-label=\"Strengths\">Restricts early interpersonal pressure; the comparison rates number and quality of ideas high.</td><td data-label=\"Trade-offs\">More structured; the comparison rates social pressure and conflict moderate, and commitment and cohesiveness moderate.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: choose a process for surfacing alternatives</summary><p>A team needs a range of options but worries that its manager speaks first and sets the direction. NGT’s silent individual writing and round-robin recording reduce the chance that early discussion controls which ideas appear. If the main need is a familiar face-to-face decision with strong commitment, an interacting group may fit better. Neither choice is best for every criterion.</p></details>\n<h3>Nominal group technique, step by step</h3>\n<p>NGT limits communication at key points while keeping members physically together. The order matters: members create ideas independently first, all ideas are recorded before discussion, clarification and evaluation happen together, and each member ranks ideas independently before aggregation.</p>\n<section aria-label=\"Nominal group technique: from private ideas to an aggregate choice\" class=\"worked-calculation\"><h3>Nominal group technique: from private ideas to an aggregate choice</h3><div class=\"example-input\"><p><strong>Teaching example:</strong> a customer-support group needs to choose a response to recurring complaints. The method separates private generation and ranking from group clarification and evaluation.</p></div><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>1 · Silent individual generation</h4><p>Each member independently writes possible fixes for recurring customer complaints. Their private lists might include clearer intake questions, a call-back option, or a staff reminder. No group discussion has started, so early speakers cannot anchor the list.</p></section><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>2 · Round-robin presentation and recording</h4><p>Members present one idea at a time. Record all proposals, including the intake-form revision, call-back option, and reminder. Do not discuss or evaluate them until every member has presented.</p></section><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>3 · Clarify and evaluate together</h4><p>Now discuss the recorded ideas to clarify what each would involve and evaluate them. For example, clarify which intake questions cause confusion and whether a call-back would address the same complaint. This is the discussion stage; it follows, rather than interrupts, idea presentation.</p></section><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>4 · Silent ranking and aggregate decision</h4><p>Each member independently rank-orders the ideas. Combine the rankings. In this <strong>Teaching example</strong>, suppose revising the intake form receives the highest aggregate ranking; that becomes the selected decision. The example specifies the result without inventing or implying a scoring rule. No individual member’s preference alone determines the group decision.</p></section></section>\n<h3>Compare the effectiveness ratings</h3>\n<p>The exhibit rates each technique on eight distinct criteria. A high score is not always desirable: for example, high idea quality is an advantage, while high social pressure or conflict is a cost. Read each row in context rather than treating a column as an overall winner.</p>\n<div aria-label=\"Group effectiveness ratings across three decision techniques\" class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\"><table aria-label=\"Eight effectiveness criteria rated for interacting groups, brainstorming, and nominal group technique\" class=\"effectiveness-matrix comparison\" data-stack=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Effectiveness criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Interacting</th><th scope=\"col\">Brainstorming</th><th scope=\"col\">Nominal</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Number and quality of ideas</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">Low</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">Moderate</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">High</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Social pressure</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">High</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">Low</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">Moderate</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Money costs</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">Low</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">Low</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">Low</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Speed</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">Moderate</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">Moderate</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">Moderate</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Task orientation</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">Low</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">High</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">High</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Potential for interpersonal conflict</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">High</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">Low</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">Moderate</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Commitment to solution</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">High</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">Not applicable</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">Moderate</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Development of group cohesiveness</th><td data-label=\"Interacting\">High</td><td data-label=\"Brainstorming\">High</td><td data-label=\"Nominal\">Moderate</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<script>\ndocument.querySelectorAll('main .table-scroll[role=\"region\"]').forEach((region) => {\n  region.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n    const step = Math.max(80, Math.round(region.clientWidth * 0.7));\n    if (event.key === 'ArrowRight') { event.preventDefault(); region.scrollBy({left: step}); }\n    else if (event.key === 'ArrowLeft') { event.preventDefault(); region.scrollBy({left: -step}); }\n    else if (event.key === 'Home') { event.preventDefault(); region.scrollTo({left: 0}); }\n    else if (event.key === 'End') { event.preventDefault(); region.scrollTo({left: region.scrollWidth}); }\n  });\n});\n</script>\n<p>For example, the matrix rates NGT high for idea number and quality, but interacting groups high for commitment to the solution and group cohesiveness. All three are rated moderate for speed. The criteria point to different trade-offs rather than one universally superior method.</p>\n<section aria-label=\"Choose the decision technique\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"techniques\"><h3>Choose the decision technique</h3><label>Choose a case<select aria-label=\"Choose a case\" data-case=\"\"></select></label><p data-scenario=\"\"></p><div aria-label=\"Choose an answer\" class=\"activity-choices\" data-choices=\"\" role=\"group\"></div><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-feedback=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Teaching cases. Choose an answer, read why it fits, then try another case.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript for this activity. The definitions and worked examples above remain available.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-5\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Saying group decisions are always better. Compare information, viewpoints, acceptance, time, conformity, dominance, and accountability.</li>\n<li>Calling any agreement groupthink. Look for conformity pressure and blocked critical appraisal.</li>\n<li>Treating groupshift as always risky. Positions can become more extreme in a cautious direction too.</li>\n<li>Confusing groupthink with groupshift: one concerns suppressed evaluation; the other concerns post-discussion extremity.</li>\n<li>Treating brainstorming’s no-criticism rule as a claim that solo ideation is inferior. Criticism is deferred during generation, and the lecture cautions about idea-generation efficiency.</li>\n<li>Discussing NGT proposals before all members have presented and all ideas have been recorded.</li>\n<li>Calling one method best overall based on one row of the effectiveness exhibit.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-6\"></span><h2 id=\"section-7\">Practice questions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>A committee has several members with relevant knowledge, but a senior participant dominates and members are short on time. Name two potential group benefits and two costs that matter here.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Pooling knowledge and diverse viewpoints can improve the information and alternatives considered; participation can also improve acceptance. Costs include time, domination, conformity pressure, and diluted responsibility. In this case, dominance and the deadline directly threaten the benefits.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>A member privately doubts a proposal but says nothing; another screens out a critical report. Name the two most direct groupthink symptoms.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p><strong>Self-censorship</strong> is withholding the member’s doubt. <strong>Mind guarding</strong> is shielding the group from dissenting information.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>A cautious group rejects an even wider range of options after discussion. Could this be groupshift? Explain.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Yes. Groupshift can move a position toward greater caution as well as greater risk. The diagnostic feature is a more extreme post-discussion position relative to members’ individual positions.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Put the four NGT stages in order and state when discussion begins.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Silent individual idea writing; one-at-a-time presentation and recording of all ideas; group clarification and evaluation; silent independent ranking with aggregate selection. Discussion begins only after every idea has been presented and recorded.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Which technique is rated high for number and quality of ideas? Which is rated high for commitment to solution?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>Nominal group technique is rated <strong>high</strong> for number and quality of ideas. Interacting groups are rated <strong>high</strong> for commitment to solution.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>During brainstorming, should members immediately criticize unusual suggestions? Does the method always produce more ideas than people working alone?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>No. Criticism is paused during idea generation and ideas are recorded for later evaluation. The lecture says individuals working alone often generate more ideas than brainstorming groups, so brainstorming is not an automatic improvement over solo generation.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Why does the exhibit’s “High” rating require interpretation before choosing a method?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"question\">Check your answer</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Check your answer</summary><p>The row determines whether high is desirable. High idea quality can be an advantage, while high social pressure or interpersonal conflict is a cost. The exhibit therefore supports a criterion-based trade-off, not an all-purpose ranking.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-7-group-structure-and-behaviour.html\"><small>Previous</small>Group Structure and Behaviour <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness.html\"><small>Next</small>Work Teams and Effectiveness <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness":{"title":"Work Teams and Effectiveness · OB and Management","description":"Decide when a team helps, then examine its context, composition and process.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Group behaviour <span>/</span> Lecture 8</div><h1>Work Teams and Effectiveness</h1><p class=\"lead\">Decide when a team helps, then examine its context, composition and process.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 9 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#section-0\">Choose teamwork for interdependent work</a><a href=\"#section-1\">What makes a team different from a work group?</a><a href=\"#section-2\">Common forms of work teams</a><a href=\"#section-3\">The team-effectiveness model</a><a href=\"#section-4\">Developing team players</a><a href=\"#section-5\">Decide whether work belongs in a team</a><a href=\"#section-6\">A practitioner framework: New Rules for Teamwork</a><a href=\"#section-7\">Common mistakes</a><a href=\"#section-9\">Practice questions</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"section-0\">Choose teamwork for interdependent work</h2>\n<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A work team combines interdependent effort toward a shared purpose and collective performance.</li><li>Test whether several people can do better, share a purpose and depend on one another.</li><li>Context, Composition and Process organize the lecture model. The assigned article adds a separate practice of routines, measurement and adjustment.</li></ul>\n<p>The assigned practitioner article adds a separate operating approach: establish routines, measure how work and outcomes are going, then adapt the system. Its recommendations and company examples complement the lecture, but they are not part of the lecture’s team-effectiveness model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1\">What makes a team different from a work group?</h2>\n<p>A <strong>team</strong> is a group whose members work interdependently toward a shared goal or mission. A <strong>work team</strong> coordinates member effort so the collective performance can exceed the sum of individual inputs; this is called <strong>positive synergy</strong>. A work team is a purposeful subset of work groups.</p><p>A group may still generate ideas, pool resources or coordinate logistics, but it need not depend on shared responsibility for collective performance.</p>\n<p>The lecture’s “Strong Sense of Teamwork” image uses four interlocking pieces: <strong>Goal</strong> (everyone works toward the same goal), <strong>Harmony</strong> (trust, respect and enjoyment of working together), <strong>Roles</strong> (clarity about tasks and responsibilities), and <strong>Support</strong> (helping teammates and putting team success before one’s own). These ideas describe features of teamwork; the source does not present them as a guarantee of results.</p>\n<div aria-label=\"Work group and work team comparison\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<table>\n<caption>Lecture comparison of work groups and work teams</caption>\n<thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Dimension</th><th scope=\"col\">Work groups</th><th scope=\"col\">Work teams</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Goal</th><td>Share information</td><td>Collective performance</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Synergy</th><td>Neutral, sometimes negative</td><td>Positive</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Accountability</th><td>Individual</td><td>Individual and mutual</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Skills</th><td>Random and varied</td><td>Complementary</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p>The second source comparison contrasts designated leadership and individual work products in groups with shared leadership, mutual accountability and collective work products in teams. Teams also use joint goals, feedback and active problem-solving. These are lecture contrasts, not claims that every group lacks collaboration.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: when a group becomes a team</summary><p>Several analysts meet monthly to share sales updates, but each owns a separate report and is evaluated individually. They coordinate information as a work group.</p><p>If they instead depend on one another to produce a single forecast, agree shared goals, coordinate complementary analyses and accept mutual responsibility for the combined result, their work has the interdependence and collective-performance orientation of a team. Calling the meeting a “team meeting” alone would not make the difference.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-2\">Common forms of work teams</h2>\n<p>Team types describe how work is organized. Some dimensions can overlap: for example, a cross-functional team may also be virtual.</p>\n<table class=\"comparison\" data-stack=\"\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Type</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">How it works</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Useful distinction or challenge</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Problem-solving</th>\n<td data-label=\"How it works\">Often 5–10 people from one department meet regularly about a specific problem; a facilitator may guide discussion.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction or challenge\">The group develops and considers solutions; the format alone does not mean it has authority to implement every one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Self-managed</th>\n<td data-label=\"How it works\">Members organize and manage their work without traditional hierarchical supervision.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction or challenge\">Fully self-managed forms may select members and evaluate one another; former supervisor positions may become less important or disappear.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Cross-functional</th>\n<td data-label=\"How it works\">People at a similar hierarchical level from different work areas exchange information, create ideas, solve problems and coordinate projects.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction or challenge\">Coordination is demanding and early development can take longer as members learn to work across diverse expertise. Face-to-face collaboration is a strength highlighted in the lecture.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Virtual</th>\n<td data-label=\"How it works\">Members collaborate electronically while geographically dispersed rather than sharing one location.</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction or challenge\">Trust, progress visibility and publicizing results matter. Isolation, weaker social rapport and dispersed information can create challenges.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"row\">Multiteam system</th>\n<td data-label=\"How it works\">Two or more interdependent teams coordinate toward a higher shared goal: a “team of teams.”</td>\n<td data-label=\"Useful distinction or challenge\">It links teams’ contributions across boundaries; it is not just one cross-functional team.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: cross-functional team or multiteam system?</summary><p>A product launch group that brings together marketing, design and engineering employees is cross-functional: individuals from multiple functions work together. If separate product, logistics and customer-support teams remain distinct but depend on coordinated handoffs to deliver the launch, the larger arrangement is a multiteam system. A boundary spanner coordinates with other subteams and can reduce the number of direct cross-team contacts each member must manage.</p></details>\n<p>For virtual teams, the lecture recommends establishing trust, monitoring progress and making work visible. Sharing unique information and sharing more information overall are different outcomes.</p><details><summary>Virtual-team research reported in the slides</summary><p>A review of 94 studies and more than 5,000 groups found better sharing of uniquely held information in virtual teams, but less information sharing overall. The lecture also reports differences by online communication level. These patterns do not mean that less communication is always better.</p></details>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: separate unique information from total sharing</summary><p>A remote project team includes a safety specialist whose concern is known only to them. The team surfaces that unique fact during its review, yet members exchange fewer updates and assumptions overall than a co-located group. Both observations can be true: finding a distinctive piece of information is different from sharing a larger volume of information.</p><p>Managers should check whether essential information is being surfaced and coordinated rather than treating message count as the outcome.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">The team-effectiveness model</h2>\n<p>The lecture combines three views of effectiveness: objective productivity measures, managers’ ratings of team performance, and aggregated member satisfaction. A fully effective team meets key stakeholder standards, has internal processes that can sustain future collaboration, and provides an experience that meets members’ personal needs.</p>\n<p>The model is a guide, not a rigid formula. Teams differ in form and structure, and work should be assigned to teams only when teamwork is preferable to individual work. The model groups considerations into <strong>Context</strong>, <strong>Composition</strong> and <strong>Process</strong>. The source diagram connects each whole category to team effectiveness; it does not draw arrows from individual items or establish guaranteed causal effects.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:560.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Lecture model: Context, Composition and Process are grouped inputs that converge on Team effectiveness. The diagram does not assign item-level causal arrows.\" height=\"450pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 560.00 450.00\" width=\"560pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 439.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- context -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-node1\">\n<title>context</title>\n<path d=\"M306.6,-428.4C306.6,-428.4 12,-428.4 12,-428.4 6,-428.4 0,-422.4 0,-416.4 0,-416.4 0,-336 0,-336 0,-330 6,-324 12,-324 12,-324 306.6,-324 306.6,-324 312.6,-324 318.6,-330 318.6,-336 318.6,-336 318.6,-416.4 318.6,-416.4 318.6,-422.4 312.6,-428.4 306.6,-428.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-406.95\">CONTEXT</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-388.95\">Adequate resources</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-370.95\">Leadership and structure</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-352.95\">Climate of trust</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-334.95\">Performance evaluation and reward systems</text>\n</g>\n<!-- outcome -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-node4\">\n<title>outcome</title>\n<path d=\"M525.95,-241.2C525.95,-241.2 362.6,-241.2 362.6,-241.2 356.6,-241.2 350.6,-235.2 350.6,-229.2 350.6,-229.2 350.6,-217.2 350.6,-217.2 350.6,-211.2 356.6,-205.2 362.6,-205.2 362.6,-205.2 525.95,-205.2 525.95,-205.2 531.95,-205.2 537.95,-211.2 537.95,-217.2 537.95,-217.2 537.95,-229.2 537.95,-229.2 537.95,-235.2 531.95,-241.2 525.95,-241.2\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"444.28\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-217.95\">TEAM EFFECTIVENESS</text>\n</g>\n<!-- context&#45;&gt;outcome -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-edge1\">\n<title>context-&gt;outcome</title>\n<path d=\"M298.53,-323.58C305.39,-320.22 312.12,-316.76 318.6,-313.2 353.98,-293.78 391.06,-265.91 415.48,-246.34\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"416.61,-248.36 420.23,-242.5 413.75,-244.82 416.61,-248.36\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- composition -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-node2\">\n<title>composition</title>\n<path d=\"M227.85,-302.4C227.85,-302.4 90.75,-302.4 90.75,-302.4 84.75,-302.4 78.75,-296.4 78.75,-290.4 78.75,-290.4 78.75,-156 78.75,-156 78.75,-150 84.75,-144 90.75,-144 90.75,-144 227.85,-144 227.85,-144 233.85,-144 239.85,-150 239.85,-156 239.85,-156 239.85,-290.4 239.85,-290.4 239.85,-296.4 233.85,-302.4 227.85,-302.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-280.95\">COMPOSITION</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-262.95\">Abilities of members</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-244.95\">Personality</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-226.95\">Allocating roles</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-208.95\">Diversity</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-190.95\">Size of teams</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-172.95\">Member flexibility</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-154.95\">Member preferences</text>\n</g>\n<!-- composition&#45;&gt;outcome -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-edge2\">\n<title>composition-&gt;outcome</title>\n<path d=\"M240.25,-223.2C271.95,-223.2 308.77,-223.2 342.28,-223.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"342.25,-225.48 348.75,-223.2 342.25,-220.93 342.25,-225.48\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- process -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-node3\">\n<title>process</title>\n<path d=\"M218.1,-122.4C218.1,-122.4 100.5,-122.4 100.5,-122.4 94.5,-122.4 88.5,-116.4 88.5,-110.4 88.5,-110.4 88.5,-12 88.5,-12 88.5,-6 94.5,0 100.5,0 100.5,0 218.1,0 218.1,0 224.1,0 230.1,-6 230.1,-12 230.1,-12 230.1,-110.4 230.1,-110.4 230.1,-116.4 224.1,-122.4 218.1,-122.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-100.95\">PROCESS</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-82.95\">Common purpose</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-64.95\">Specific goals</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-46.95\">Team efficacy</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">Conflict levels</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"159.3\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">Social loafing</text>\n</g>\n<!-- process&#45;&gt;outcome -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-1-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-effectiveness-model-edge3\">\n<title>process-&gt;outcome</title>\n<path d=\"M230.36,-89.49C258.42,-101.74 290.66,-116.98 318.6,-133.2 353.14,-153.26 389.9,-180.61 414.48,-199.89\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"413.07,-201.67 419.58,-203.91 415.89,-198.1 413.07,-201.67\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Lecture model: Context, Composition and Process are grouped inputs that converge on Team effectiveness. The diagram does not assign item-level causal arrows.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Context: conditions around the team</h3>\n<p><strong>Adequate resources</strong> include timely information, equipment, staffing, encouragement and administrative support. Scarcity can hinder performance because teams rely on the wider organization. <strong>Leadership and structure</strong> should clarify roles and workload while integrating skills. Leadership can be managerial or shared; the lecture notes that shared leadership and delegation may outperform a traditional single-leader structure in some settings, especially multiteam systems.</p>\n<p>A <strong>climate of trust</strong> supports cooperation and reduces the need for constant monitoring. Members can be more willing to be vulnerable, take interpersonal risks and accept a leader’s decisions. <strong>Performance evaluation and reward systems</strong> should recognize both individual contribution and team outcomes. Individual incentives alone can hinder team development; changing the reward system by itself does not instantly create trust.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: diagnose a context problem before blaming members</summary><p>A team misses handoffs because it lacks timely access to inventory data and no one knows who can approve a substitute. The first diagnosis is contextual: information and decision structure are missing. Adding a motivational speech or a team bonus does not supply the data or clarify authority.</p><p>A better response is to provide the information and agree on the approval path, while still tracking individual and shared responsibilities.</p></details>\n<h3>Composition: who is on the team and how work is covered</h3>\n<p>Team performance depends on members’ knowledge, skills and abilities and how well these fit changing conditions; it is not a simple sum of individual ability. At the team level, higher average <strong>conscientiousness</strong> and <strong>openness to experience</strong> are linked with better performance in the lecture.</p><p>Some degree of <strong>agreeableness</strong> matters too: one or more highly disagreeable members can hurt the team, with emotional contagion offered as a related explanation. These are associations and qualifications, not personality-based predictions about a particular person.</p>\n<p><strong>Role allocation</strong> should ensure that needed roles are covered. The lecture recommends putting able, experienced and conscientious members in central roles. <strong>Diversity</strong> can broaden knowledge and support creativity and innovation when members respect and communicate with one another; communication difficulties and conflict can instead make membership less attractive and contribute to turnover.</p>\n<p>Keep a team as small as the task permits. The lecture gives <strong>five to nine</strong> as a general effective range, while emphasizing that the fewest people sufficient for the work is preferable. Too many members can reduce cohesion and accountability, increase social loafing, and raise communication and coordination costs, especially under time pressure.</p><p><strong>Member flexibility</strong> helps the team respond when work changes. <strong>Member preference</strong> also matters: forcing people into group work may harm morale and satisfaction.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: choose a team size and role mix for a changing task</summary><p>A service redesign needs customer insight, process knowledge and someone who can test a new workflow. Start with the smallest group that covers these roles and can coordinate the handoffs. If the service changes frequently, members who can adapt across tasks add flexibility. A larger group may add perspectives, but it also creates more coordination and accountability needs.</p><p>The five-to-nine range is a guide from the lecture, not a universal target.</p></details>\n<h3>Process: how members work together</h3>\n<p>A <strong>common purpose</strong> gives members a shared mission and strategy. Effective teams agree on it, then use <strong>reflexivity</strong>: they review how the plan is working and adjust when conditions change. They translate purpose into <strong>specific goals</strong> that are measurable and realistic, helping members coordinate and judge progress.</p>\n<p><strong>Team efficacy</strong> is members’ shared belief that the team can succeed; this belief can motivate effort, and training may improve it. When members share knowledge of one another’s capabilities, they can more effectively seek one another’s opinions, strengthening the link between members’ self-efficacy and individual creativity.</p><p>Do not confuse efficacy with a <strong>mental model</strong>, which is the shared understanding of the work and environment. If team members have inaccurate or divergent mental models, they may disagree about methods, especially under acute stress.</p>\n<p><strong>Conflict</strong> depends on its kind and level. Relationship conflict involves tension, incompatibility or animosity and is almost always dysfunctional. Task conflict concerns the work or ideas. For non-routine work, moderate task conflict early can prompt discussion and critical assessment; very low or very high task conflict can hurt performance.</p><p>Effective teams address issues explicitly, while ineffective discussions focus on personalities or how comments were said. <strong>Social loafing</strong> is reduced effort when responsibility is shared; individual and joint accountability can help counter it.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: task disagreement, relationship conflict and a shared mental model</summary><p>A team must choose a hospital handoff process. One member argues for a shorter form because staff have little time; another argues that it omits a safety check. That is task conflict and can help if they compare evidence and clarify the shared understanding of what a safe handoff requires.</p><p>If the discussion shifts to personal insults or claims about someone’s motives, it becomes relationship conflict. The team can restore focus by restating the purpose, checking the workflow assumptions together and assigning clear accountability for the agreed process.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">Developing team players</h2>\n<p>The lecture notes that teams may fit more readily in countries that score higher on collectivism, but this broad cultural comparison cannot determine whether an individual will be a good teammate. Select members for the role and technical requirements, alongside qualities that can support cross-cultural communication: openness to new experiences, comfort with ambiguity, empathy, social orientation and self-monitoring.</p>\n<p>Training can let employees experience the benefits of teamwork and build skills through workshops in problem-solving, communication, negotiation, conflict management and coaching. Reward systems should support cooperative contributions such as sharing information, training colleagues, helping resolve conflict and mastering needed skills. Balance recognition of individual contribution with team outcomes.</p><p>A switch from competitive to cooperative rewards does not immediately ensure information sharing or undo rushed decisions and distrust.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: align selection, training and rewards</summary><p>A new cross-functional team needs technical coverage and frequent negotiation across departments. Select people who meet the technical requirements and can work through ambiguity; train the group in problem-solving and conflict management; and recognize both reliable individual contributions and help that improves the combined result.</p><p>If members still withhold information after the reward change, investigate trust, roles and norms rather than assuming the incentive alone solved the problem.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">Decide whether work belongs in a team</h2>\n<p>The lecture gives three tests: <strong>Can several people do the work better?</strong> Complex work may benefit from varied perspectives, while simple tasks may be better left to individuals. <strong>Does the work create a common purpose beyond the aggregate of individual goals?</strong> <strong>Are members interdependent</strong>, so the whole depends on each contribution and each member’s success depends on others?</p><p>Use all three questions before choosing a team.</p>\n<section aria-label=\"Should this work be done by a team?\" class=\"worked-calculation\"><h3>Should this work be done by a team?</h3><div class=\"example-input\"><p>Use all three lecture questions. A “yes” to only one does not establish that teamwork is the best arrangement.</p></div><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>Test 1 · Better together?</h4><p>Ask whether multiple people can do this work better than one person, for example because it is complex or needs different perspectives. If it is simple and independent, one person may be the better fit.</p></section><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>Test 2 · Shared purpose?</h4><p>Ask whether the work creates a common purpose that is greater than the sum of members’ separate goals. A shared label or meeting does not by itself create that purpose.</p></section><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>Test 3 · Interdependent?</h4><p>Ask whether the whole depends on each member’s contribution and whether members’ success depends on one another. If each person can finish independently and combine outputs at the end, interdependence may be weak.</p></section><section class=\"calculation-part\"><h4>Decision · Vehicle-service example</h4><p>A dealership links customer service, mechanics, parts specialists, and sales staff around meeting a customer’s service needs. Different perspectives can improve the response; the common purpose is customer service; and the linked handoffs make members interdependent. A team is therefore plausible. The tests are a decision aid, not a promise that every team will perform well.</p></section></section>\n<section aria-label=\"Does this work call for a team?\" class=\"activity\" data-activity=\"team-check\"><h3>Does this work call for a team?</h3><label>Could several people do this better?<select data-input=\"better\"><option value=\"yes\">Yes</option><option value=\"no\">No</option></select></label><label>Is there a shared purpose beyond individual goals?<select data-input=\"purpose\"><option value=\"yes\">Yes</option><option value=\"no\">No</option></select></label><label>Does success depend on members’ contributions to one another?<select data-input=\"interdependence\"><option value=\"yes\">Yes</option><option value=\"no\">No</option></select></label><output aria-live=\"polite\" data-result=\"\"></output><p class=\"activity-note\">Apply the three lecture questions. This is a discussion aid, not a score or prediction of success.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to change the inputs. The course model is explained above.</p></noscript></section><h2 id=\"section-6\">A practitioner framework: New Rules for Teamwork</h2>\n<p>The article by Angus Dawson and Katy George presents a practitioner approach, not another version of the lecture model. It proposes three connected practices: build an operating system for teamwork, actively measure practices and outcomes, and use feedback to improve the system.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"diagram-frame\" style=\"--diagram-width:874.0px\"><?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE svg PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD SVG 1.1//EN\"\n \"http://www.w3.org/Graphics/SVG/1.1/DTD/svg11.dtd\">\n\n<!-- Generated by graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)\n -->\n<!-- Title: G Pages: 1 -->\n<svg aria-label=\"Article authors’ proposed operating logic: routines make work visible, measures inform learning, and adaptations update routines; this is a practitioner framework, not a causal model from the lecture.\" height=\"102pt\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0.00 0.00 874.00 102.00\" width=\"874pt\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><style>svg text{fill:var(--foreground,#34304a)}</style>\n<g class=\"graph\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-2-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-practitioner-improvement-loop-graph0\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 91.2)\">\n<title>G</title>\n<!-- system -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-2-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-practitioner-improvement-loop-node1\">\n<title>system</title>\n<path d=\"M239.1,-50.4C239.1,-50.4 12,-50.4 12,-50.4 6,-50.4 0,-44.4 0,-38.4 0,-38.4 0,-12 0,-12 0,-6 6,0 12,0 12,0 239.1,0 239.1,0 245.1,0 251.1,-6 251.1,-12 251.1,-12 251.1,-38.4 251.1,-38.4 251.1,-44.4 245.1,-50.4 239.1,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"125.55\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">1 · Operating system</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"125.55\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">Routines, roles, norms, handovers</text>\n</g>\n<!-- measure -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-2-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-practitioner-improvement-loop-node2\">\n<title>measure</title>\n<path d=\"M593.45,-80.4C593.45,-80.4 295.1,-80.4 295.1,-80.4 289.1,-80.4 283.1,-74.4 283.1,-68.4 283.1,-68.4 283.1,-42 283.1,-42 283.1,-36 289.1,-30 295.1,-30 295.1,-30 593.45,-30 593.45,-30 599.45,-30 605.45,-36 605.45,-42 605.45,-42 605.45,-68.4 605.45,-68.4 605.45,-74.4 599.45,-80.4 593.45,-80.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"444.27\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-58.95\">2 · Measurement</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"444.27\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-40.95\">Compare practices, experience and outcomes</text>\n</g>\n<!-- system&#45;&gt;measure -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-2-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-practitioner-improvement-loop-edge1\">\n<title>system-&gt;measure</title>\n<path d=\"M251.27,-37.01C259.06,-37.75 266.97,-38.5 274.9,-39.25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"274.66,-41.51 281.34,-39.86 275.09,-36.98 274.66,-41.51\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- improve -->\n<g class=\"node\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-2-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-practitioner-improvement-loop-node3\">\n<title>improve</title>\n<path d=\"M840.55,-50.4C840.55,-50.4 649.45,-50.4 649.45,-50.4 643.45,-50.4 637.45,-44.4 637.45,-38.4 637.45,-38.4 637.45,-12 637.45,-12 637.45,-6 643.45,0 649.45,0 649.45,0 840.55,0 840.55,0 846.55,0 852.55,-6 852.55,-12 852.55,-12 852.55,-38.4 852.55,-38.4 852.55,-44.4 846.55,-50.4 840.55,-50.4\" fill=\"var(--diagram-fill,#e8f1f3)\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-border,#7f9da5)\"></path>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"745\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-28.95\">3 · Continuous improvement</text>\n<text font-family=\"Georgia\" font-size=\"15.00\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"745\" xml:space=\"preserve\" y=\"-10.95\">Test, learn and adapt</text>\n</g>\n<!-- measure&#45;&gt;improve -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-2-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-practitioner-improvement-loop-edge2\">\n<title>measure-&gt;improve</title>\n<path d=\"M605.57,-39.1C613.56,-38.3 621.51,-37.5 629.33,-36.72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"629.35,-39 635.59,-36.09 628.9,-34.47 629.35,-39\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n<!-- improve&#45;&gt;system -->\n<g class=\"edge\" id=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-figure-2-lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness-practitioner-improvement-loop-edge3\">\n<title>improve-&gt;system</title>\n<path d=\"M636.98,-20.1C626.33,-19.73 615.68,-19.42 605.45,-19.2 462.22,-16.1 426.34,-16.46 283.1,-19.2 275.27,-19.35 267.22,-19.54 259.1,-19.77\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></path>\n<polygon fill=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\" points=\"259.24,-17.49 252.81,-19.94 259.37,-22.03 259.24,-17.49\" stroke=\"var(--diagram-edge,#547b86)\"></polygon>\n</g>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</div><figcaption>Article authors’ proposed operating logic: routines make work visible, measures inform learning, and adaptations update routines; this is a practitioner framework, not a causal model from the lecture.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>1. Develop an operating system</h3>\n<p>The article uses “operating system” for the building blocks that define how team members collaborate, make changes and support each other. It includes what value the team creates, what it should achieve, each member’s contribution, how work is managed, and the constructive norms that guide behaviour.</p><p>The authors recommend a system that provides consistent structure while remaining flexible enough to adapt to changing conditions. They discuss the Toyota Production System and Agile as examples of approaches that connect routines and learning.</p>\n<p>The Way We Work approach uses kickoffs, feedback, retrospectives, coaching and complete handovers. The routines make responsibilities and learning visible.</p><details><summary>The article’s bank example</summary><p>A Latin American bank team began with unclear responsibilities, long hours and little progress. A restart included consultants in the kickoff, recorded retrospectives, feedback and coaching. The authors report product launches and a handover after nine months. This case illustrates their approach; it does not prove the same result elsewhere.</p></details>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: connect a routine to a coordination problem</summary><p>A project has duplicated work and missing approvals. A kickoff can make responsibilities and cadence explicit; one-to-ones can surface individual blockers; retrospectives can identify repeated handoff failures; and a handover can preserve decisions for the next team. These routines create opportunities to notice and address coordination problems. They do not guarantee success without suitable resources, trust and follow-through.</p></details>\n<h3>2. Invest in active, real-time measurement</h3>\n<p>Measure both how the team works and what it achieves. The article combines employee feedback, routine-use and project-experience surveys, and confidential client feedback. Leaders use changes in the results to decide what to adjust.</p><details><summary>Measures and statistics reported by the article</summary><ul><li>A weekly anonymous, segmented survey asks how people feel using a five-emoji scale.</li><li>The fortnightly Engagement Team Survey covers routines, project experience and progress toward personal and professional goals.</li><li>Teams using all WWW elements scored 10 percentage points higher on client collaboration and 23 points higher on client capability-building.</li><li>In 40 teams with 217 consultants, those in person about half the time were 10 times as likely to report effective collaboration, and members were 25% more likely to report satisfaction with support.</li><li>For in-person rather than remote problem-solving sessions, 77% versus 59% of leaders reported meaningful client relationships.</li></ul><p>These are the authors’ reported comparisons in their setting. They do not establish an ideal schedule for every team or isolate location as the cause.</p></details>\n<p>The article also describes tradeoffs: teams co-locating more than half the time faced greater work-life and focus challenges, while teams working remotely about half the time were twice as likely to report enough recovery and more likely to see their jobs as sustainable.</p><p>These figures are the authors’ reported results from their context; they do not establish a universal ideal schedule or prove that location alone caused the differences.</p>\n<p>Other examples in the article include trauma centers tracking patient stabilization time and trying timers, and aviation organizations sharing incident reports around a common safety goal. They illustrate choosing measures connected to desired outcomes and learning across teams; they are not part of the lecture model.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: choose a measure before choosing a practice</summary><p>If a cross-functional group’s problem is slow shift handover, first define the desired outcome, such as a shorter handover while required information remains complete. Then track the outcome and test a specific change, such as assigning workstations in advance. Check both speed and information quality so improving one measure does not hide a worse result elsewhere.</p><p>This follows the article’s measurement logic; it does not assume that its example will work in every workplace.</p></details>\n<h3>3. Create a system for continuous improvement and innovation</h3>\n<p>The article recommends giving team leaders access to feedback and authority to test adjustments while work is underway. Its Way We Work center of excellence has 14 full-time employees who examine patterns, support teams and share practices.</p><p>The article also describes Bosch China using rapid prototypes and tests of digital tools, Hindustan Unilever’s Dapada factory using an app to track worker productivity, and McKinsey’s Lilli platform to search and synthesize organizational knowledge.</p>\n<p>The authors say improvement requires transparency and trust, an adaptable operating system, consideration of different perspectives, access to a shared knowledge repository, time, resources and senior accountability. They report that executives in high-performing teams are five times as productive as those in average teams. Treat this as the article’s reported comparison, not a guaranteed causal return from adopting its recommendations.</p>\n<details class=\"worked\"><summary>Teaching example: run a small improvement cycle</summary><p>A team sees repeated delays at one approval step. It reviews the relevant measures, asks members from each affected function what happens at the handoff, tests a revised approval routine, and checks the next results. If the change does not help, the team revises it and shares what it learned.</p><p>A manager needs both permission and time to make these adjustments; collecting feedback without acting on it is not continuous improvement.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"section-7\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Calling any set of coworkers a team. Look for a shared purpose, interdependence and collective performance.</li>\n<li>Treating positive synergy as automatic. It describes a potential result of coordinated effort, not a guaranteed effect of assembling people.</li>\n<li>Confusing cross-functional teams (individuals from different work areas) with multiteam systems (two or more interdependent teams). Virtual work describes collaboration across distance and can overlap either form.</li>\n<li>Collapsing the virtual-team finding about unique information into a claim about total information sharing; the lecture reports different directions for those two outcomes.</li>\n<li>Reading the effectiveness diagram as a rigid formula or assigning its arrows to individual factors. The source groups inputs into Context, Composition and Process, which converge on effectiveness.</li>\n<li>Treating diversity as always beneficial, larger teams as always better, or five to nine members as a universal rule. Benefits and coordination costs depend on task and conditions.</li>\n<li>Mixing up team efficacy (confidence the team can succeed), mental models (shared understanding of the work) and reflexivity (reviewing and adjusting the plan).</li>\n<li>Treating task conflict and relationship conflict as equivalent. Work-focused disagreement can sometimes help; personal tension is almost always harmful.</li>\n<li>Assuming a reward change alone will build trust, stop loafing or guarantee information sharing.</li>\n<li>Treating the practitioner article’s statistics or 50% co-location finding as universal or causal advice.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"section-anchor\" id=\"section-8\"></span><h2 id=\"section-9\">Practice questions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>What distinguishes a work team from a work group in the lecture’s four-dimension comparison?</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><p>Teams orient toward collective performance, positive synergy, individual and mutual accountability, and complementary skills. Work groups are described as sharing information, having neutral or sometimes negative synergy, individual accountability and random or varied skills. This is a source comparison, not a claim that every group lacks cooperation.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>A hospital’s emergency, intensive-care and recovery units remain separate teams but coordinate linked stages of treatment toward patient recovery. What structure is this, and who might act as a boundary spanner?</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><p>This is a multiteam system because multiple interdependent teams share a higher goal. A boundary spanner coordinates with the other subteams and can reduce the number of direct cross-team communication demands.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Put these factors in the lecture model: accurate staffing data, ability coverage, a measurable shared target, and reviewing the plan after conditions change.</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><p>Staffing data is Context (resources); ability coverage is Composition (abilities and role allocation); a measurable shared target is Process (specific goals and common purpose); reviewing and adapting the plan is Process (reflexivity).</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>A team debates two possible procedures, compares evidence, then begins criticizing one member’s personality. Distinguish the two kinds of conflict and explain what the team can do.</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><p>Comparing procedures is task conflict and may help non-routine work if it stays moderate and focused on ideas. Personal criticism is relationship conflict and is almost always dysfunctional. The team can return to its shared purpose, make assumptions explicit and evaluate evidence rather than motives.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Apply the three team-use tests to routine filing that each employee can complete independently and combine at the end.</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><p>Several people may not do the task better; it may not create a common purpose beyond separate goals; and members are not strongly interdependent if each can finish independently. The lecture suggests simple work like this may be better left to individuals.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>How can virtual teams share unique information better while sharing less information overall?</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><p>Unique information is held by individual members, while overall information sharing concerns the total exchange. A group can surface a member’s distinctive knowledge yet still exchange less information overall. The lecture reports this as a review finding, not a contradiction or universal rule.</p></details>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the three principles in the article’s practitioner framework and state one evidence limit.</li>\n</ol>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary><p>Develop an operating system, invest in active real-time measurement, and create a system for continuous improvement and innovation. The article’s company examples and reported statistics are context-specific author claims; they should not be presented as guaranteed causal results or part of the lecture’s effectiveness model.</p></details><div class=\"section-finish\"><h2>Keep the essentials</h2><p><a href=\"groups-revision.html\">Short note: groups and teams</a>. A compact recap and three recall questions for this part of the course.</p></div></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-7-group-decision-making.html\"><small>Previous</small>Group Decision-Making <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a href=\"groups-revision.html\"><small>Next</small>Short note: groups and teams <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer>Organizational Behavior and Management · Study guide <span>Understand work. Connect people, teams, and organizations.</span></footer>"},"groups-revision":{"title":"Short note: groups and teams · OB and Management","description":"Read the core distinctions, then test your recall. Follow a topic link for the full explanation and sources.","content":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Group behaviour <span>/</span> Revision</div><h1>Short note: groups and teams</h1><p class=\"lead\">Read the core distinctions, then test your recall. Follow a topic link for the full explanation and sources.</p><details class=\"contents\"><summary>On this page · 5 sections</summary><nav aria-label=\"On this page\" class=\"on-page\"><a href=\"#structure\">Read the group before judging the result</a><a href=\"#cohesion\">Cohesion amplifies the norms</a><a href=\"#decisions\">Keep discussion open to challenge</a><a href=\"#teams\">Choose and design the team</a><a href=\"#recall\">Try without looking</a></nav></details><article><h2 id=\"structure\">Read the group before judging the result</h2>\n<p>A group involves interaction and interdependence toward objectives. A work team coordinates effort toward a shared purpose and collective performance. Putting people together does not guarantee positive synergy.</p>\n<table class=\"comparison\" data-compact=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Remember</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Role</th><td>Expected conduct for a position.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Norm</th><td>A shared standard of acceptable behaviour.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Status</th><td>A socially assigned rank that can affect voice.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Social loafing</th><td>Less individual effort in group work than alone.</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Faultline</th><td>Aligned attributes can divide members into potential subgroups.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Make contributions identifiable and goals clear. Hear relevant expertise even when the speaker has low status. Diversity provides potential information; interaction determines how the group uses it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cohesion\">Cohesion amplifies the norms</h2>\n<p>High cohesion with high performance norms supports high productivity in the lecture model. High cohesion with low norms supports low productivity. Closeness alone is insufficient. Development stages are a useful lens, not an inevitable sequence for every group.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decisions\">Keep discussion open to challenge</h2>\n<p><strong>Groupthink</strong> suppresses critical appraisal through conformity pressure. <strong>Groupshift</strong> moves the group toward a more extreme position after discussion; the shift can be risky or cautious.</p>\n<p>Brainstorming defers criticism during idea generation. Nominal group technique separates four activities: silent generation, round-robin presentation, group clarification/evaluation and private ranking. Interacting groups discuss directly. Compare trade-offs rather than naming one universal winner.</p>\n<h2 id=\"teams\">Choose and design the team</h2>\n<ul><li>Can several people do the work better?</li><li>Is there a common purpose beyond individual goals?</li><li>Are members interdependent?</li></ul>\n<p>If teamwork fits, review <strong>Context</strong>, including resources, leadership, trust and rewards; <strong>Composition</strong>, including abilities, roles, size and preferences; and <strong>Process</strong>, including purpose, goals, efficacy, mental models, conflict and effort.</p>\n<p>Efficacy is belief in the team’s ability. Mental models are shared understandings of the work. Confidence does not ensure that the understanding is accurate.</p>\n<p>The practitioner article adds routines → measurement → adjustment. Keep that operating approach separate from the lecture model. Its company examples and reported numbers are not guaranteed results.</p>\n<h2 id=\"recall\">Try without looking</h2>\n<details><summary>A close group deliberately keeps output low. Is cohesion missing?</summary><p>No. Cohesion may reinforce low performance norms. Examine the standards the group shares.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does groupshift always mean taking more risk?</summary><p>No. Discussion can move a group toward greater caution as well as greater risk.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does nominal group technique exclude discussion?</summary><p>No. Clarification and evaluation happen after independent generation and presentation, before private ranking.</p></details></article><nav aria-label=\"Study sequence\" class=\"pager\"><a href=\"lecture-8-work-teams-and-effectiveness.html\"><small>Previous</small>Work Teams and Effectiveness <span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></nav><footer class=\"footer\">MN4063 · Organizational Behavior and Management</footer>"}};
